      var embeddedContent_1056964767 = {data: [{Field0: (-38787200), Field1: [100,101,101,112,95,114,101,118,105,101,119,47,99,111,108,108,101,99,116,105,110,103,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Collecting a review\x0Aorder: 1\x0A---\x0A\x0A# Collecting a review\x0A\x0AA review is produced by one command and opened by another:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act review collect \xE2\x80\xA6   # build a review dataset from recordings + a diff\x0Act review <PATH>      # open it\x0A```\x0A\x0A`collect` needs three things: a **diff** (a commit range, or a patch file), the\x0A**source** the diff applies to, and one or more **recordings** of that code\x0Arunning. It writes a `review.json` into the output directory, and the directory\x0Ait names is exactly what `ct review` opens.\x0A\x0A## DeepReview is not rr-only\x0A\x0A`ct review collect` chooses a collector by **inspecting the recordings you give\x0Ait**. There is no `--backend` flag and you never name one.\x0A\x0A| Recordings                                                             | Collector                                        |\x0A| ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\x0A| Native / rr (C, C++, Rust recorded through the rr backend)             | the native replay backend (`ct-native-replay`)   |\x0A| Materialized (Python, Ruby, JavaScript, Noir, and every other language that records a trace database) | the db-backend collector, over the same trace the debugger reads |\x0A\x0AA directory holding recordings of **both** kinds is refused, rather than\x0Ahalf-collected:\x0A\x0A```console\x0A$ ct review collect --repo . --diff HEAD~..HEAD --recordings ./mixed -o /tmp/out\x0Aerror: `ct review collect` refuses a mixed recordings directory: \'./mixed\' holds recordings of more than one kind.\x0A    native (rr): 1 (rr-run)\x0A    materialized (CTFS): 1 (py-run)\x0A  One collector is chosen per run by inspecting the recordings, and datasets produced by\x0A  two different collectors are not merged.  Point --recordings at recordings of one kind.\x0A```\x0A\x0AAn empty directory, a directory with no recordings in it, and a `--recordings`\x0Apath that does not exist are three different mistakes and get three different\x0Amessages:\x0A\x0A```console\x0A$ ct review collect --repo . --diff HEAD~..HEAD --recordings ./empty -o /tmp/out\x0Aerror: `ct review collect` found no recordings in \'./empty\': the directory is empty.\x0A  Record the runs you want reviewed first (`ct record \xE2\x80\xA6`), then point --recordings at\x0A  the directory holding them.\x0A\x0A$ ct review collect --repo . --diff HEAD~..HEAD --recordings ./nope -o /tmp/out\x0Aerror: `ct review collect` found no recordings directory at \'./nope\'.\x0A  --recordings names the directory that HOLDS the recordings, one subdirectory each.\x0A```\x0A\x0A## A worked example: reviewing a Noir change\x0A\x0AThis walks the whole loop on a materialized-trace language. Noir is used because\x0A`nargo` ships with CodeTracer; the steps are identical for Python, Ruby or\x0AJavaScript \xE2\x80\x94 only the `ct record` invocation changes (see the\x0A[Getting Started](/getting_started) guide for your language).\x0A\x0A### 1. The program, before the change\x0A\x0A`Nargo.toml`:\x0A\x0A```toml\x0A[package]\x0Aname = \"scale_sum\"\x0Atype = \"bin\"\x0Aauthors = [\"\"]\x0A\x0A[dependencies]\x0A```\x0A\x0A`Prover.toml`:\x0A\x0A```toml\x0Ax = \"5\"\x0A```\x0A\x0A`src/main.nr`:\x0A\x0A```rust\x0Afn main(x: Field) {\x0A    let mut sum: Field = 0;\x0A    for i in 0..4 {\x0A        sum = sum + x;\x0A    }\x0A    let final_result = sum;\x0A    assert(final_result == 20);\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0ACommit it:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Agit init -q\x0Agit add -A\x0Agit commit -qm \"base: sum x four times\"\x0A```\x0A\x0A### 2. The change to review\x0A\x0AScale each term by the loop index instead of adding `x` four times:\x0A\x0A```rust\x0Afn main(x: Field) {\x0A    let mut sum: Field = 0;\x0A    for i in 0..4 {\x0A        let contribution = (i as Field) * x;\x0A        sum = sum + contribution;\x0A    }\x0A    let final_result = sum;\x0A    assert(final_result == 30);\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Agit add -A\x0Agit commit -qm \"scale each term by the loop index\"\x0A```\x0A\x0A### 3. Record the change running\x0A\x0AA review needs recordings. Record the runs you want reviewed into one directory,\x0Aone subdirectory per run:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act record -o .ct/runs/run-1 .\x0A```\x0A\x0A```console\x0ASaved trace to /home/you/scale_sum/.ct/runs/run-1\x0ArecordingId:01a016ab-5ec2-7269-ae4c-f99e9003223f\x0A```\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AThose are the **last two** lines. Recording a Noir program first prints a block\x0Aof `warning: unused import __debug_member_assign_N` diagnostics \xE2\x80\x94 one per\x0Avariable the instrumenter tracks \xE2\x80\x94 on **stdout**, not stderr. The eight-line\x0Aprogram above produces eighteen such lines; a real program produces hundreds.\x0AThey come from `nargo` compiling the instrumented copy, they are not a problem\x0Awith your code, and there is no flag to suppress them today. A script that\x0Aparses this output should read the last lines rather than the first, and cannot\x0Aseparate the noise by redirecting stderr.\x0A:::\x0A\x0ARecord as many runs as you want reviewed \xE2\x80\x94 a second run with a different\x0A`Prover.toml` input, say, or the same program under a different test. Each\x0Abecomes a **trace context** in the review:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act record -o .ct/runs/run-2 .\x0A```\x0A\x0A### 4. Collect the dataset\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act review collect --repo . --diff HEAD~..HEAD --recordings .ct/runs --output .ct/review\x0A```\x0A\x0A```console\x0ADeepReview collect complete: 2 recordings collected, 1 files, 1 with coverage, 2 function executions\x0AReview dataset ready: .ct/review/review.json\x0AOpen it with: ct review .ct/review\x0A```\x0A\x0A`collect` writes `review.json` into the output directory, so the directory it\x0Anames is exactly what `ct review` opens.\x0A\x0A### 5. Open the review\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act review .ct/review\x0A```\x0A\x0AEither the directory or the `review.json` inside it works. A path that does not\x0Aexist is diagnosed by `ct` before anything launches.\x0A\x0AWhat you see next is covered by [Reading a review](/deep_review/reading).\x0A\x0A## Reviewing native (rr) recordings\x0A\x0ANothing about the workflow changes. Record the runs the way you normally would\x0Afor your language (for native programs that means the rr backend \xE2\x80\x94 see\x0A[`ct record`](/reference/ct_cli)), point `--recordings` at the directory holding\x0Athem, and `ct review collect` routes to the native collector. The `collect` and\x0A`review` commands are the same ones used above; only the recordings differ.\x0A\x0AThe native collector is reached as a subprocess. `ct` finds it on `PATH` as\x0A`ct-native-replay`, or wherever `CODETRACER_NATIVE_REPLAY_PATH` points. If it is\x0Amissing, only native collection fails \xE2\x80\x94 collecting a Python or Noir recording on\x0Aa machine that never installed the rr backend works normally, and says so if it\x0Acannot.\x0A\x0AReading a diff out of a git repository with `--repo` needs the native\x0Acollector\'s optional git support, which the default build does not enable. If\x0A`--repo` is refused, generate the patch yourself and pass it instead:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Agit diff main..HEAD > change.patch\x0Act review collect --diff-file change.patch --recordings .ct/runs --output .ct/review\x0A```\x0A\x0AA patch file names no commits, so a dataset collected that way carries empty\x0Acommit ids rather than invented ones.\x0A\x0A## Inspecting a dataset without a GUI\x0A\x0A**The dataset the worked example above just produced cannot be inspected\x0Atoday.** `ct review inspect` reads only the **native** collector\'s format \xE2\x80\x94 the\x0Adirectory holding a `manifest.dr` \xE2\x80\x94 and the materialized collector writes a\x0Asingle `review.json` and no `.dr` chunks. Running it on `.ct/review` fails:\x0A\x0A```console\x0A$ ct review inspect .ct/review\x0Aerror: `ct review inspect` needs the native replay backend, and no `ct-native-replay` binary was found.\x0A  Add it to PATH, or point CODETRACER_NATIVE_REPLAY_PATH at it.\x0A```\x0A\x0AWith the native backend present it gets one step further and then fails on the\x0Amissing `manifest.dr`. Either way the exit status is 1, so a CI job that shells\x0Aout to it on a materialized dataset will fail rather than print statistics.\x0A\x0AFor a materialized dataset, read `review.json` directly \xE2\x80\x94 it is plain JSON, and\x0A`files[].coverage`, `files[].flow` and `files[].diff` are the same numbers\x0A`inspect` would summarise:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Ajq \'{files: (.files | length), commit: .commitSha, recordings: .recordingCount}\' .ct/review/review.json\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor a **native** dataset the command is:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act review inspect <PATH> [--format text|json]\x0A```\x0A\x0Awhich prints file and symbol counts, coverage and flow steps.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A- [Reading a review](/deep_review/reading) \xE2\x80\x94 what the three panels show.\x0A- [The agent workflow](/deep_review/agent_workflow) \xE2\x80\x94 collecting from an agent\x0A  session, and the end-of-turn hook.\x0A- [`ct` CLI reference](/reference/ct_cli) \xE2\x80\x94 the full `ct review` flag tables.\x0A- [Not yet available](/deep_review/not_yet_available) \xE2\x80\x94 the deferrals, named.\x0A")}, {Field0: 73032064, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,112,121,116,104,111,110,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Python\x0Aorder: 6\x0A---\x0A## Python\x0A\x0AThis guide explains how to install the Python recorder wheel, capture a trace with your own interpreter, and open the resulting recording in CodeTracer.\x0A\x0A### 1. Install the Python recorder package\x0A\x0ACodeTracer\'s Python support runs inside your Python interpreter \xE2\x80\x94 the `codetracer_python_recorder` package must be installed in the same environment whose code you intend to trace.  `ct record` discovers and invokes it automatically.\x0A\x0A1. Activate the environment that you normally use to run your Python code (virtualenv, `uv`, Conda, system Python, etc.). `ct record` always reuses whichever interpreter is active in your shell.\x0A2. Install (or upgrade) the recorder package:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   python -m pip install --upgrade codetracer_python_recorder\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   If you use a tool like `uv` or `pipx`, run the equivalent command inside that tool so the recorder ends up on the same `sys.path` as your application.\x0A3. Optional: confirm the recorder is ready by running CodeTracer\'s readiness probe.\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   ct doctor python\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   The probe reports the interpreter it would invoke and whether the recorder package is importable from it.\x0A\x0A:::caution\x0AIf `ct record` cannot import the recorder package, recording will fail with a module import error. Install the package in the interpreter you intend to trace or point Codetracer at a specific interpreter via `CODETRACER_PYTHON_INTERPRETER=/path/to/python`.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### 2. Record a trace\x0A\x0A1. From the same shell where the interpreter (and recorder wheel) are available, run:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   ct record path/to/script.py [-- <script arguments>]\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   - Omit `--lang`; Python scripts are auto-detected.\x0A   - Any arguments after `--` are passed directly to your script.\x0A   - Use `CODETRACER_PYTHON_INTERPRETER` if you need to force a specific interpreter (for example, inside CI).\x0A2. The command prints the trace identifier and the output directory (defaults to `.`, configurable with `-o`). The directory contains `trace.json`, `trace_paths.json`, and `trace_metadata.json` generated by the Python recorder via the db backend.\x0A\x0A### 3. Open the trace\x0A\x0AYou can explore the recording immediately in the UI or later on:\x0A\x0A- Launch the GUI with the most recent trace:\x0A\x0A  ```bash\x0A  ct replay\x0A  ```\x0A\x0A- Replay a specific Python trace by path:\x0A\x0A  ```bash\x0A  ct replay --trace-folder path/to/trace-output\x0A  ```\x0A\x0A- Or jump straight from recording to replay in one command:\x0A\x0A  ```bash\x0A  ct run path/to/script.py [-- <script arguments>]\x0A  ```\x0A\x0AAll three commands replay the same trace artefacts `ct record` produced.\x0A\x0A## Recording a web application\x0A\x0AFlask, Django, FastAPI and any other WSGI/ASGI application are recorded as a\x0Arunning server rather than a single script, and each HTTP request appears in\x0Athe Request Panel as it arrives. That has its own guide:\x0A[Live requests \xE2\x80\x94 Python](/usage_guide/live-requests-python).\x0A\x0AIn short:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang python -o ./trace -- flask --app app run --port 8000\x0A```\x0A\x0AThen `ct replay -t ./trace` in another terminal shows the requests live.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-1510100863), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,111,109,110,105,115,99,105,101,110,116,45,100,98,45,115,105,122,101,45,98,101,110,99,104,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Omniscient-DB size benchmark\x0Aorder: 110\x0A---\x0A# Omniscient-DB on-disk size benchmark (P2)\x0A\x0AThe `ct-bench omniscient-db-size` subcommand drives the\x0APerformance + E2E Coverage campaign\'s P2 benchmark \xE2\x80\x94 per-language\x0Aon-disk artefact sizes for the omniscient-DB build flow.\x0A\x0AFor each fixture program, the bench:\x0A\x0A1. Records the program through `ct record` (which routes to the appropriate\x0A   per-language recorder backend internally).\x0A2. Invokes `ct trace omniscient-prep <slice-dir> --mode on` to materialise\x0A   the omniscient artefacts under `meta_dat/`.\x0A3. Measures the on-disk artefact sizes via `std::fs::metadata`.\x0A\x0AOutput lands in `src/codetracer-bench/target/codetracer-bench/omniscient-db-size/`\x0Ain three formats:\x0A\x0A- `report.csv` \xE2\x80\x94 for spreadsheet ingestion.\x0A- `report.json` \xE2\x80\x94 for downstream tooling.\x0A- `report.md` \xE2\x80\x94 the human-friendly form (rendered below).\x0A\x0A## How to invoke\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Default: Python + C++ \xC3\x97 3 fixtures each (6 rows).\x0Ajust bench-omniscient-db-size\x0A\x0A# Narrow to a single language.\x0Ajust bench-omniscient-db-size --languages=python\x0A\x0A# Full 10-language matrix (skips per-language when the recorder is absent).\x0Ajust bench-omniscient-db-size --all-languages\x0A```\x0A\x0AOperators on hosts where the recorders live under different binary\x0Anames can override per-language via the `RECORDER` env var inside each\x0Afixture\'s `regenerate.sh`, or via the global `CT_BIN` env var for the\x0A`ct` driver.\x0A\x0A## How to read the report\x0A\x0AColumns:\x0A\x0A| column | meaning |\x0A| --- | --- |\x0A| `id` | `<language>/<fixture name>` |\x0A| `language` | recorder language |\x0A| `run_length_events` | event count from the trace manifest (0 when unavailable) |\x0A| `trace_size_raw` | bytes on disk of the recorded trace |\x0A| `trace_size_compressed` | post-compression bytes (currently same as raw \xE2\x80\x94 recorders ship pre-compressed) |\x0A| `omniscient_size_raw` | `meta_dat/memwrites.tc + linehits.tc` bytes |\x0A| `omniscient_size_compressed` | as above (recorders ship pre-compressed) |\x0A| `origin_meta_size_raw` | `meta_dat/originmeta.tc + varwrites.tc + source_exprs.tc + origin-config.toml` bytes |\x0A| `origin_meta_size_compressed` | as above |\x0A| `ratio_omniscient_over_trace` | ratio of omniscient artefacts to the underlying trace |\x0A| `ratio_origin_meta_over_trace` | same for the origin-metadata artefacts |\x0A| `ratio_compressed_over_raw` | post-compression ratio across trace + omniscient |\x0A\x0AThe ratios are computed at report time from the raw byte columns so\x0Adownstream consumers can recompute them with different denominators.\x0A\x0A## How to extend the fixture set\x0A\x0AEach language has a `src/codetracer-bench/fixtures/omniscient-db-size/<language>/`\x0Adirectory holding one sub-directory per fixture. To add a fixture:\x0A\x0A1. Create `src/codetracer-bench/fixtures/omniscient-db-size/<language>/<name>/`.\x0A2. Add `main.<ext>` with the fixture program.\x0A3. Copy `regenerate.sh` from an existing fixture and adjust the\x0A   recorder env-var name if required.\x0A\x0AThe bench picks fixtures up automatically \xE2\x80\x94 no code changes required.\x0A\x0A## SKIP discipline\x0A\x0AEach language probe checks for exactly one binary on PATH and emits a\x0Aprecise sentinel when it\'s missing:\x0A\x0A```\x0ASKIPPED python: codetracer-python-recorder not on PATH\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe bench never SKIPs broadly. Operators can read the trailing log\x0Aoutput to see exactly which dependency the bench wants.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-778482815), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,115,108,105,99,101,45,112,114,101,112,45,115,112,101,101,100,45,98,101,110,99,104,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Slice prep speed benchmark\x0Aorder: 112\x0A---\x0A# Slice generation speed + concurrent processing speedup bench (P3)\x0A\x0AThe `ct-bench slice-prep-speed` subcommand drives the P3 benchmark.\x0ASlices are an MCR concept. For a fixed program (default: the P2\x0A`mid_length_compute` fixture in the selected language), the current\x0Adriver records K independent MCR-shaped trace directories as a\x0Aslice-prep/concurrency proxy for K \xE2\x88\x88 {1, 2, 4, 8, 16}, then runs the\x0Aomniscient-prep subprocess against each trace at concurrency\x0AC \xE2\x88\x88 {1, 2, 4, 8}.\x0A\x0AThe bench measures three things:\x0A\x0A1. **Trace size overhead** \xE2\x80\x94 `(trace_size(K=N) / trace_size(K=1)) - 1`,\x0A   ideally close to zero.\x0A2. **Per-slice prep wall-clock** \xE2\x80\x94 `mean(per-slice times)` at each\x0A   K \xC3\x97 C cell.\x0A3. **Coordinator reduce wall-clock** \xE2\x80\x94 approximated as\x0A   `total_wall_clock \xE2\x88\x92 per_slice_wall_clock` and reported per cell.\x0A\x0A## How to invoke\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Default \xE2\x80\x94 full 5\xC3\x974 = 20 cell matrix.\x0Ajust bench-slice-prep-speed\x0A\x0A# Narrower run.\x0Ajust bench-slice-prep-speed --slice-counts=1,2 --prep-concurrency=1,2\x0A\x0A# Different native language through the MCR-compatible ct record path.\x0Ajust bench-slice-prep-speed --language=c_plus_plus\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Reading the report\x0A\x0AThe CSV columns are `slice_count`, `prep_concurrency`,\x0A`per_slice_wall_clock_ms`, `coordinator_wall_clock_ms`,\x0A`total_wall_clock_ms`, `trace_size_bytes`.\x0A\x0AThe campaign\'s headline numbers are:\x0A\x0A- \"K=16 trace size within 10 % of K=1\" \xE2\x80\x94 derived by computing\x0A  `(trace_size_bytes(K=16) - trace_size_bytes(K=1)) /\x0A  trace_size_bytes(K=1)` and comparing to 0.10.\x0A- \"speedup vs. linear\" \xE2\x80\x94 `(total_wall_clock_ms(K, C=1) / C) /\x0A  total_wall_clock_ms(K, C)` for each `(K, C)` cell.\x0A\x0AOperators chart these from the CSV.\x0A\x0A## SKIP discipline\x0A\x0AThe bench skips narrowly when:\x0A\x0A- the per-language recorder is not on PATH, or\x0A- the `ct` (or `replay-server`) binary is not on PATH.\x0A\x0AIn either case the bench writes the skip reason to stderr and emits\x0Aan empty report; operators can spot the missing dependency in the log.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-1261575675), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,103,117,105,45,111,112,115,45,108,97,116,101,110,99,121,45,98,101,110,99,104,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: GUI-ops latency matrix benchmark\x0Aorder: 113\x0A---\x0A# GUI-feature latency matrix benchmark (P4)\x0A\x0AThe `ct-bench gui-ops` subcommand drives the P4 benchmark \xE2\x80\x94 a\x0Abackend \xC3\x97 platform \xC3\x97 language \xC3\x97 operation matrix of wall-clock\x0Alatencies.\x0A\x0AThe bench measures the V1 set of 11 user-triggered operations:\x0A\x0A- `ct/load-locals`\x0A- `ct/load-history(1K)` + `ct/load-history(10K)`\x0A- `ct/load-flow`\x0A- `ct/originChain`\x0A- `ct/originSummary(batch)`\x0A- `tracepoint-eval`\x0A- `jump-to-line` + `jump-to-call`\x0A- `reverse-step` (RR / MCR / TTD only)\x0A- `watchpoint` (RR / MCR / TTD only)\x0A\x0A\xE2\x80\xA6against five backends (`materialized`, `rr`, `mcr-omniscient`,\x0A`mcr-no-omniscient`, `ttd`) across three platforms (`linux`, `macos`,\x0A`windows`) and 2 default languages (`python`, `c_plus_plus`).\x0A\x0A## Matrix shape\x0A\x0AThe campaign\'s V1 measurements ship for:\x0A\x0A- Python materialized + Linux.\x0A- C++ RR + Linux.\x0A- C++ MCR-with-omniscient + Linux.\x0A- C++ MCR-without-omniscient + Linux.\x0A\x0AEvery other cell \xE2\x80\x94 including TTD (Windows-only) and the macOS /\x0AWindows columns \xE2\x80\x94 is reported as `PENDING` so the matrix is visible\x0Ato capacity planners. The Markdown report renders each pending cell\x0Aas the literal string `PENDING`.\x0A\x0A## How to invoke\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Ajust bench-gui-ops\x0Ajust bench-gui-ops --backends=materialized,rr\x0Ajust bench-gui-ops --operations=ct/load-locals,ct/originChain\x0Ajust bench-gui-ops --languages=python\x0A```\x0A\x0AOutput lands in\x0A`src/codetracer-bench/target/codetracer-bench/gui-ops-latency/`.\x0A\x0A## Reading the report\x0A\x0AEach row in the report is an operation; each column is a\x0A`<backend>-<platform>-<language>` triple. Cell content is either:\x0A\x0A- `p50=X.XXms p95=Y.YYms` \xE2\x80\x94 the measured numbers, or\x0A- `PENDING` \xE2\x80\x94 the cell is intentionally not measured.\x0A\x0A## Driver wiring\x0A\x0AThe per-cell measurement loop spawns a `replay-server dap-server\x0A--stdio` subprocess against a pre-recorded fixture, sends one DAP\x0Arequest per operation, and times each round-trip with\x0A`std::time::Instant`. The driver iterates 100\xC3\x97 per cell by default\x0A(configurable via `--iterations`) and reports the p50 / p95 of the\x0Aresulting distribution.\x0A\x0AThe campaign currently ships the driver scaffolding; the per-cell DAP\x0Aplumbing is a separate post-P4 follow-on once the headless DAP\x0Aharness stabilises. Until then every cell renders as PENDING with\x0Athe precise sentinel:\x0A\x0A```\x0Adap-driver pending: <backend> <language> <operation>\x0A```\x0A\x0A\xE2\x80\xA6which surfaces the deferred state honestly per the campaign\'s\x0A\"every unmeasured cell shows up explicitly\" requirement.\x0A\x0A## Tracepoint benchmark extension\x0A\x0AThe campaign also asks for the existing `tracepoint_interpreter`\x0Abenchmark to emit through the matrix format. The current state: the\x0Abenchmark file is not present in\x0A`codetracer/src/db-backend/benches/`. The\x0A`tracepoint_benchmark_emits_matrix_format` verification test SKIPs\x0Awith that observation so the gap is visible \xE2\x80\x94 once the benchmark\x0Afile lands, wiring it through the matrix format reuses this crate\'s\x0A`BenchReport` shape.\x0A")}, {Field0: 1430492806, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,105,110,100,101,120,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Getting Started\x0Aorder: 0\x0A---\x0A# Overview\x0A\x0AWelcome to the CodeTracer\'s getting started section. This guide will help you understand how to record and replay programs in each of the supported programming languages.\x0A\x0A## Core Concept: Record and Replay\x0A\x0AUnlike traditional debuggers that attach to a running process, CodeTracer works by first **recording** your application\'s execution into a trace file. This trace captures everything that happens during the run.\x0A\x0AOnce a recording is made, you can **replay** it as many times as you need in the CodeTracer GUI. This allows you to inspect the application\'s state at any point in time, move forwards and backwards through the execution, and use powerful features like tracepoints without having to run your application again.\x0A\x0AThink of it like recording a video of your program\'s execution that you can then analyze in detail.\x0A\x0A## How to Use This Guide\x0A\x0AThis guide is structured to help you get started quickly and then dive deeper into the features that interest you.\x0A\x0A### General-Purpose Languages\x0A\x0AThese languages are recorded directly via the `ct` CLI (`ct record`, `ct run`):\x0A\x0A- [**Python**](./python.md): Steps for installing the recorder, creating a trace, and replaying it with your interpreter.\x0A- [**Ruby**](./ruby.md): Examples and steps how to trace a Ruby program.\x0A- [**JavaScript / TypeScript**](./javascript.md): Instrument and record JS/TS programs.\x0A- [**WASM**](./wasm.md): Examples and steps how to trace a WASM program.\x0A\x0A### Zero-Knowledge Languages\x0A\x0A- [**Noir**](./noir.md): Examples and steps how to trace a Noir program.\x0A- [**Circom**](./circom.md): Trace witness generation for Circom zero-knowledge circuits.\x0A- [**Miden**](./miden.md): Trace programs on the Miden STARK-based VM.\x0A- [**Leo (Aleo)**](./leo.md): Trace Leo smart contracts on the Aleo network.\x0A\x0A### Smart Contract Languages\x0A\x0AThese languages use dedicated recorder binaries. Record with the language-specific recorder, then view with `ct replay --trace-folder <dir>`:\x0A\x0A- [**Solidity (EVM)**](./solidity.md): Trace Solidity contracts via debug_traceTransaction.\x0A- [**Stylus**](./stylus.md): Examples and steps how to trace a Stylus contract.\x0A- [**Cairo (StarkNet)**](./cairo.md): Trace Cairo programs and StarkNet contracts.\x0A- [**Aiken (Cardano)**](./aiken.md): Step through UPLC CEK machine execution.\x0A- [**Cadence (Flow)**](./cadence.md): Trace Cadence contracts via interpreter instrumentation.\x0A- [**Move (Sui / Aptos)**](./move.md): Trace Move VM execution on Sui and Aptos.\x0A- [**Solana**](./solana.md): Trace Solana SBF programs with DWARF source mapping.\x0A- [**Sway (FuelVM)**](./sway.md): Trace Sway contracts on FuelVM.\x0A- [**PolkaVM (ink!)**](./polkavm.md): Trace PolkaVM programs and ink! contracts.\x0A- [**Tolk (TON)**](./tolk.md): Trace Tolk/FunC contracts on the TON blockchain.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-1044648307), Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,114,101,99,111,114,100,101,114,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Recorder CLI Reference\x0Aorder: 104\x0A---\x0A## Recorder CLI Reference\x0A\x0AEach supported blockchain language has its own recorder binary that produces trace files in CodeTracer\'s format. This page provides a comprehensive reference for all recorder CLIs.\x0A\x0AAll recorders share common conventions:\x0A\x0A- Output directory: `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` (default: `./ct-traces/`)\x0A- Output format: `-f, --format <FMT>` (`binary` or `json`, default: `binary`)\x0A- Version: `version` subcommand\x0A- Output files: `trace.bin` (or `trace.json`), `trace_metadata.json`, `trace_paths.json`\x0A\x0AAfter recording, open the trace with `ct replay --trace-folder <DIR>`.\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-evm-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Solidity smart contracts on the EVM.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-evm-recorder record <solidity-file> [options]\x0A```\x0A\x0A| Flag                | Description                       |\x0A| ------------------- | --------------------------------- |\x0A| `<solidity-file>`   | Path to the `.sol` source file    |\x0A| `--trace-dir <DIR>` | Output directory for trace files  |\x0A| `--function <NAME>` | Function to call (default: `run`) |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-circom-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Circom zero-knowledge circuits during witness generation.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-circom-recorder record <circom-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-circom-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                          |\x0A| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<circom-file>`       | Path to the `.circom` source file                    |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)           |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)               |\x0A| `-b, --backend <BE>`  | Witness generator: `wasm` or `cpp` (default: `wasm`) |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-cairo-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Cairo programs and StarkNet contracts.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-cairo-recorder record <cairo-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-cairo-recorder trace-starknet <trace-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-cairo-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-cairo-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a standalone Cairo program.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<cairo-file>`        | Path to the `.cairo` source file           |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**trace-starknet** \xE2\x80\x94 Convert an snforge trace JSON.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<trace-file>`        | Path to snforge trace JSON                 |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain StarkNet transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                           |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--tx-hash <HASH>`    | Transaction hash (e.g., `0x04a3c...`) |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`     | StarkNet JSON-RPC endpoint            |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>` | Directory with contract source code   |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-cardano-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Aiken smart contracts on Cardano (UPLC CEK machine).\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-cardano-recorder record <aiken-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-cardano-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-cardano-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record an Aiken source file.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<aiken-file>`        | Path to the `.ak` source file              |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain Cardano transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                     | Description                                          |\x0A| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--tx-hash <HASH>`       | Transaction hash (64 hex characters)                 |\x0A| `--blockfrost-key <KEY>` | Blockfrost API key (or `BLOCKFROST_API_KEY` env var) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`    | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)           |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`     | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)               |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-flow-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Cadence smart contracts on the Flow blockchain.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-flow-recorder record <cdc-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-flow-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-flow-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a Cadence source file.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<cdc-file>`          | Path to the `.cdc` source file             |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain Flow transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `--tx-hash <HASH>`    | Flow transaction hash                      |\x0A| `--access-node <URL>` | Flow Access Node gRPC endpoint             |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>` | Directory with Cadence source files        |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-solana-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Solana programs (SBF virtual machine).\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-solana-recorder record <elf-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-solana-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-solana-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record from a compiled Solana program.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                   |\x0A| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<elf-file>`          | Path to the compiled `.so` ELF file           |\x0A| `--regs <FILE>`       | Pre-generated register trace (`.regs` binary) |\x0A| `--elf <PATH>`        | Unstripped ELF for DWARF source mapping       |\x0A| `--idl <FILE>`        | Anchor IDL JSON for account data decoding     |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)    |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)        |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay a Solana transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                   | Description                                                     |\x0A| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--signature <SIG>`    | Transaction signature (base-58)                                 |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`      | Solana JSON-RPC endpoint (default: `http://localhost:8899`)     |\x0A| `--program-dir <PATH>` | Directory with compiled `.so` files (default: `target/deploy/`) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`  | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                      |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`   | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)                          |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-move-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Move smart contracts on Sui and Aptos.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder record <trace-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder aptosreplay [options]\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Convert a Move trace file to CodeTracer format.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<trace-file>`        | Trace file (`.json` or `.json.zst`)        |\x0A| `-s, --source <FILE>` | Move source file                           |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay a Sui transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                         |\x0A| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--digest <DIGEST>`   | Sui transaction digest                              |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`     | Sui RPC endpoint (default: `http://localhost:9000`) |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>` | Directory with Move source files                    |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)          |\x0A\x0A**aptosreplay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an Aptos transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                                           |\x0A| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--txn-version <N>`   | Transaction version (ledger version)                                  |\x0A| `--node-url <URL>`    | Aptos REST API (default: `https://fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com/v1`) |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>` | Directory with Move source files                                      |\x0A| `--profile-gas`       | Run gas profiling (default: `true`)                                   |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                            |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-fuel-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Sway smart contracts on FuelVM.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-fuel-recorder record [project-dir] [options]\x0Acodetracer-fuel-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-fuel-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a Sway project.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                     |\x0A| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `[project-dir]`       | Path to Sway project (must contain `Forc.toml`) |\x0A| `--bytecode <FILE>`   | Raw FuelVM bytecode file                        |\x0A| `--abi <FILE>`        | Sway ABI JSON for variable enrichment           |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)      |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)          |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain Fuel transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                                              |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `--tx-id <ID>`        | Transaction ID (hex with `0x` prefix)                                    |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`     | fuel-core GraphQL endpoint (default: `http://localhost:4000/v1/graphql`) |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>` | Directory with forc build output                                         |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                               |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-miden-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Miden VM programs and contracts.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-miden-recorder record <program> [options]\x0Acodetracer-miden-recorder contract [options]\x0Acodetracer-miden-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-miden-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a MASM program.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                        |\x0A| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<program>`           | Path to MASM source (`.masm`) or package (`.masp`) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)         |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)             |\x0A\x0A**contract** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a MockChain contract simulation.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                    |\x0A| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--wallet-id <ID>`    | Account ID for wallet (hex, default: `0x1000`) |\x0A| `--faucet-id <ID>`    | Account ID for faucet (hex, default: `0x2000`) |\x0A| `--symbol <SYM>`      | Faucet token symbol (default: `TEST`)          |\x0A| `--debug`             | Enable debug mode (default: `true`)            |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)     |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain Miden transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                       | Description                                |\x0A| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `--node-url <URL>`         | Miden node RPC endpoint                    |\x0A| `--account-id <ID>`        | Account ID (hex)                           |\x0A| `--transaction-id <ID>`    | Transaction ID (hex)                       |\x0A| `--captured-inputs <FILE>` | Path to captured TransactionInputs JSON    |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`      | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-polkavm-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces PolkaVM programs and ink! smart contracts.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-polkavm-recorder record <program> [options]\x0Acodetracer-polkavm-recorder trace-ink [options]\x0Acodetracer-polkavm-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-polkavm-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a PolkaVM program.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<program>`           | Path to PolkaVM program blob (`.polkavm`)  |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**trace-ink** \xE2\x80\x94 Record an ink! contract execution.\x0A\x0A| Flag                   | Description                                       |\x0A| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--contract <PATH>`    | ink! contract (`.polkavm` blob or `.contract`)    |\x0A| `--constructor <NAME>` | Constructor to call (e.g., `new`, `default`)      |\x0A| `--message <NAME>`     | ink! message name (e.g., `flip`, `get`)           |\x0A| `--arg <VALUE>`        | Message arguments (repeatable, hex-encoded SCALE) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`  | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)        |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain contract.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                             |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--address <ADDR>`    | Contract address (SS58 or hex)                          |\x0A| `--selector <NAME>`   | ink! message selector name                              |\x0A| `--calldata <HEX>`    | Hex-encoded calldata (default: empty)                   |\x0A| `--endpoint <URL>`    | Substrate RPC endpoint (default: `ws://127.0.0.1:9944`) |\x0A| `--block <HASH>`      | Block hash for state fetch (latest if omitted)          |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>` | Contract source directory                               |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)              |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-leo-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Leo smart contracts on Aleo.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-leo-recorder record <leo-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-leo-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-leo-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a Leo source file.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<leo-file>`          | Path to the `.leo` source file             |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain Aleo program.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                                      |\x0A| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--program-id <ID>`   | On-chain program ID (e.g., `credits.aleo`)                       |\x0A| `--function <NAME>`   | Function to execute                                              |\x0A| `--input <VALUE>`     | Input values (repeatable, e.g., `--input 10u32`)                 |\x0A| `--endpoint <URL>`    | Aleo node REST API (default: `https://api.explorer.aleo.org/v1`) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                       |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-ton-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces Tolk/FunC smart contracts on TON.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-ton-recorder record <tolk-file> [options]\x0Acodetracer-ton-recorder trace-sandbox [options]\x0Acodetracer-ton-recorder replay [options]\x0Acodetracer-ton-recorder version\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a Tolk source file.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<tolk-file>`         | Path to the `.tolk` source file            |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)     |\x0A\x0A**trace-sandbox** \xE2\x80\x94 Convert `@ton/sandbox` VM log output.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                         |\x0A| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--vm-log <FILE>`     | Path to vm_logs_full output                         |\x0A| `--source <FILE>`     | Path to Tolk source file (default: `contract.tolk`) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)          |\x0A\x0A**replay** \xE2\x80\x94 Replay an on-chain TON transaction.\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                                         |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--tx-hash <HASH>`    | Transaction hash (hex-encoded)                                      |\x0A| `--address <ADDR>`    | Contract address                                                    |\x0A| `--endpoint <URL>`    | Liteserver endpoint (default: `https://ton.org/global-config.json`) |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>` | Contract source directory                                           |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                          |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### codetracer-js-recorder\x0A\x0ATraces JavaScript and TypeScript programs.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-js-recorder record <file> [options] [-- app-args]\x0Acodetracer-js-recorder instrument <src> --out <dir> [options]\x0A```\x0A\x0A**record** \xE2\x80\x94 Record a JS/TS program.\x0A\x0A| Flag               | Description                                      |\x0A| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<file>`           | Entry file or directory                          |\x0A| `--out-dir <DIR>`  | Trace output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`) |\x0A| `--format <FMT>`   | `json` or `binary` (default: `json`)             |\x0A| `--include <GLOB>` | Include glob pattern (repeatable)                |\x0A| `--exclude <GLOB>` | Exclude glob pattern (repeatable)                |\x0A| `-- <args>`        | Arguments passed to the instrumented program     |\x0A\x0A**instrument** \xE2\x80\x94 Instrument source files without executing.\x0A\x0A| Flag               | Description                       |\x0A| ------------------ | --------------------------------- |\x0A| `<src>`            | Source file or directory          |\x0A| `--out <DIR>`      | Output directory (required)       |\x0A| `--source-maps`    | Emit source maps                  |\x0A| `--include <GLOB>` | Include glob pattern (repeatable) |\x0A| `--exclude <GLOB>` | Exclude glob pattern (repeatable) |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### Python Recorder\x0A\x0AThe Python recorder is a pip-installable package, not a standalone binary.\x0A\x0A```\x0Apython -m codetracer_python_recorder [options] <script> [-- args]\x0Apython -m codetracer_python_recorder --pytest [pytest-args]\x0Apython -m codetracer_python_recorder --unittest [unittest-args]\x0A```\x0A\x0AUsually invoked automatically via `ct record script.py`. See the [Python Getting Started](../getting_started/python.md) guide.\x0A\x0A| Flag                        | Description                                           |\x0A| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<script>`                  | Python script to trace                                |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`       | Output directory (default: `./trace-out/`)            |\x0A| `--format <FMT>`            | `binary` or `json`                                    |\x0A| `--pytest [ARGS]`           | Run pytest with arguments                             |\x0A| `--unittest [ARGS]`         | Run unittest with arguments                           |\x0A| `--activation-path <PATH>`  | Gate tracing to specific path                         |\x0A| `--trace-filter <PATH>`     | Trace filter file path (repeatable)                   |\x0A| `--io-capture <MODE>`       | Stdout/stderr capture: `off`, `proxies`, `proxies+fd` |\x0A| `--on-recorder-error <ACT>` | Response to errors: `abort` or `disable`              |\x0A| `--require-trace`           | Exit 1 if no trace produced                           |\x0A| `--keep-partial-trace`      | Preserve partial traces on failure                    |\x0A| `--log-level <LEVEL>`       | Log verbosity (e.g., `info`, `debug`)                 |\x0A| `--log-file <PATH>`         | Write logs to file instead of stderr                  |\x0A| `--json-errors`             | Emit JSON error trailers on stderr                    |\x0A\x0A---\x0A\x0A### Ruby Recorder\x0A\x0AThe Ruby recorder is a gem installed alongside your Ruby environment.\x0A\x0A```\x0Acodetracer-ruby-recorder <script.rb> [args]\x0A```\x0A\x0AUsually invoked automatically via `ct record script.rb`. See the [Ruby Getting Started](../getting_started/ruby.md) guide.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-1307137777), Field1: [115,105,103,110,45,105,110,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Sign In\x0Adescription: Sign in to your CodeTracer account.\x0Alayout: minimal\x0Ahidden: true\x0A---\x0A# Sign In\x0A\x0ASign in to your CodeTracer account.\x0A\x0A:::form action=\"/sign-in\" method=\"get\" submit=\"Sign in\"\x0A@field name=\"Email\" label=\"Email address\" type=email placeholder=\"Email\" required\x0A@field name=\"Password\" label=\"Password\" type=password placeholder=\"Password\" required\x0A@field name=\"remember\" label=\"Remember me\" type=checkbox\x0A:::\x0A\x0ANot registered? [Sign up](/sign-up).\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 511093009, Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,98,117,105,108,100,105,110,103,95,100,111,99,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Building the documentation\x0Aorder: 4\x0A---\x0A## Building the documentation\x0A\x0AThe documenation for codetracer is written in Markdown using [mdbook](https://rust-lang.github.io/mdBook/).\x0AWe use some extensions from [GitHub Flavoured Markdown](https://docs.github.com/en/get-started/writing-on-github/getting-started-with-writing-and-formatting-on-github/basic-writing-and-formatting-syntax) : specifically alert boxes with [mdbook-alerts](https://crates.io/crates/mdbook-alerts).\x0A\x0ATo build the documentation run `just build-docs`. If you want to iterate on the documentation for local development, run `just serve-docs [<hostname>] [<port>]` or by going in the docs directory and running `mdbook serve [--hostname <hostname>] [--port <port>]` and a web server will be started, by default on http://localhost:3000 .\x0A\x0AThe built doc files are stored under `docs/experimental-documentation/build`, while the markdown files are under `docs/experimental-documentation` and its child directories.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-992971630), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,103,117,105,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Graphical interface\x0Aorder: 2\x0A---\x0A# Graphical User Interface (GUI)\x0A\x0AThe CodeTracer GUI is the primary tool for replaying and analyzing trace files. It provides a rich, interactive environment to inspect every detail of your program\'s execution.\x0A\x0AYou can launch the GUI in a few ways:\x0A*   Run `ct` in your terminal to open the Startup Screen.\x0A*   Run `ct replay` returns a list of recorded traces. Enter the number of a trace to open it in CodeTracer\'s UI.\x0A*   Run `ct run <application>` to record a new trace and immediately open it for replay.\x0A\x0ANote: For some programming languages it is required to provide the path to a source file that contains the entry point, for other languages the path to the project folder is sufficient.\x0AExample:\x0A\x0A```\x0Act run examples/ruby_space_ship/main.rb\x0Act run examples/noir_space_ship\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Startup Screen\x0A\x0AIf you start CodeTracer by just running the `ct` command you will see the startup screen.\x0A\x0AFrom here, you can:\x0A*   **View and open recent traces:** The main list shows traces you have recently recorded or opened. You can click on any trace to open it in the Replay Interface.\x0A*   **Open a local trace:** Click the \"Open local trace\" button to open a trace file from your computer. This is useful for opening traces that are not in your recent list or for opening traces shared by others.\x0A*   **Record a new trace:** The \"Record new trace\" button allows you to start a new recording session. This is currently supported for Noir projects. You will be prompted to select the root folder of your Noir project, and CodeTracer will handle the rest. Once the recording is complete, the new trace will open automatically.\x0A\x0A## Replay Interface\x0A\x0AThe Replay Interface is where you will spend most of your time analyzing traces. It is composed of several panels, each providing a different view into your program\'s state.\x0A\x0AWatch this video for a demonstration of the key features:\x0A\x0A![CodeTracer Replay Demo](https://img.youtube.com/vi/xZsJ55JVqmU/maxresdefault.jpg)\x0A\x0A[Watch on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xZsJ55JVqmU)\x0A\x0AHere is a brief overview of the main components:\x0A\x0A*   **Source Code Panel:** Displays your source code, highlighting the currently executing line. Thanks to a feature called \"Omniscience\", taken code branches are also highlighted while non-executed code is grayed out. This allows you to see the future and the past: you can click on any line to see the program\'s state and the values of all variables at that point in time. You can also right-click to add tracepoints.\x0A*   **Filesystem Panel:** Provides a tree-like view of the project\'s source code, allowing you to browse and open files.\x0A*   **Calltrace Panel:** Shows the entire execution trace as a hierarchical list of function calls. You can navigate through the program\'s execution by jumping to any point in the call trace.\x0A*   **State Panel:** Displays the state of all local variables at the currently selected point in the execution. As you navigate through the code, this panel updates to reflect the variables that are accessible from the current line of code as well as their exact values.\x0AThe state panel also gives quick access to a variable\'s value history.\x0A*   **Scratchpad Panel:** Allows you to \"set aside\" a specific variable or object at its current state and conveniently browse through object properties or big values.\x0A*   **Event Log:** A log of all significant events that occurred during the execution like stdout and strerr streams, read events, write events, network events, trace events, file operations, errors and evm events.\x0A*   **Terminal Output Panel:** Shows the complete terminal output (stdout/stderr) of the traced program. Clicking on any part of the terminal output will result in a jump to the exact moment when the character has been printed.\x0A\x0AAll the components are designed to let you move freely through your program\'s execution, making it easy to pinpoint the cause of bugs or understand complex behavior.\x0A\x0A## Visual Replay\x0A\x0AWhen you open an MCR `.ct` trace that contains captured graphics activity, CodeTracer starts the visual replay pipeline automatically. The GUI extracts the graphics stream from the trace, starts the replay player, and adds panels for the rendered frame, draw calls, pixel history, and shader debugging.\x0A\x0ASee [Visual recordings](./visual_recordings.md) for the recording commands and the full GUI workflow.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-2098069613), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,101,108,105,120,105,114,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Elixir & Erlang\x0Aorder: 105\x0A---\x0A## Elixir & Erlang\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports Elixir 1.15 and newer, and Erlang/OTP 26 and newer.\x0A\x0AThe recorder is hosted in the\x0A[codetracer-beam-recorder](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer-beam-recorder)\x0Arepo; `ct` finds it for you.\x0A\x0A## How to record an Elixir program\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act run hello.exs\x0A```\x0A\x0AArguments after the filename are passed through unchanged:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act run script.exs --count 10\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor a Mix project, record the command you would normally run:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record -o ./trace mix run -e \'MyApp.main()\'\x0Act replay ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Recording a Phoenix or Plug application\x0A\x0AWeb applications are recorded as a running server. That has its own guide:\x0A[Live requests \xE2\x80\x94 Elixir & Erlang](/usage_guide/live-requests-elixir).\x0A\x0AIn short:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang elixir -o ./trace -- mix phx.server\x0A```\x0A\x0AThen `ct replay -t ./trace` in another terminal shows requests as they\x0Aarrive.\x0A\x0A## BEAM processes in a recording\x0A\x0AElixir code tends to spawn many lightweight processes. These are recorded as\x0A*threads* within a single recording rather than as separate recordings, so\x0Amessage passing between them stays visible in one place, and you can follow\x0Awork as it moves from one process to another.\x0A")}, {Field0: 42884883, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,99,111,100,101,116,114,97,99,101,114,95,115,104,101,108,108,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: CodeTracer Shell\x0Aorder: 4\x0A---\x0A## Codetracer Shell\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AThis feature is in its infancy, but in development. The API, implementation approach and feature set/goals may be subject to change!\x0A\x0AIt\'s not a part of the public CodeTracer feature set or source code for now.\x0A:::\x0A\x0ACodeTracer Shell is a special feature of CodeTracer that creates an environment, and optionally an interactive shell,\x0Athat can be used to build applications with complex, or unorthodox build systems.\x0A\x0ACodeTracer requires some additional compile flags and instrumentation functions to be built with your application.\x0ABecause of this, CodeTracer only supports building from a single file. Support for multi-file projects using build systems\x0Ais instead relegated to CodeTracer Shell.\x0A\x0AWhen you execute commands in the CodeTracer Shell\'s interactive shell, or environment, CodeTracer Shell inserts itself into\x0Athe subprocesses that are launched during the build process of your application, making building and running applications\x0Awith more complex build systems easier.\x0A\x0AFor example, let\'s say you want to compile a C/C++ application using CMake. With CodeTracer Shell you can do either:\x0A```\x0Auser $ ct shell\x0A(ct shell) user $ mkdir build && cd build\x0A(ct shell) user $ cmake ..\x0A(ct shell) user $ make\x0A```\x0Ausing the interactive shell, or the following using commands:\x0A```\x0Auser $ ct shell -- bash -c \"mkdir build && cd build && cmake .. && make\"\x0A```\x0A")}, {Field0: 1371625361, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,105,110,100,101,120,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Usage Guide\x0Aorder: 0\x0A---\x0A# Overview\x0A\x0AWelcome to the CodeTracer Usage Guide. This guide will help you understand how to use CodeTracer effectively to debug your applications.\x0A\x0A## Core Concept: Record and Replay\x0A\x0AUnlike traditional debuggers that attach to a running process, CodeTracer works by first **recording** your application\'s execution into a trace file. This trace captures everything that happens during the run.\x0A\x0AOnce a recording is made, you can **replay** it as many times as you need in the CodeTracer GUI. This allows you to inspect the application\'s state at any point in time, move forwards and backwards through the execution, and use powerful features like tracepoints without having to run your application again.\x0A\x0AThink of it like recording a video of your program\'s execution that you can then analyze in detail.\x0A\x0A## How to Use This Guide\x0A\x0AThis guide is structured to help you get started quickly and then dive deeper into the features that interest you.\x0A\x0A*   **To learn about the different ways to interact with CodeTracer**, see the following sections:\x0A    *   [**Graphical User Interface (GUI)**](./gui.md): Learn how to use the visual interface to replay traces, inspect state, and set tracepoints.\x0A    *   [**Command-Line Interface (CLI)**](./cli.md): Discover how to record traces and manage trace files from your terminal.\x0A*   **To learn about specific features**, see these sections:\x0A    *   [**Tracepoints**](./tracepoints.md): A deep dive into using tracepoints for advanced debugging scenarios.\x0A*   **To review a change with its recordings attached**, see the\x0A    [**DeepReview**](/deep_review) section: what each changed line did \xE2\x80\x94 which\x0A    lines ran, with which values \xE2\x80\x94 shown on the diff itself, plus the agent\x0A    handoff.\x0A*   **For advanced use cases**, explore these topics:\x0A    *   [**CodeTracer Shell**](./codetracer_shell.md): For integrating CodeTracer with complex or custom build systems.\x0A")}, {Field0: 293425555, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,118,97,108,117,101,45,111,114,105,103,105,110,45,116,114,97,99,107,105,110,103,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Value Origin Tracking\x0Aorder: 106\x0A---\x0A# Value Origin Tracking\x0A\x0A**Value Origin Tracking** answers the question \"where did this value come\x0Afrom?\" by walking backward through the recorded execution. Starting from\x0Aa variable at a chosen step, CodeTracer follows assignments, parameter\x0Apasses, return captures, and field/index accesses until it reaches a\x0A**terminator** \xE2\x80\x94 a computational expression (e.g. `a + b`), a literal, a\x0Afunction parameter at the recording boundary, an external read, or one\x0Aof a handful of other well-defined stopping conditions. The result is an\x0A**origin chain**: an ordered list of **hops**, each classified by the\x0Akind of dataflow step it represents, ending in a terminator that\x0Aexplains why the search stopped.\x0A\x0AThis is distinct from value _history_ (which lists the prior values a\x0Avariable held). History tells you \"what values has this variable had?\";\x0Aorigin tracking tells you \"what computation produced this particular\x0Avalue?\". The two compose well \xE2\x80\x94 pinning a historic value and then asking\x0Afor its origin is one of the canonical workflows.\x0A\x0AThis chapter describes the user-facing surfaces of value-origin tracking.\x0A\x0A## When to use it\x0A\x0AOrigin tracking is most useful when:\x0A\x0A- A variable has an unexpected value and you want to know which\x0A  assignment line produced it, not just \"what step set it last\".\x0A- A bug appears far from its root cause and you want to walk back across\x0A  function boundaries without manually stepping out frame-by-frame.\x0A- You are reviewing data flowing across a wrapper / forwarder /\x0A  serialisation boundary and want to skip the trivial copies that\x0A  shuttle the value around.\x0A- An agent or replay script needs to programmatically explain how a\x0A  value was computed.\x0A\x0AIf you only need \"what was the previous value of this variable\", the\x0Avalue-history popover is faster. Origin tracking is the right tool when\x0Ayou specifically care about the **source-level computation** that\x0Aproduced the value, not the sequence of prior values.\x0A\x0A## In the CodeTracer GUI\x0A\x0A### The inline origin badge\x0A\x0AEvery row in the Variable State Pane carries a compact **inline origin\x0Abadge** to the right of the value. The badge shows a small icon for the\x0Aterminator kind (literal, computational expression, parameter at entry,\x0Aexternal read, etc.) and a truncated label with the terminator\'s source\x0Aexpression:\x0A\x0A```\x0Atotal       137    [\xE2\x97\x8E a + b]\x0Aitems       [..]   [\xE2\x97\x8E list literal]\x0Actx         {..}   [\xE2\x97\x8E param at entry]\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe badge is pre-fetched together with the locals \xE2\x80\x94 no user gesture is\x0Arequired to see it. When the backend deferred the chain (e.g. for\x0Aoff-screen history rows), the badge renders as a low-contrast `[?]`\x0Apill; clicking it issues a lazy lookup and replaces the placeholder in\x0Aplace.\x0A\x0A### Expanding the chain in place\x0A\x0AClicking the badge expands the row to show the full hop chain inline,\x0Anewest hop first, ending in the terminator. Each hop renders on two\x0Ashort lines:\x0A\x0A- a header line with a small pie-chart timeline indicator,\x0A  `filename:line`, and one or two SVG icons (classification icon plus an\x0A  optional `\xE2\x86\x98`/`\xE2\x86\x97` frame-transition arrow);\x0A- the source line of the assignment, monospace.\x0A\x0ATrivial-copy hops are de-emphasised; the computational origin hop is\x0Ahighlighted with a subtle accent background. For computational hops a\x0Achevron expands a per-operand snapshot table (`name = value` rows).\x0A\x0AClicking the badge again collapses the chain.\x0A\x0A### Reading an origin chain\x0A\x0AThree concepts cover everything you see in the panel:\x0A\x0A- **Hops** \xE2\x80\x94 each row represents one dataflow step. The\x0A  classification icon names *what kind* of step it was: a trivial copy\x0A  (`b = a`), a field or index access (`x.field`, `arr[i]`), a return\x0A  capture (`y = foo()`), a parameter pass (the callee receives the\x0A  caller\'s argument), an explicit computation (`r = a + b`), or \xE2\x80\x94 on\x0A  RR/MCR backends \xE2\x80\x94 a cross-thread copy. The full enum is in the\x0A  [Origin kinds reference](../reference/origin-kinds.md).\x0A- **Frame transitions** \xE2\x80\x94 the inline `\xE2\x86\x98`/`\xE2\x86\x97` glyphs mark the hops\x0A  where the chain crosses a function boundary: `\xE2\x86\x98` when the chain\x0A  steps *into* a callee through a `ParameterPass`, `\xE2\x86\x97` when it returns\x0A  *up* into the caller through a `ReturnCapture`. The chain seeks to\x0A  the right frame automatically when you click such a hop.\x0A- **Terminator** \xE2\x80\x94 the bottom row is not a regular hop. It explains\x0A  *why the backward walk stopped*. The terminator kind is the closed\x0A  enum `TerminatorKind` \xE2\x80\x94 for example `Literal` (a literal expression\x0A  bottomed out the chain), `Computational` (the chain ended at an\x0A  expression like `a + b`), `ParameterAtRecordStart` (the value was\x0A  already in a parameter when recording began), `RecordingStart`\x0A  (walked off the beginning of the trace), `DepthLimit` /\x0A  `OutOfBudget` (the walker stopped under a configured cap), or\x0A  `UnknownSource` / `UnknownVariable` (the classifier could not parse\x0A  the line). The full enum lives in the\x0A  [Origin kinds reference](../reference/origin-kinds.md).\x0A\x0AThe order is always newest-first: the topmost hop is the most recent\x0Aassignment to the queried value, and the terminator at the bottom is\x0Athe original source of the data.\x0A\x0A### Pinning, breadcrumbs, and copying\x0A\x0AInside the side panel a few small navigation aids accumulate as you\x0Awork:\x0A\x0A- **Breadcrumbs at the top of the panel** record the\x0A  `(variable, step)` pairs you have already inspected in this session.\x0A  Clicking a breadcrumb jumps back to that query without re-issuing the\x0A  backend round-trip. These breadcrumbs are *session-local* \xE2\x80\x94 they\x0A  reflect *your navigation path* through chains, not anything intrinsic\x0A  to the trace. (Under the hood this is the `breadcrumbStack` signal in\x0A  `OriginChainVM`.)\x0A- **Pinning** a hop or a full chain copies it into the **Scratchpad\x0A  Pane** as a folded card. Pin two chains side-by-side and CodeTracer\x0A  renders a unified hop diff \xE2\x80\x94 useful for \"why is iteration 17\x0A  different from iteration 16?\". Right-click a hop or the chain header\x0A  to access pin actions.\x0A- **Copy as markdown** (right-click the chain header) serialises the\x0A  chain to a markdown fragment with the per-hop table, ready to paste\x0A  into a bug report. The same renderer is exposed as the `markdown`\x0A  format on the `ct trace origin` CLI.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AThe breadcrumb chips described above are distinct from\x0Athe *cross-trace* `CrossProcessSpan` boundary chips covered below\x0Ain [Cross-tracer sessions](#cross-tracer-sessions) \xE2\x80\x94 those chips\x0Areflect the chain crossing recording boundaries, not your navigation\x0Ahistory.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### The dedicated side panel\x0A\x0AFor deeper chains that crowd the narrow State Pane, right-clicking a\x0Avariable row exposes the **\"Show value origin\"** action. This opens the\x0Asame chain in a dedicated **Origin Chain Side Panel** with richer\x0Alayout: full file paths, taller per-hop rows, operand tables, and\x0Abreadcrumbs across previously-queried `(variable, step)` pairs.\x0A\x0AThe same panel can be opened directly from the keyboard with\x0A`Ctrl+Shift+O` on Linux/Windows or `Cmd+Shift+O` on macOS (the default\x0Abinding for `codetracer.showValueOrigin`; rebind it through the\x0Astandard keybinding settings).\x0A\x0AInside the side panel you can:\x0A\x0A- **Click a hop** to seek to that step (the editor pane scrolls and the\x0A  call-trace pane opens the relevant frame).\x0A- **Cmd/Ctrl-click a hop** to open the file at `(path, line)` in a new\x0A  tab without changing the active step.\x0A- **Click an operand** in an expanded computational hop to recursively\x0A  query its origin at the origin step.\x0A- **Right-click a hop \xE2\x86\x92 \"Pin to scratchpad\"** to drop the hop into the\x0A  Scratchpad Pane for later comparison.\x0A- **Right-click the chain \xE2\x86\x92 \"Copy as markdown\"** to serialise the chain\x0A  for a bug report.\x0A\x0A### Editor and scratchpad integration\x0A\x0AWhile a chain is active a subtle gutter glyph appears on every editor\x0Aline that participates in the chain; hovering the glyph shows a hop\x0Asummary. Pinned chains in the Scratchpad Pane render as folded cards,\x0Aand pinning two chains side-by-side renders a unified diff of the hop\x0Asequences (useful for \"why is iteration 17 different from iteration\x0A16?\").\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AThe Origin Chain Side Panel screenshot for this section is\x0Apending \xE2\x80\x94 the Playwright capture suite runs end-to-end on\x0Arecorder-equipped CI runners but SKIPs cleanly in dev shells without\x0Athe Python recorder installed.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## In VS Code\x0A\x0AThe CodeTracer VS Code extension embeds the same Origin Chain Panel\x0Ainside a webview. The same gestures apply:\x0A\x0A- Inline origin badges render on every row of the embedded State Pane.\x0A- `Ctrl+Shift+O` / `Cmd+Shift+O` invokes the\x0A  `ct-vscode.showValueOrigin` command and opens the embedded panel.\x0A- Right-clicking a variable row in the State Pane exposes the\x0A  **\"Show value origin\"** entry.\x0A- Clicking a hop dispatches an `OriginChainVM.onSeekToHop` event which\x0A  the extension translates into a `ct/history-jump` followed by a VS\x0A  Code editor reveal, so the active editor scrolls to the hop\'s\x0A  `(path, line)`.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AThe animated screencast of the VS Code experience is pending\x0A\xE2\x80\x94 see the M7 verification entries for capture status.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### VS Code Event Log marker chips\x0A\x0AWhen a session has correlation markers (see the M25 docs in the spec),\x0Athe VS Code Event Log gains a row per marker firing with:\x0A\x0A- a **direction icon** \xE2\x80\x94 `\xE2\x86\x91` for a `Send` (the marker recorded a\x0A  value crossing *out* of the recording at this boundary) and `\xE2\x86\x93` for\x0A  a `Recv` (the value crossing *in*);\x0A- a **boundary chip** \xE2\x80\x94 the marker\'s `boundary_id` (for example\x0A  `http`, `js-wasm-realm`) rendered as a small filled badge;\x0A- the marker\'s **`show_value`** \xE2\x80\x94 the expression value the marker\x0A  declaration captured at that firing, formatted per the marker\'s\x0A  `format` field (default `summary:80`, middle-ellipsis-truncated).\x0A\x0AClicking the chip on a marker row **jumps to the counterpart row**:\x0Awhen the counterpart is in a sibling recording the active process\x0Aswitches first (via `ct/listProcesses` + `ct/goto-ticks`), then the\x0Atimeline seeks to the counterpart\'s step, then the Event Log scrolls\x0Ato highlight the counterpart row. While markers are still loading the\x0Apanel shows a `loading correlation markers\xE2\x80\xA6 X / Y firings` banner;\x0Ajump buttons defer until both endpoints of a pair are loaded.\x0A\x0AThe same chips + jump targets render in the CodeTracer Electron GUI\'s\x0AEvent Log pane \xE2\x80\x94 both surfaces drive the same `EventLogVM`.\x0A\x0A## Cross-tracer sessions\x0A\x0AWhen CodeTracer opens a multi-recording session via `session.toml`,\x0Athe same Origin Chain Side Panel additionally renders\x0A**`CrossProcessSpan` boundary chips** at every cross-recording hop.\x0AA chip shows which recording the next hop range belongs to (one chip\x0Aper process the chain visits \xE2\x80\x94 JS frontend, WASM module, Python\x0Abackend, etc.) and clicking it switches the active recording and\x0Aseeks to the matched boundary step.\x0A\x0AThis is distinct from the session-local breadcrumbs described above:\x0Abreadcrumbs reflect *your navigation history* through prior queries,\x0Awhile `CrossProcessSpan` chips are an intrinsic property of the\x0Areturned chain \xE2\x80\x94 they tell you \"the next K hops happen inside\x0Arecording R\" and they survive switching the active recording.\x0A\x0AThe canonical user-facing walkthrough for cross-tracer chain walking\x0Alives in [Cross-Tracer Demo](./cross-tracer-demo.md), which boots the\x0Athree-trace `account-balance-with-wasm` fixture so you can right-click\x0A`balance` in the backend, watch the chain render its `CrossProcessSpan`\x0Achips, and click through into the JS frontend and the WASM module\x0Awithout leaving the GUI.\x0A\x0A## From a Python replay script\x0A\x0AThe Python replay-script API exposes value origin through\x0A`trace.value_origin(...)`. The same script body runs through the MCP\x0A`exec_script` tool and the `ct trace exec --script` CLI subcommand, so\x0Aagents and CLI users share a single workflow:\x0A\x0A```python\x0A# example.py \xE2\x80\x94 passed through `ct trace exec --script example.py <trace>`\x0Achain = trace.value_origin(\"c\", step=42, max_hops=8)\x0A\x0A# Inspect the terminator\x0Aprint(chain.terminator.kind)        # e.g. \"Computational\"\x0Aprint(chain.terminator.expression)  # e.g. \"a + b\"\x0A\x0A# Walk the hops (newest first)\x0Afor hop in chain.hops:\x0A    print(hop.kind, hop.location.path, hop.location.line)\x0A\x0A# Render it as plain text or markdown\x0Aprint(chain.to_text())\x0Aprint(chain.to_markdown())\x0A```\x0A\x0A`value_origin` returns a `codetracer.origin.OriginChain` dataclass that\x0Amirrors the wire shape from spec \xC2\xA74.1 one-for-one\x0A(`OriginHop`, `OperandSnapshot`, `Terminator`, `FrameTransition`,\x0A`OriginMetrics`, plus the closed-enum types `OriginKind`,\x0A`TerminatorKind`, `FrameTransitionKind`). The full signature is:\x0A\x0A```python\x0Atrace.value_origin(\x0A    expression: str,\x0A    *,\x0A    step: int | None = None,\x0A    frame: int | None = None,\x0A    max_hops: int = 16,\x0A    lazy: bool = False,\x0A    continuation_token: str | None = None,\x0A) -> OriginChain\x0A```\x0A\x0AWhen `lazy=True` the backend may return early with a\x0A`continuation_token`; pass it back on the next call to resume the walk.\x0A\x0AThe binding is implemented in `python-api/codetracer/trace.py` and the\x0Adataclasses live in `python-api/codetracer/origin.py`.\x0A\x0A## From the CLI\x0A\x0AFor one-shot queries from the terminal use `ct trace origin`:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act trace origin <trace-path> --variable c --format text\x0A```\x0A\x0ACommon flags:\x0A\x0A- `--variable <NAME>` (required) \xE2\x80\x94 the identifier to query.\x0A- `--step <N>` \xE2\x80\x94 query at a specific step id (defaults to current).\x0A- `--frame <N>` \xE2\x80\x94 query within a specific DAP frame (defaults to the\x0A  topmost frame).\x0A- `--max-hops <N>` \xE2\x80\x94 limit the walk (default 16).\x0A- `--format <json|markdown|text>` \xE2\x80\x94 choose the renderer (default\x0A  `text`).\x0A- `--lazy` \xE2\x80\x94 allow the backend to return a continuation token instead\x0A  of walking the full chain.\x0A\x0AThe text renderer matches the ASCII layout from spec \xC2\xA73.2 (newest hop\x0Afirst, terminator at the bottom, frame-transition glyphs inline). The\x0Amarkdown renderer emits a fenced chain with a per-hop table \xE2\x80\x94 paste it\x0Astraight into a bug report. The JSON renderer pretty-prints the\x0Acanonical `OriginChain` wire shape so other tools can parse it.\x0A\x0AFor longer agent workflows that need to compose origin lookups with\x0Alocals/history/breakpoints, prefer\x0A`ct trace exec --script <file.py> <trace-path>` and call\x0A`trace.value_origin(...)` inside the script \xE2\x80\x94 the trace stays loaded\x0Aacross multiple calls, and the classifier\'s pattern cache is reused.\x0A\x0ASee the [`ct` CLI reference](../reference/ct_cli.md#ct-trace-origin) for\x0Athe full flag table.\x0A\x0A## From an MCP-capable agent\x0A\x0AThe CodeTracer MCP server registers two tools that surface origin\x0Atracking to LLM agents directly:\x0A\x0A- **`get_value_origin`** \xE2\x80\x94 one-shot lookup. Returns the canonical\x0A  `OriginChain` JSON for `variable` at `step`/`frame`. Use this when you\x0A  only need a single chain without authoring a script.\x0A- **`resolve_variable_step`** \xE2\x80\x94 helper that maps a variable name to the\x0A  most recent step at which it was assigned. Pair it with\x0A  `get_value_origin` when you want the chain at the assignment site\x0A  rather than the current step.\x0A\x0AThe **preferred** multi-step path is to send a Python script through the\x0Aexisting `exec_script` MCP tool and call `trace.value_origin(...)`\x0Ainside it \xE2\x80\x94 that composes with `trace.locals()`, `trace.history()`,\x0Abreakpoints, and watchpoints, and reuses the loaded trace + pattern\x0Acache across calls.\x0A\x0ASee the [MCP tool reference](../reference/mcp-tools.md) for the full\x0Ainput/output schemas.\x0A\x0A## Per-language coverage\x0A\x0ABackward-walk support is rolled out per language and per recording\x0Abackend:\x0A\x0A| Backend / Language                                | Status                                                                                                          |\x0A| ------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| Materialized DB \xE2\x80\x94 Python (`codetracer-python-recorder`) | Supported.                                                                                                      |\x0A| Materialized DB \xE2\x80\x94 Ruby (`codetracer-ruby-recorder`)     | Supported.                                                                                                      |\x0A| Materialized DB \xE2\x80\x94 JavaScript (`codetracer-js-recorder`) | Supported (with the granularity caveat from spec \xC2\xA76.1.4).                                                       |\x0A| RR-driver \xE2\x80\x94 C / C++ / Rust / Nim / Go / D         | Planned (M11). Uses watchpoint + reverse-continue against `codetracer-native-backend`.                          |\x0A| MCR \xE2\x80\x94 hybrid backend                              | Planned (M14). Uses the omniscient undo-map for the last mile and breakpoints for deeper prefixes.              |\x0A| MCR \xE2\x80\x94 omniscient acceleration                     | Planned (M21, gated on the M10d\xE2\x80\x93e omniscient DB).                                                               |\x0A\x0AOrigin chains across all backends produce the same response shape; only\x0Athe realistic maximum hop depth, the quality of `kind` classification,\x0Aand the recording-boundary behaviour differ between backends.\x0A\x0A## Customising classification\x0A\x0ACodeTracer ships with a built-in catalogue of language-specific patterns\x0Athat identify trivial copies, forwarders, and field/index accesses.\x0AProjects can extend or override the catalogue without touching CodeTracer\x0Aitself.\x0A\x0APatterns live in TOML files. The classifier evaluates them in this\x0A**precedence order**, first match wins:\x0A\x0A1. **Trace-local overrides** \xE2\x80\x94 `meta_dat/origin-patterns/_overrides.toml`\x0A   inside the trace folder. The recorder never writes this file; create\x0A   it explicitly when you want to override an embedded library default\x0A   for a specific trace.\x0A2. **User-personal overrides** \xE2\x80\x94\x0A   `~/.config/codetracer/origin-patterns.toml`. Use this for judgement\x0A   calls you want applied across every trace you replay.\x0A3. **Embedded library patterns** \xE2\x80\x94 `meta_dat/origin-patterns/` inside the\x0A   trace, populated automatically by the recorder from each library\'s\x0A   `.codetracer/origin-patterns.toml`.\x0A4. **Built-in catalogue** \xE2\x80\x94 the language-specific default patterns that\x0A   ship with CodeTracer.\x0A\x0AA typical project authors `.codetracer/origin-patterns.toml` at the\x0Aproject root; the recorder picks it up and embeds it under\x0A`meta_dat/origin-patterns/` so the patterns travel with the trace and\x0Aremain reproducible on any machine that opens it.\x0A\x0APattern files use a single TOML schema across all four levels. See spec\x0A\xC2\xA77.4 for the full schema reference. A common case is marking a wrapper\x0Aconstructor as a trivial copy so the chain skips it:\x0A\x0A```toml\x0A[[trivial_copy]]\x0A# Skip Wrapper.into() \xE2\x80\x94 it just unboxes the inner value.\x0Alanguage = \"rust\"\x0Acall_pattern = \"Wrapper::into\"\x0Acontinuation = \"self.inner\"\x0A```\x0A\x0AAll four surfaces above \xE2\x80\x94 the GUI, the VS Code embedded panel, the MCP\x0Atools, and the CLI subcommand \xE2\x80\x94 honour the same precedence stack, so a\x0Apattern added at any level applies uniformly to every caller.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A- [`ct` CLI reference](../reference/ct_cli.md#ct-trace-origin) \xE2\x80\x94 full\x0A  flag table for `ct trace origin`.\x0A- [MCP tool reference](../reference/mcp-tools.md) \xE2\x80\x94 input/output\x0A  schemas for `get_value_origin` and `resolve_variable_step`.\x0A- [Origin kinds reference](../reference/origin-kinds.md) \xE2\x80\x94 the closed\x0A  `OriginKind`, `TerminatorKind`, and `FrameTransitionKind` enums with\x0A  per-variant guidance.\x0A- [Cross-Tracer Demo](./cross-tracer-demo.md) \xE2\x80\x94 interactive launcher\x0A  for the three-trace cross-process chain-walking scenario.\x0A- `codetracer-specs/GUI/Debugging-Features/Value-Origin-Tracking.md` \xE2\x80\x94\x0A  the authoritative feature spec (UI layout, wire schemas, algorithm\x0A  contracts, per-language semantics).\x0A- `codetracer-specs/Planned-Features/Value-Origin-Campaign-Closeout.md`\x0A  \xE2\x80\x94 campaign close-out memo enumerating the milestone set that lands\x0A  the four user-facing surfaces described in this chapter.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 358167449, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,115,116,121,108,117,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Stylus\x0Aorder: 3\x0A---\x0A## Getting Started with Stylus\x0A\x0AThis guide will walk you through tracing an Arbitrum Stylus smart contract with CodeTracer. The process involves deploying a contract and then sending transactions to it, which CodeTracer will automatically record.\x0A\x0AThe implementation is based on a `wazero` WASM interpreter and has been tested with Stylus contracts. You can find more information on `wazero` [here](https://wazero.io/).\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A*   **Rust `wasm32` Target:** You must have the `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target installed for Rust. You can add it by running:\x0A    ```bash\x0A    rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown\x0A    ```\x0A*   **Three Terminal Windows:** You will need three separate terminals to run all the necessary components.\x0A\x0A### Environment Setup\x0A\x0ATo follow this guide, you need a local Arbitrum development node and Foundry\'s `cast` tool. We provide instructions for two setups: a Nix-based environment (recommended for Linux) and a manual setup for macOS.\x0A\x0A<details>\x0A<summary><b>Option 1: Nix-based Setup (Recommended for Linux)</b></summary>\x0A\x0AThis is the easiest way to get started if you have Nix installed.\x0A\x0AIn your **first terminal**, launch the Stylus development environment. This command will download all the necessary tools.\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Anix develop \'github:metacraft-labs/nix-blockchain-development?ref=stylus-tools#stylus\'\x0A```\x0A\x0AOnce inside the new shell, start the local Arbitrum node:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Arun-nitro-devnode\x0A```\x0A\x0AYou should see it producing blocks. Keep this terminal running.\x0A\x0AFor sending transactions in Step 2, you will also use this Nix shell in your **third terminal** to get access to the `cast` command-line tool.\x0A\x0A</details>\x0A\x0A<details>\x0A<summary><b>Option 2: Manual Setup (macOS)</b></summary>\x0A\x0AIf you are on macOS or do not use Nix, follow these steps.\x0A\x0A**1. Install Foundry**\x0A\x0AInstall `foundryup` by following the instructions at https://getfoundry.sh. This will provide the `cast` command-line tool.\x0A\x0A**2. Install Stylus**\x0A\x0ARun the following command to install the Stylus CLI:\x0A```bash\x0Acargo install cargo-stylus\x0A```\x0A:::important\x0AMake sure `cargo` is from a `rustup` installation, not from Homebrew. If you installed Rust via Homebrew, this step might fail.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A**3. Run the Local Devnode**\x0A\x0AIn your **first terminal**, clone and run the `nitro-devnode`:\x0A```bash\x0Agit clone https://github.com/OffchainLabs/nitro-devnode.git\x0Acd nitro-devnode\x0A./run-dev-node.sh\x0A```\x0AYou should see it producing blocks. Keep this terminal running.\x0A\x0A</details>\x0A\x0A### Step 1: Deploy the Demo Contract\x0A\x0AThis step is the same for both setups.\x0A\x0AIn your **second terminal**, navigate to the directory of the example program and use CodeTracer to deploy the smart contract. The `ct arb deploy` command handles this for you.\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Navigate to the example contract\x0Acd test-programs/stylus_fund_tracker\x0A\x0A# Deploy it to the local devnode\x0Act arb deploy\x0A```\x0A\x0AAfter a moment, CodeTracer will print the deployed smart contract address. **Copy this address**, as you will need it in the next step.\x0A\x0A### Step 2: Send Transactions\x0A\x0AIn your **third terminal**, you will use the `cast` tool to send transactions.\x0A\x0A-   **Nix users:** Enter the Stylus development shell first:\x0A    ```bash\x0A    nix develop \'github:metacraft-labs/nix-blockchain-development?ref=stylus-tools#stylus\'\x0A    ```\x0A-   **macOS users:** The `cast` command should be available in your PATH if you installed Foundry correctly.\x0A\x0ANext, set up some environment variables to make the commands easier to read. **Paste the contract address you copied from the previous step.**\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# The private key for the pre-funded devnet account\x0Aexport PK=0xb6b15c8cb491557369f3c7d2c287b053eb229daa9c22138887752191c9520659\x0A\x0A# The RPC URL for the local devnode\x0Aexport RPC_URL=http://localhost:8547\x0A\x0A# The address of your deployed contract\x0Aexport CONTRACT_ADDR=\"<paste-your-contract-address-here>\"\x0A```\x0A\x0ANow you can use `cast send` to call functions on your contract. These transactions will be automatically recorded by CodeTracer.\x0A\x0ALet\'s send a few funding transactions and then trigger a custom event:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Fund with 9\x0Acast send --rpc-url \"$RPC_URL\" --private-key \"$PK\" \"$CONTRACT_ADDR\" \"fund(uint256)\" 9\x0A\x0A# Fund with 6\x0Acast send --rpc-url \"$RPC_URL\" --private-key \"$PK\" \"$CONTRACT_ADDR\" \"fund(uint256)\" 6\x0A\x0A# Fund with 11\x0Acast send --rpc-url \"$RPC_URL\" --private-key \"$PK\" \"$CONTRACT_ADDR\" \"fund(uint256)\" 11\x0A\x0A# Trigger the largeIncomes function\x0Acast send --rpc-url \"$RPC_URL\" --private-key \"$PK\" \"$CONTRACT_ADDR\" \"largeIncomes(uint256)\" 7\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Step 3: Explore the Recorded Traces\x0A\x0AGo back to your **second terminal** (where you ran `ct arb deploy`). Now, run the CodeTracer explorer:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act arb explorer\x0A```\x0A\x0AA window will open displaying the recent transactions. Select any transaction to view its full execution trace in CodeTracer.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-327914215), Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,98,117,105,108,100,95,115,121,115,116,101,109,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Build systems\x0Aorder: 0\x0A---\x0A## Build systems\x0A\x0ACodetracer uses nix, just, tup and direnv as parts of its build system.\x0A\x0A## Breakdown of the different components\x0A\x0A### Nix\x0A\x0AThe [Nix](https://nixos.org/) package manager/build system deals with managing all dependencies, required by CodeTracer, as well as packaging\x0Ait for NixOS. It can package for other operating systems through an AppImage generator utility, however currently we are building our\x0Aappimages with custom shell scripts in `appimage-scripts`.\x0A\x0A### Just\x0A\x0AThe [just](https://just.systems/) task runner is used to trigger build commands and various other actions. It\'s semi-analogous to a Makefile. The following commands are\x0Athe most widely-used:\x0A\x0A1. `just build` - Builds the project with Tup and starts the automatic build process, which is used for active development\x0A1. `just build-once` - Just builds the project using Tup\x0A1. `just build-nix` - Builds the project and packages it for Nix\x0A1. `just build-docs` - Builds the documentation. More info can be found [here](https://dev-docs.codetracer.com/Misc/BuildDocs)\x0A1. `just attic-push-nix-package` - Pushes nix package artefacts to Attic\x0A1. `just attic-push-devshell` - Pushes the current dev shell to Attic\x0A1. `just reset-db` - Resets the local user\'s trace database\x0A1. `just clear-local-traces` - Clears the local user\'s traces\x0A1. `just reset-layout` - Resets the GUI window arrangements layout if your user\'s layout is incompatible with the latest version of CodeTracer. Further [documentation](https://dev-docs.codetracer.com/Introduction/Configuration)\x0A1. `just reset-config` - Resets the user\'s configuration if it\'s incompatible with the latest version of CodeTracer. Further [documentation](https://dev-docs.codetracer.com/Introduction/Configuration)\x0A\x0A### Tup\x0A\x0AThe [Tup](https://gittup.org/tup/) build system is used for local builds of CodeTracer and deals with calling the actual low-level build instructions.\x0A\x0A### Direnv\x0AThe [direnv](https://direnv.net/) utility sets up your local environment for using CodeTracer.\x0A\x0A## Packaging\x0A\x0A### More detailed breakdown of the Nix package\x0A\x0AComing soon!\x0A\x0A### Packaging for non-NixOS distributions\x0A\x0A:::tip\x0AIf you\'re a user that wants a package for your distribution contact us. We\'re currently in the process of creating\x0Apackages for popular distributions, such as Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora/RHEL, Arch Linux, Gentoo, Void, etc.\x0A:::\x0A\x0ATo package for another Linux distribution with a ports-based package manager, you can utilise our AppImage(currently unreleased),\x0Awhich you can install to `/usr/bin`. Along with it, you should also install our icon and desktop file\x0Afrom `resources/` to the needed directories, such as `/usr/share/pixmaps` and `/usr/share/applications`.\x0A\x0A### Packaging for Windows(DB-backend only)\x0A\x0AComing soon!\x0A\x0A### Packaging for macOS(DB-backend only)\x0A\x0AComing soon!\x0A")}, {Field0: (-1584803559), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,108,105,118,101,45,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,115,45,114,117,98,121,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Live Requests \xE2\x80\x94 Ruby\x0Aorder: 7\x0A---\x0A# Live request tracking in Ruby\x0A\x0AWorks with any Rack application \xE2\x80\x94 Rails, Sinatra, Roda and friends. Request\x0Aspans are published by Rack middleware running inside your server process,\x0Aso there is nothing to add to your controllers.\x0A\x0A## Record your server\x0A\x0AStart the server under CodeTracer instead of directly:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang ruby -o ./trace -- rails server --port 3000\x0A```\x0A\x0Aor, for a Sinatra app:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang ruby -o ./trace -- ruby app.rb -p 4567\x0A```\x0A\x0AFlags after the program name are passed through unchanged. The command\x0Astays in the foreground; stop it with `Ctrl-C`, which is a normal end.\x0A\x0A## Watch requests arrive\x0A\x0AIn a second terminal, with the server still running:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay -t ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0ASend some traffic and the **Requests** panel fills in:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acurl http://localhost:3000/users/7\x0A```\x0A\x0ADouble-click a row to land in the action that handled it. Because the whole\x0Aserver is one recording, you can step backwards out of your controller into\x0Athe Rack middleware stack that ran first \xE2\x80\x94 including middleware you did not\x0Awrite.\x0A\x0A## Open a recording later\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A## What gets recorded\x0A\x0A- **Route patterns**, so `/users/7` and `/users/9` group under the same\x0A  endpoint.\x0A- **Status and duration** per request.\x0A- **Errors**, including the exception message for requests that raised.\x0A\x0A## Requirements\x0A\x0ARuby 3.0 or newer.\x0A\x0A:::caution\x0AOn macOS, install Ruby through [Homebrew](https://brew.sh) (`brew install\x0Aruby`). The Ruby that ships with macOS is many years old and recording\x0Aagainst it fails.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A:::note\x0ARails applications are supported. If you tried Rails with CodeTracer some\x0Atime ago and hit a hang during boot, that was a recorder bug in integer\x0Aconversion on large values; it is fixed.\x0A:::\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 59290145, Field1: [105,110,100,101,120,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Welcome to CodeTracer Docs\x0Adescription: CodeTracer is a user-friendly time-traveling debugger designed to support a wide range of programming languages. Install it, record your first trace, and explore the usage guide.\x0Aorder: 1\x0A---\x0A# Welcome to CodeTracer Docs\x0A\x0A:::hero title=\"Welcome to CodeTracer Docs\"\x0A:::button href=\"/getting_started/installation\" variant=\"primary\"\x0AGet Started\x0A:::button href=\"https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer/issues\" variant=\"secondary\"\x0ASupport\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Overview\x0A\x0ACodeTracer is a user-friendly time-traveling debugger designed to support a wide range of programming languages. It records the execution of a program into a shareable, self-contained trace file, which you can load to move forward and backward through the execution to examine the state of memory and variables at any point.\x0A\x0AUse the sidebar to get started with installation, learn how to record and replay traces for your language, and explore the usage guide.\x0A\x0A:::video xZsJ55JVqmU title=\"CodeTracer - Noir Release Demo\"\x0A\x0A## Start here\x0A\x0A:::cards\x0A:::card title=\"Getting Started\" icon=\"/assets/img/icon__start.svg\" href=\"/getting_started\"\x0AInstall CodeTracer and record your first trace \xE2\x80\x94 Noir, Stylus, WASM, Ruby and Python.\x0A:::card title=\"Reference\" icon=\"/assets/img/icon__style.svg\" href=\"/reference/build_systems\"\x0ABuild systems, contributing, troubleshooting, environment variables and building the docs.\x0A:::card title=\"Usage Guide\" icon=\"/assets/img/icon__components.svg\" href=\"/usage_guide\"\x0AThe command-line interface, the GUI replay interface, tracepoints and the CodeTracer shell.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Popular articles\x0A\x0A:::cards variant=\"compact\"\x0A:::card title=\"Introduction\" href=\"/getting_started/introduction\"\x0AGetting Started\x0A:::card title=\"Installation\" href=\"/getting_started/installation\"\x0AGetting Started\x0A:::card title=\"Tracepoints\" href=\"/usage_guide/tracepoints\"\x0AUsage Guide\x0A:::card title=\"Graphical interface\" href=\"/usage_guide/gui\"\x0AUsage Guide\x0A:::card title=\"Command-line interface\" href=\"/usage_guide/cli\"\x0AUsage Guide\x0A:::card title=\"Build systems\" href=\"/reference/build_systems\"\x0AReference\x0A:::\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-1945763162), Field1: [100,101,101,112,95,114,101,118,105,101,119,47,110,111,116,95,121,101,116,95,97,118,97,105,108,97,98,108,101,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Not yet available\x0Aorder: 4\x0A---\x0A\x0A# Not yet available\x0A\x0AEverything on this page is real behaviour you will meet, written down so you do\x0Anot read it as a bug or, worse, as a measurement. It is deliberately a page of\x0Aits own rather than a footnote at the end of a longer one: the fastest way to\x0Amislead a reviewer is to describe a surface that shows execution data without\x0Asaying which parts of it are not driven by the data yet.\x0A\x0A## The trace-context selector names the recordings; it does not filter yet\x0A\x0AChoosing a different context is remembered, but it does not re-drive the overlay\x0Afrom that recording. Coverage counts are the **sum across every recording** in\x0Athe dataset, and the invocation stepper walks every recorded call of a function\x0Aregardless of which run it came from. The selector\'s entries also show labels\x0Aonly (`run-1`) \xE2\x80\x94 both collectors leave each context\'s recording id empty.\x0A\x0A## `ct review inspect` reads native datasets only\x0A\x0AThe materialized collector writes a single `review.json` and no `.dr` chunks, so\x0A`inspect` over a materialized dataset reports a missing `manifest.dr`. On a\x0Amachine with no native backend installed it reports that instead. Open the\x0Adataset with `ct review`, or read `review.json` directly \xE2\x80\x94 it is plain JSON.\x0A[Collecting a review](/deep_review/collecting) shows both transcripts.\x0A\x0A## Values are one strip per line, not the debugger\'s parallel value columns\x0A\x0AA review renders every value a step recorded as chips at the end of the line.\x0AValues also arrive from the collectors as pre-rendered strings, so a struct or a\x0Avector shows its rendered text and does not expand.\x0A\x0A## The loop control is a stepper, not a dragged slider\x0A\x0AIt has previous/next buttons and a counter. The debugger\'s draggable loop slider\x0Ais sized from measurements a review has no live flow component to take.\x0A\x0A## Draggable context-boundary edge lines are not implemented\x0A\x0AThe `Expand N lines` buttons are the supported control.\x0A\x0A## Coverage is what the recording observed\x0A\x0AThe materialized collector reports the lines a run actually executed, so the\x0Abadge reads `N/N` on a fully-covered file; a line no recording touched is absent\x0Afrom the coverage list rather than listed as uncovered. `unreachable` is always\x0A`false`: a trace can say a line was not observed, never that it cannot be\x0Areached.\x0A\x0A## Symbols carry no declared type or visibility\x0A\x0AA materialized trace records that a function ran, not how it was declared, so\x0A`typeDesc` and `visibility` are emitted empty rather than guessed.\x0A\x0A## Flow is collected for functions the diff *changed*, not for everything they call\x0A\x0AA changed line `let x = helper(y);` gets flow for its caller; `helper` gets a\x0Acall count and coverage, but no flow overlay of its own. Following callees would\x0Apull an arbitrary depth of untouched code into the review.\x0A\x0A## A review carries no test results\x0A\x0A`DeepReviewData` has no test name, status or duration field, which is why the\x0AAgent Activity panel\'s Tests tile reads *not available for this dataset*.\x0A`ct test` ships (`ct test discover` works from `ct`; `ct test run` needs the\x0Astandalone runner binary, which `ct` tells you when it cannot run it) but\x0Aissues **no test certificates** \xE2\x80\x94 and nothing in the review surface, or in\x0Athe text `ct agent prompt` prints, claims otherwise.\x0A\x0A## Sharing a review is still handing over a directory\x0A\x0A`ct review` has exactly three forms \xE2\x80\x94 the launch form, `collect` and `inspect`.\x0ANone of them packages a dataset into a single file, uploads one, or produces a\x0Alink; handing a review to somebody means handing over the directory `collect`\x0Awrote. Note what that hands over: a dataset carries the source text of every\x0Achanged file and the recorded variable values, so whoever receives it gets all\x0Aof it.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A- [Introduction](/deep_review) \xE2\x80\x94 what DeepReview is for.\x0A- [Reading a review](/deep_review/reading) \xE2\x80\x94 the surfaces these limits apply to.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-1334578138), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,118,97,114,105,97,98,108,101,45,114,101,110,97,109,101,45,108,105,115,116,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Variable Rename List\x0Aorder: 108\x0A---\x0A# Variable Rename List\x0A\x0AMinified JavaScript bundles strip identifier names \xE2\x80\x94 `function add(left, right)` becomes `function a(b,c)`, and your debugger watch pane shows `a = [1,2,3,4,5]` instead of `array = [1,2,3,4,5]`.\x0A\x0AWhen the bundle ships a [source map V3](https://sourcemaps.info/spec.html) with a populated `names` field, CodeTracer\'s [server-side sourcemap translation](#sourcemap-translation-pipeline) recovers the original names for free.\x0A\x0AWhen the bundle does **not** ship a sourcemap, or its `names` table is missing the binding you care about, you can supply a **rename list** \xE2\x80\x94 a small TOML file that maps minified names back to readable ones.  The replay-server loads the list at trace open and applies it whenever it surfaces a variable name to the UI.\x0A\x0AThis document covers:\x0A\x0A* [TOML schema](#toml-schema)\x0A* [Where the file lives](#where-the-file-lives)\x0A* [Composition rules](#composition-rules)\x0A* [Environment variables](#environment-variables)\x0A* [Caveats](#caveats)\x0A\x0A## TOML schema\x0A\x0A```toml\x0A# Optional metadata.  Reserved for future schema bumps; the current\x0A# parser accepts and surfaces these fields without enforcing them.\x0A[meta]\x0Aversion = \"1\"\x0Acomment = \"lodash 4.17.21 minified bundle\"\x0A\x0A# Per-file [[rename]] entries.  Each maps a minified variable name to\x0A# a human-readable one.  `scope` defaults to \"global\" \xE2\x80\x94 set to\x0A# \"function:<funcname>\" or \"block:L<line>\" to constrain.\x0A\x0A[[rename]]\x0Afile = \"lodash.min.js\"     # required \xE2\x80\x94 recorded path (relative or absolute)\x0Ascope = \"global\"           # optional \xE2\x80\x94 defaults to \"global\"\x0Afrom = \"e\"                 # required \xE2\x80\x94 minified binding name\x0Ato = \"array\"               # required \xE2\x80\x94 readable name shown by the UI\x0A\x0A[[rename]]\x0Afile = \"lodash.min.js\"\x0Ascope = \"function:chunk\"\x0Afrom = \"t\"\x0Ato = \"result\"\x0A\x0A[[rename]]\x0Afile = \"lodash.min.js\"\x0Ascope = \"block:L42\"\x0Afrom = \"f\"\x0Ato = \"iteration_index\"\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Required fields\x0A\x0A| Field    | Type            | Description                                                              |\x0A|----------|-----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------|\x0A| `file`   | string          | Path the recording observed.  Either the basename (`lodash.min.js`) or an absolute path; the loader matches both. |\x0A| `from`   | string          | The minified binding name as it appears in the recorded trace.           |\x0A| `to`     | string          | The readable name the UI should show in its place.                       |\x0A\x0A### Optional fields\x0A\x0A| Field    | Type            | Default      | Description                                                                                                 |\x0A|----------|-----------------|--------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\x0A| `scope`  | string          | `\"global\"`   | Lookup scope.  Recognised values: `\"global\"`, `\"function:<funcname>\"`, `\"block:L<line>\"`, or `\"block:<n>\"`.  |\x0A\x0A### Parser tolerance\x0A\x0A* Unknown top-level tables (other than `[meta]`) and unknown keys inside `[[rename]]` are **logged at warn level and skipped** \xE2\x80\x94 a typo on one key (e.g. `flie = \"x\"` next to a correct `file = \"lodash.min.js\"`) does not drop the rename.\x0A* Missing required fields (`file`, `from`, `to`) produce a typed `MissingField` error and refuse to load the list.\x0A* Duplicate `(file, scope, from)` triples produce a typed `DuplicateEntry` error.\x0A\x0A## Where the file lives\x0A\x0AThe replay-server looks for the rename list in this order:\x0A\x0A1. The path passed via the CLI flag `--rename-list <path>` on `replay-server dap-server`.\x0A2. The path passed via the DAP `launch` argument `renameList`.\x0A3. The conventional sibling location `<recording-dir>/renames.toml`.\x0A\x0AWhen none of those resolve to an existing file, no rename list is installed and only the sourcemap `names[]` data is consulted (see [composition rules](#composition-rules) below).\x0A\x0A### CLI flag\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Areplay-server dap-server --rename-list ~/recordings/my-trace/renames.toml --stdio\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe CLI flag applies to **every** trace the server opens during its lifetime.  Per-launch DAP `renameList` arguments override the CLI default on a per-trace basis.\x0A\x0A### Sibling lookup\x0A\x0A```text\x0Amy-recording/\x0A\xE2\x94\x9C\xE2\x94\x80\xE2\x94\x80 trace.ct\x0A\xE2\x94\x9C\xE2\x94\x80\xE2\x94\x80 meta_dat/\x0A\xE2\x94\x9C\xE2\x94\x80\xE2\x94\x80 renames.toml       <-- picked up automatically at trace open\x0A\xE2\x94\x94\xE2\x94\x80\xE2\x94\x80 ...\x0A```\x0A\x0ANo flag needed \xE2\x80\x94 the loader probes for `renames.toml` next to the trace folder at trace open.\x0A\x0A## Composition rules\x0A\x0AWhen the UI requests the name for a recorded binding, the resolver consults three sources in order:\x0A\x0A1. **User rename list** (this file).  Lookup is scope-aware:\x0A   * A `Scope::Function(name)` hint matches a `function:<name>`-scoped entry first, then falls back to a `global` entry.\x0A   * A `Scope::Block(line)` hint matches a `block:L<line>`-scoped entry first, then falls back to a `global` entry.\x0A   * A `None` / unknown scope hint matches `global` entries only \xE2\x80\x94 function- and block-scoped entries are intentionally narrower.\x0A2. **Sourcemap V3 `names[]` array** (from [\xC2\xA7P3](#sourcemap-translation-pipeline) translation).  When the sourcemap declares an entry for the minified name, the resolver echoes the recorded name back as confirmation that the bundle preserved a known original.\x0A3. **`None`** \xE2\x80\x94 the recorded name flows through unchanged.\x0A\x0A**Precedence on conflict**: the user rename list always wins.  If you map `e -> userId` in `renames.toml` and the bundle\'s sourcemap names `e` as `e` (or anything else), the UI shows `userId`.\x0A\x0A## Environment variables\x0A\x0A| Variable                  | Default | Effect when set to one of `0`/`off`/`false`/`no`                                                 |\x0A|---------------------------|---------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\x0A| `CT_RENAME_LIST`          | on      | Disables the rename-list loader entirely \xE2\x80\x94 even an explicit `--rename-list` path is ignored.     |\x0A| `CT_SOURCEMAP_TRANSLATION`| on      | Disables [\xC2\xA7P3](#sourcemap-translation-pipeline) sourcemap translation (separate kill switch).    |\x0A| `CT_AUTOFORMAT`           | on      | Disables the [\xC2\xA7P4](#sourcemap-translation-pipeline) auto-format fallback for sourcemap-less minified sources, **and** the [\xC2\xA7P6.2](#sourcemap-translation-pipeline) recorder-side pre-format hook. |\x0A| `CT_AUTOFORMAT_THRESHOLD` | 500     | Override the average-line-length heuristic used by both the recorder pre-format and the replay-server fallback to decide whether a source looks minified.  Integer \xE2\x89\xA5 1. |\x0A\x0AThe four kill switches are independent.  Turning off `CT_RENAME_LIST` does not affect sourcemap translation or auto-format.\x0A\x0A## Auto-format pipeline (where formatting happens)\x0A\x0AThere are **two** places minified-source formatting can run, depending on which recorder produced the trace:\x0A\x0A* **Recorder-side (P6.2, recommended).**  Recorders from version 0.2.0 onward (e.g. `codetracer-js-recorder`) detect minified sources at record start, shell out to `prettier` (JavaScript) / `black` (Python) once, and bake the formatted view + a Source Map V3 sourcemap (formatted \xE2\x86\x92 original) into the trace under `<trace>/files/<file>.fmt.js{,.map}`.  The replay-server\'s existing [\xC2\xA7P3](#sourcemap-translation-pipeline) sourcemap path discovers the `.map` sibling and translates positions with no replay-time subprocess.\x0A* **Replay-server-side (P4, fallback).**  For traces produced by older recorders, the replay-server lazily formats minified sources at trace open, caching the result under the trace\'s cache directory.  The behaviour is identical from the UI\'s perspective; the only difference is *when* the formatter runs (record time vs. view time) and how it\'s cached.\x0A\x0AThe same `CT_AUTOFORMAT` env var disables both \xE2\x80\x94 useful when you want to inspect the raw minified source.  Disabling autoformat on a trace that already carries a `.fmt.js` sibling is a no-op (the formatted view is already on disk; the env var only gates the lookup pipeline).\x0A\x0AThe JS recorder exposes a CLI flag `--no-autoformat` that disables the recorder-side hook for a single invocation, falling back to the P4 lazy path at view time.\x0A\x0A## Caveats\x0A\x0A* **Render-time only.**  The rename list is applied when the value-stream renderer surfaces a binding to the UI.  The recorded trace itself is **not** rewritten \xE2\x80\x94 every recorded variable name stays in its original (minified) form on disk.  Re-opening the same trace with a different rename list yields different rendered names.\x0A* **No schema/format change.**  Adding a rename list does not bump the trace format or require the recorder to be aware of it.  Any trace produced by any recorder version works with any rename list.\x0A* **Origin tracking unchanged.**  Variable-origin chains (the \xC2\xA73 of the spec) continue to look up by the recorded variable id, not by the rendered name.  Renaming a binding does not break the origin index.\x0A* **Scope hints are best-effort.**  The resolver computes a scope hint from the surrounding call\'s function name and the current step\'s line.  When neither is available (e.g. the step has no associated path), only the global-scope user entries and the sourcemap `names[]` table are consulted.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A* [Source Map V3 spec](https://sourcemaps.info/spec.html) \xE2\x80\x94 the format the \xC2\xA7P3 translation consumes.\x0A* [Value-Origin Tracking](./value-origin-tracking.md) \xE2\x80\x94 the parallel pipeline that recovers value provenance independently of binding names.\x0A\x0A## Sourcemap translation pipeline\x0A\x0A*\xC2\xA7P3 (sourcemap translation), \xC2\xA7P4 (replay-side auto-format fallback), \xC2\xA7P5 (this document), and \xC2\xA7P6.2 (recorder-side auto-format hook) are milestones of the **Column-Aware Tracing & Source Deminification** campaign; the milestones spec lives at `codetracer-specs/Planned-Features/Column-Aware-Tracing-And-Deminification.milestones.org`.*\x0A")}, {Field0: 409672232, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,115,111,108,97,110,97,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Solana\x0Aorder: 115\x0A---\x0A## Solana\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Solana programs (smart contracts) written in Rust. The recorder captures per-instruction register state from the SBF (Solana Bytecode Format) virtual machine and maps it back to Rust source code via DWARF debug info.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Solana CLI** and **Anchor** framework for building programs\x0A- **codetracer-solana-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Build your Solana program with debug info:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   cd my-solana-program\x0A   anchor build\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record from a pre-generated register trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-solana-recorder record target/deploy/my_program.so \\\x0A       --regs trace.regs \\\x0A       --elf target/deploy/my_program.so \\\x0A       -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A3. Or with an Anchor IDL for account data decoding:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-solana-recorder record target/deploy/my_program.so \\\x0A       --regs trace.regs \\\x0A       --idl target/idl/my_program.json \\\x0A       -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Transaction\x0A\x0ATo debug a transaction from mainnet, devnet, or a local validator:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-solana-recorder replay \\\x0A    --signature 5abc123...xyz \\\x0A    --rpc-url https://api.mainnet-beta.solana.com \\\x0A    --program-dir target/deploy/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor local development with `solana-test-validator`:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-solana-recorder replay \\\x0A    --signature 5abc123...xyz \\\x0A    --rpc-url http://localhost:8899 \\\x0A    --program-dir target/deploy/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                       | Description                                                     |\x0A| -------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <ELF>`             | Record from a compiled `.so` ELF file                           |\x0A| `--regs <FILE>`            | Pre-generated register trace file (`.regs` binary)              |\x0A| `--elf <PATH>`             | Unstripped ELF for DWARF source mapping                         |\x0A| `--idl <FILE>`             | Anchor IDL JSON for account data decoding                       |\x0A| `replay --signature <SIG>` | Replay a transaction by its base-58 signature                   |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`          | Solana JSON-RPC endpoint (default: `http://localhost:8899`)     |\x0A| `--program-dir <PATH>`     | Directory with compiled `.so` files (default: `target/deploy/`) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`      | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                      |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`       | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)           |\x0A")}, {Field0: 957457065, Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,99,116,95,99,108,105,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: ct CLI Reference\x0Aorder: 101\x0A---\x0A## ct CLI Reference\x0A\x0AThe `ct` command is the main CodeTracer CLI. It records program executions, replays traces, and manages the CodeTracer environment.\x0A\x0A### Synopsis\x0A\x0A```\x0Act <command> [options] [<program>] [<args>]\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Commands\x0A\x0A#### Recording and Replay\x0A\x0A| Command                                       | Description                                                          |\x0A| --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `ct run <program> [args]`                     | Record and immediately open in the GUI                               |\x0A| `ct record <program> [args]`                  | Record a trace to disk                                               |\x0A| `ct replay`                                   | Open a trace in the GUI                                              |\x0A| `ct import <trace-folder>`                    | Import a trace from a folder                                         |\x0A| `ct trace extract-gfx -o <dir> <trace>`       | Extract the graphics stream from a `.ct` container                   |\x0A| `ct trace export --portable -o <out> <trace>` | Export a portable trace (embeds binaries and debug symbols)          |\x0A| `ct gfx-replay --gfx-stream <dir>`            | Replay an extracted graphics stream (used by the visual replay GUI)  |\x0A\x0A#### DeepReview\x0A\x0A| Command                      | Description                                                          |\x0A| ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `ct review <PATH>`           | Open a review over an exported review dataset                        |\x0A| `ct review collect [options]`| Produce a review dataset from recordings + a diff                    |\x0A| `ct review inspect <PATH>`   | Summarise a dataset without opening a GUI                            |\x0A| `ct agent evidence <PATH>`   | Hand a review dataset to CodeTracer from inside an agent session     |\x0A| `ct agent end-of-turn`       | Run `ct review collect` then `ct agent evidence`, for a project hook |\x0A| `ct agent prompt`            | Print the prompt text that teaches an agent the pair                 |\x0A\x0ASee the [DeepReview](/deep_review) section for the workflow.\x0A\x0A#### Stylus / EVM\x0A\x0A| Command           | Description                                       |\x0A| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `ct arb deploy`   | Deploy a Stylus contract to a local devnode       |\x0A| `ct arb explorer` | Open the transaction explorer for recorded traces |\x0A| `ct arb record`   | Record a Stylus contract execution                |\x0A| `ct arb replay`   | Replay a Stylus trace                             |\x0A\x0A#### CI Integration\x0A\x0A| Command        | Description                           |\x0A| -------------- | ------------------------------------- |\x0A| `ct ci start`  | Start a CI recording session          |\x0A| `ct ci attach` | Attach to a running CI session        |\x0A| `ct ci exec`   | Execute a command within a CI session |\x0A| `ct ci finish` | Finalize a CI session                 |\x0A| `ct ci run`    | Run a command with CI recording       |\x0A| `ct ci log`    | View CI session logs                  |\x0A| `ct ci status` | Check CI session status               |\x0A| `ct ci cancel` | Cancel a CI session                   |\x0A\x0A#### Utility\x0A\x0A| Command               | Description                                                                       |\x0A| --------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `ct install`          | Install the CLI tools                                                             |\x0A| `ct version`          | Print the CodeTracer version                                                      |\x0A| `ct help`             | Display help information                                                          |\x0A| `ct list`             | List recorded traces                                                              |\x0A| `ct console`          | Open the interactive console                                                      |\x0A| `ct doctor <lang>`    | Probe recorder-readiness for a language (currently `python`; more languages soon) |\x0A\x0A#### Online Sharing\x0A\x0A| Command       | Description                           |\x0A| ------------- | ------------------------------------- |\x0A| `ct upload`   | Upload a trace                        |\x0A| `ct download` | Download a trace                      |\x0A| `ct login`    | Authenticate with the sharing service |\x0A\x0A### ct record\x0A\x0ARecords the execution of a program into a trace.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act record [options] <program> [-- <program-args>]\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Options:**\x0A\x0A| Flag                          | Description                                                  |\x0A| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `--lang <LANG>`               | Override language detection (e.g., `noir`, `python`, `ruby`) |\x0A| `-o, --output-folder <DIR>`   | Output directory for trace files                             |\x0A| `--backend <BACKEND>`         | Recording backend (e.g., `plonky2` for Noir)                 |\x0A| `-e, --export <ZIP>`          | Export trace as a zip archive                                |\x0A| `-c, --cleanup-output-folder` | Remove the output folder after export                        |\x0A| `--trace-kind <KIND>`         | Trace kind: `db` (default), `rr`, or `ttd`                   |\x0A| `--rr-support-path <PATH>`    | (internal) Override path for the RR-backend DAP binary       |\x0A| `--python-interpreter <PATH>` | Path to the Python interpreter to use                        |\x0A| `--pytest [ARGS]`             | Run pytest with the given arguments                          |\x0A| `--unittest [ARGS]`           | Run unittest with the given arguments                        |\x0A| `-t, --stylus-trace <PATH>`   | Path to a Stylus trace file                                  |\x0A| `-a, --address <ADDR>`        | Contract address (Stylus)                                    |\x0A| `--socket <PATH>`             | Unix socket path for event reporting                         |\x0A| `--use-interpose`             | Record graphics API calls for visual replay (MCR backend only) |\x0A\x0A**Language detection:** When `--lang` is not provided, `ct` detects the language from the file extension or project structure:\x0A\x0A| Extension / Marker              | Language                  |\x0A| ------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\x0A| `.py`                           | Python                    |\x0A| `.rb`                           | Ruby                      |\x0A| `.nr` or `Nargo.toml`           | Noir                      |\x0A| `.wasm`                         | WASM                      |\x0A| `.small`                        | Small                     |\x0A| `Cargo.toml` with wasm32 target | Rust WASM                 |\x0A| `Cargo.toml`                    | Rust (native, via rr/TTD) |\x0A\x0A:::note\x0ABlockchain-specific languages (Circom, Cairo, Aiken, Cadence, Move, Sway, Miden, PolkaVM, Leo, Tolk) use their own recorder binaries. See the [Getting Started](/getting_started) guides for each language.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AVisual recordings for native graphics programs are MCR `.ct` traces produced with `ct record --use-interpose`. CodeTracer opens these traces in the GUI and starts the visual replay player automatically. See [Visual recordings](../usage_guide/visual_recordings.md).\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### ct replay\x0A\x0AOpens a previously recorded trace in the CodeTracer GUI.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act replay [options] [<program-name>]\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Options:**\x0A\x0A| Flag                   | Description                                 |\x0A| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<program-name>`       | Open the most recent trace for this program |\x0A| `--id=<TRACE_ID>`      | Open a trace by its numeric ID              |\x0A| `--trace-folder=<DIR>` | Open a trace from a specific directory      |\x0A\x0AWhen called without arguments, `ct replay` opens an interactive dialog to choose from recent traces.\x0A\x0A### ct run\x0A\x0ARecords a program and immediately opens the trace in the GUI. Equivalent to `ct record` followed by `ct replay`.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act run [options] <program> [-- <program-args>]\x0A```\x0A\x0AAccepts the same options as `ct record`.\x0A\x0A### ct review\x0A\x0AOpens a review over an exported review dataset \xE2\x80\x94 the diff, its recorded\x0Aexecutions, coverage and flow. See\x0A[Reading a review](/deep_review/reading).\x0A\x0A```\x0Act review <PATH>\x0A```\x0A\x0A| Argument | Description                                                                            |\x0A| -------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<PATH>` | A `review.json` file, or the directory `ct review collect` wrote it into. Required.     |\x0A\x0A`ct review` is an ordinary `ct` command, so `ct`\'s global options (`--cwd`,\x0A`--inspect`, `--remote-debugging-port`, the `--env-file` family) work with it.\x0AA path that does not exist is diagnosed by `ct` rather than by the renderer.\x0A\x0A`ct review` on its own \xE2\x80\x94 and `ct review --help` \xE2\x80\x94 prints the whole group\'s\x0Ausage, not just the launch form.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0A`ct --deepreview <PATH>` and the `ct-native-replay deepreview\x0A{collect,export,inspect}` subcommands are retired. They fail with a message\x0Anaming the `ct review` replacement rather than being kept as aliases.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### ct review collect\x0A\x0AProduces a review dataset from a set of recordings and a diff. The collector is\x0Achosen by **inspecting the recordings** \xE2\x80\x94 native/rr recordings go to the native\x0Areplay backend, materialized traces (Python, Ruby, JavaScript, Noir, \xE2\x80\xA6) go to\x0Athe db-backend collector. There is no `--backend` flag.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act review collect (--repo <DIR> --diff <BASE..HEAD> | --diff-file <PATH>) \\\x0A                  --recordings <DIR> --output <DIR> \\\x0A                  [--preset <NAME>] [--progress]\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Options:**\x0A\x0A| Flag                   | Required                       | Default   | Description                                                                      |\x0A| ---------------------- | ------------------------------ | --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--repo <DIR>`         | Yes (unless `--diff-file`)     | \xE2\x80\x94         | Git repository the diff is read from                                             |\x0A| `--diff <BASE..HEAD>`  | Yes (unless `--diff-file`)     | \xE2\x80\x94         | Diff specification, e.g. `main..HEAD`. Must contain `..`                         |\x0A| `--diff-file <PATH>`   | Yes (unless `--repo`+`--diff`) | \xE2\x80\x94         | A unified diff file instead of `--repo`/`--diff`. Cannot be combined with either |\x0A| `--recordings <DIR>`   | Yes                            | \xE2\x80\x94         | Directory holding the recordings, one subdirectory per run                       |\x0A| `--output <DIR>`, `-o` | Yes                            | \xE2\x80\x94         | Directory the dataset is written to                                              |\x0A| `--preset <NAME>`      | No                             | `default` | Collection preset: `default`, `minimal` or `comprehensive`                       |\x0A| `--progress`           | No                             | off       | Emit JSON Lines progress events on stderr                                        |\x0A\x0ABoth `--output DIR` and `--output=DIR` are accepted. `collect` takes no\x0Apositional arguments.\x0A\x0A`collect` writes `review.json` into `--output` beside the collector\'s own\x0Achunks, so `ct review <the same DIR>` opens exactly what it produced. If the\x0Aexport half fails, the command says so and exits non-zero rather than reporting\x0Aa partial success.\x0A\x0A**Presets** differ in sampling intensity only: `minimal` keeps one sample per\x0Aline and one execution per function (fastest, smallest); `default` keeps 3 and\x0A10; `comprehensive` keeps 10 and 50 with a deeper value limit.\x0A\x0A**Progress events** (`--progress`) are one JSON object per line on stderr:\x0A\x0A```\x0A{\"fileCount\":1,\"recordingCount\":2,\"type\":\"collection_started\"}\x0A{\"percentage\":0.0,\"recordingIndex\":0,\"recordingTotal\":2,\"type\":\"recording_progress\"}\x0A{\"percentage\":50.0,\"recordingIndex\":1,\"recordingTotal\":2,\"type\":\"recording_progress\"}\x0A{\"coverageLines\":8,\"flowSteps\":42,\"path\":\"src/main.nr\",\"symbols\":1,\"type\":\"file_collected\"}\x0A{\"elapsedMs\":68,\"fileCount\":1,\"totalFlowSteps\":42,\"type\":\"collection_completed\"}\x0A```\x0A\x0AOne `recording_progress` is emitted per recording, before that recording is\x0Acollected, so the stream is as long as `--recordings` is deep.\x0A\x0A**Diagnostics.** A recordings directory that mixes native and materialized\x0Arecordings is refused with a message naming both kinds. An empty directory, a\x0Adirectory holding no recordings, and a `--recordings` path that does not exist\x0Aeach get their own message and a non-zero status \xE2\x80\x94 never a silently empty\x0Adataset.\x0A\x0A### ct review inspect\x0A\x0AReads a dataset and prints summary statistics without opening a GUI.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act review inspect <PATH> [--format text|json]\x0A```\x0A\x0A| Flag                | Default | Description                            |\x0A| ------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<PATH>`            | \xE2\x80\x94       | The dataset directory. Required.       |\x0A| `--format <FORMAT>` | `text`  | `text` (human-readable) or `json`      |\x0A\x0A:::note\x0A`inspect` reads the **native** collector\'s dataset layout (the directory\x0Acontaining `manifest.dr`) and delegates to the native replay backend. A dataset\x0Aproduced from a materialized trace is a single `review.json` with no chunks, so\x0A`inspect` reports the missing `manifest.dr`; open it with `ct review`, or read\x0Athe JSON directly.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### ct agent evidence\x0A\x0AHands a review dataset to CodeTracer from inside an agent session.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act agent evidence <PATH> [--session <ID>] [--task <ID>] [--workspace <DIR>]\x0A```\x0A\x0A| Flag                | Description                                                                         |\x0A| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<PATH>`            | The dataset: a directory from `ct review collect`, or the `review.json` in it. Required. |\x0A| `--session <ID>`    | The agent session this evidence belongs to                                          |\x0A| `--task <ID>`       | The task the session is working on                                                  |\x0A| `--workspace <DIR>` | The workspace the session is working in                                             |\x0A\x0AAll three are read from `CODETRACER_AGENT_SESSION_ID`,\x0A`CODETRACER_AGENT_TASK_ID` and `CODETRACER_AGENT_WORKSPACE` \xE2\x80\x94 the variables the\x0Aagent harness already exports \xE2\x80\x94 and the flags override them. The session falls\x0Aback to the reference `ct review collect` stamped into the dataset. If none of\x0Athe three names a session, the command exits non-zero and says which to supply;\x0Ait never invents an id.\x0A\x0AThe changeset, the recordings and the status the notification carries are read\x0Afrom the dataset, not restated by the agent.\x0A\x0A### ct agent end-of-turn\x0A\x0ARuns the two ordinary commands, in order, printing each as it runs it \xE2\x80\x94 for use\x0Aas a project\'s end-of-turn hook.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act agent end-of-turn [collect options] [--session <ID>] [--task <ID>] [--workspace <DIR>]\x0A```\x0A\x0AIt accepts all of `ct review collect`\'s options (`--repo`, `--diff`,\x0A`--diff-file`, `--recordings`, `--output`/`-o`, `--preset`, `--progress`) plus\x0Athe three above, and fills unset ones from the environment so a hook line can be\x0Abare:\x0A\x0A| Variable                       | Fills in       | Default        |\x0A| ------------------------------ | -------------- | -------------- |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_REPO`       | `--repo`       | the workspace  |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_DIFF`       | `--diff`       | \xE2\x80\x94              |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_DIFF_FILE`  | `--diff-file`  | \xE2\x80\x94              |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_RECORDINGS` | `--recordings` | `.ct/runs`     |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_OUTPUT`     | `--output`     | `.ct/review`   |\x0A\x0A### ct agent prompt\x0A\x0APrints the prompt text that teaches an agent the collect-then-hand-over pair.\x0AInstall it into a project\'s agent instructions with:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act agent prompt >> AGENTS.md\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe text describes only commands that ship. In particular it does not mention\x0Atest certificates, which `ct test` does not yet issue.\x0A\x0A### ct trace origin\x0A\x0APrints the backward dataflow chain (an \"origin chain\") for a variable.\x0AWalks assignments / parameter passes / return captures / field-or-index\x0Aaccesses backward from the queried `(variable, step, frame)` until a\x0Aterminator (computational expression, literal, parameter at the\x0Arecording boundary, etc.) is reached. Drives the same `ct/originChain`\x0ADAP request as the `Trace.value_origin(...)` Python binding and the\x0A`get_value_origin` MCP tool.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act trace origin <trace-path> --variable <NAME> [options]\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Options:**\x0A\x0A| Flag                              | Description                                                                                       |\x0A| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<trace-path>`                    | Path to the trace directory (required positional argument).                                       |\x0A| `--variable <NAME>`               | Variable identifier to query. V1 is identifier-only; dotted paths are reserved. **Required.**     |\x0A| `--step <N>`                      | Step id at which to query. Defaults to the trace\'s current execution point.                       |\x0A| `--frame <N>`                     | DAP frame id at which to query. Defaults to the topmost frame.                                    |\x0A| `--max-hops <N>`                  | Maximum hops in this batch. Default: `16`.                                                        |\x0A| `--format <json\\|markdown\\|text>` | Output renderer. Default: `text`.                                                                 |\x0A| `--lazy`                          | Allow the backend to return a `continuationToken` instead of walking the full chain in one shot.  |\x0A\x0A**Formats:**\x0A\x0A- `text` \xE2\x80\x94 ASCII layout matching spec \xC2\xA73.2 (newest hop first, terminator\x0A  at the bottom, frame-transition glyphs inline). Good for terminal\x0A  copy-paste.\x0A- `markdown` \xE2\x80\x94 fenced chain with classification badges and a per-hop\x0A  table. Paste straight into a bug report.\x0A- `json` \xE2\x80\x94 canonical `OriginChain` wire schema, pretty-printed. Use for\x0A  scripting or downstream tooling.\x0A\x0A**Example:**\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act trace origin /traces/my-bug.ct --variable total --step 137 --format text\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor multi-step agent workflows that need to compose origin lookups with\x0Alocals/history/breakpoints, prefer `ct trace exec --script <file.py>\x0A<trace-path>` and call `trace.value_origin(...)` inside the script \xE2\x80\x94 the\x0Atrace stays loaded across calls and the classifier\'s pattern cache is\x0Areused.\x0A\x0ASee [Value Origin Tracking](../usage_guide/value-origin-tracking.md) for\x0Athe user-facing walkthrough.\x0A\x0A### ct trace extract-gfx\x0A\x0AExtracts the graphics stream from a `.ct` trace container into a directory the visual replay player can consume.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act trace extract-gfx -o <output-dir> <trace>\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Options:**\x0A\x0A| Flag                       | Description                                                            |\x0A| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<trace>`                  | Path to the `.ct` trace container to extract (required positional).    |\x0A| `-o, --output-dir <DIR>`   | Directory to extract the graphics stream into. Required.               |\x0A\x0ASee [Visual recordings](../usage_guide/visual_recordings.md) for the full workflow.\x0A\x0A### ct trace export\x0A\x0AExports a recorded trace to a single file, optionally producing a portable bundle with embedded binaries and debug symbols.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act trace export [--portable] -o <output> <trace>\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Options:**\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                                        |\x0A| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `<trace>`             | Path to the source trace to export (required positional).          |\x0A| `--portable`          | Produce a portable export with embedded binaries and debug symbols.|\x0A| `-o, --output <PATH>` | Output path for the exported trace (required).                     |\x0A\x0A### ct gfx-replay\x0A\x0AReplays an extracted graphics stream. The CodeTracer GUI launches this automatically when opening a visual `.ct` trace; running it directly is useful for diagnostics.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act gfx-replay --gfx-stream <dir> [--http --port <N>] [--backend <BACKEND>]\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Options:**\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                                                |\x0A| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `--gfx-stream <DIR>`  | Path to the extracted graphics-stream directory (required).                |\x0A| `--http`              | Start the player as an HTTP server (used by the GUI).                      |\x0A| `--port <N>`          | Port for the HTTP player (only meaningful with `--http`).                  |\x0A| `--backend <BACKEND>` | Rendering backend selector \xE2\x80\x94 e.g. `software` or `hardware`.                |\x0A\x0A### ct doctor\x0A\x0AProbes recorder-readiness for a language: reports the interpreter or runtime that would be used and whether the matching recorder package is installed and importable.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act doctor <language>\x0A```\x0A\x0A**Arguments:**\x0A\x0A| Argument     | Description                                                                                  |\x0A| ------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<language>` | Recorder to probe. Currently `python` is wired; more recorders will land in future releases. |\x0A\x0ARun this when `ct record` fails with a recorder import error to confirm which interpreter `ct` is targeting and whether the recorder package is present.\x0A\x0A### ct trace exec\x0A\x0ARuns a Python replay-script against a recording. The script body is the\x0Asame one consumed by the MCP `exec_script` tool, so the same logic runs\x0Afrom a terminal session and from an agent workflow.\x0A\x0A```\x0Act trace exec --script <file.py> <trace-path>\x0A```\x0A\x0AInside the script a `trace` object is pre-bound to the loaded recording.\x0AMethods include `trace.locals()`, `trace.history()`, navigation\x0A(`trace.step_over()` etc.), and `trace.value_origin(...)` for origin\x0Achains. See the\x0A[Python API reference](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer/tree/main/python-api)\x0Afor the full surface.\x0A\x0A### Environment Variables\x0A\x0A| Variable                        | Description                                                             |\x0A| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `CODETRACER_PYTHON_INTERPRETER` | Path to the Python interpreter for recording                            |\x0A| `CODETRACER_NOIR_EXE_PATH`      | Path to the Noir tracer binary                                          |\x0A| `CODETRACER_WASM_VM_PATH`       | Path to the WASM VM binary (wazero)                                     |\x0A| `CODETRACER_RECORDING`          | Set to `1` during recording                                             |\x0A| `CODETRACER_CALLTRACE_MODE`     | Call trace mode: `FullRecord`, `RawRecordNoValues`, `NoInstrumentation` |\x0A| `CODETRACER_SHELL_ID`           | Shell session ID (for CodeTracer Shell)                                 |\x0A| `CODETRACER_CT_MCR_CMD`         | Override the internal MCR binary that `ct trace extract-gfx` invokes     |\x0A| `CODETRACER_CT_GFX_PLAYER_CMD`  | Override the internal player binary that `ct gfx-replay` launches        |\x0A| `CODETRACER_CT_GFX_PLAYER_BACKEND` | Default backend for `ct gfx-replay` (equivalent to `--backend`), for example `software` |\x0A| `CODETRACER_NATIVE_REPLAY_PATH` | Path to the `ct-native-replay` binary used by native `ct review collect` / `ct review inspect` |\x0A| `CODETRACER_AGENT_SESSION_ID`   | Agent session `ct agent evidence` and `ct review collect` attribute a review to |\x0A| `CODETRACER_AGENT_TASK_ID`      | Task the agent session is working on                                    |\x0A| `CODETRACER_AGENT_WORKSPACE`    | Workspace the agent session is working in                               |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_REPO`        | `ct agent end-of-turn` default for `--repo`                             |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_DIFF`        | `ct agent end-of-turn` default for `--diff`                             |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_DIFF_FILE`   | `ct agent end-of-turn` default for `--diff-file`                        |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_RECORDINGS`  | `ct agent end-of-turn` default for `--recordings` (falls back to `.ct/runs`) |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_OUTPUT`      | `ct agent end-of-turn` default for `--output` (falls back to `.ct/review`) |\x0A\x0A### Output Format\x0A\x0AAll recordings produce a trace directory containing:\x0A\x0A| File                        | Description                                             |\x0A| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `trace.bin` or `trace.json` | The trace data (binary or JSON format)                  |\x0A| `trace_metadata.json`       | Metadata about the trace (language, program, timestamp) |\x0A| `trace_paths.json`          | Source file paths referenced in the trace               |\x0A| `symbols.json`              | Extracted symbols (for Noir and some languages)         |\x0A")}, {Field0: (-918115287), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,108,105,118,101,45,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,45,116,114,97,99,107,105,110,103,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Live Request Tracking\x0Aorder: 5\x0A---\x0A# Live request tracking\x0A\x0ACodeTracer can record a running web server and show you its HTTP requests\x0Aas they arrive. Each request becomes a row in the **Request Panel**;\x0Adouble-clicking a row jumps to the code that handled it, with the full\x0Atime-travelling debugger available from that point \xE2\x80\x94 step backwards into\x0Athe middleware that ran before your handler, or forwards into the query\x0Athat produced the response.\x0A\x0AThis works while the server is still running. You do not stop the server,\x0Aexport anything, or convert a log: the recording is readable as it is\x0Abeing written.\x0A\x0A## The two things you will do\x0A\x0A**Record a live server**, and watch requests arrive:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang <lang> -o ./trace -- ./my-server   # terminal 1\x0Act replay -t ./trace                                         # terminal 2\x0A```\x0A\x0A`--server` tells CodeTracer the program outlives the command. Stopping it\x0Awith `Ctrl-C` is a normal end, not a crash. Everything else about\x0A`ct record` is unchanged \xE2\x80\x94 `-o`, `--backend`, `--upload`, `--with-diff`\x0Aand `--export` all still apply.\x0A\x0ATwo parts of that command line are easy to leave out, and both are needed:\x0A\x0A- **`--`** separates CodeTracer\'s own options from your server\'s. Without\x0A  it, a flag meant for your program is read as one of CodeTracer\'s:\x0A  `php -S localhost:8000` fails outright, and a flag that collides with one\x0A  of CodeTracer\'s own is worse \xE2\x80\x94 it is quietly accepted and your server\x0A  never starts.\x0A- **`--lang`** names the language. CodeTracer normally infers it from the\x0A  file you are recording, but a server is usually started through a\x0A  launcher \xE2\x80\x94 `flask`, `rails`, `mix phx.server`, `php -S` \xE2\x80\x94 with no source\x0A  file on the command line to infer from. Native servers are the exception:\x0A  they need no `--lang`.\x0A\x0A**Open a finished recording**, any time later:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay ./my-server-recording\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe Request Panel is populated the same way; the only difference is that\x0Ano new rows arrive.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0A`ct` is the only command you need. The individual language recorders are\x0Aimplementation details \xE2\x80\x94 you do not install or invoke them separately, and\x0Anothing in this guide asks you to.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Pick your language\x0A\x0AYou almost certainly care about one of these, not all of them. Each page\x0Ais self-contained: install, start your server under CodeTracer, and see\x0Arequests.\x0A\x0A- [Python](/usage_guide/live-requests-python) \xE2\x80\x94 Flask, Django, FastAPI, any WSGI/ASGI app\x0A- [Ruby](/usage_guide/live-requests-ruby) \xE2\x80\x94 Rails, Sinatra, any Rack app\x0A- [PHP](/usage_guide/live-requests-php) \xE2\x80\x94 built-in server, php-fpm pools\x0A- [Elixir & Erlang](/usage_guide/live-requests-elixir) \xE2\x80\x94 Phoenix, Plug/Cowboy\x0A- [JavaScript](/usage_guide/live-requests-javascript) \xE2\x80\x94 Express and other Node servers\x0A- [C, C++, Rust and other native servers](/usage_guide/live-requests-native)\x0A\x0A## How your server gets recorded\x0A\x0AYou do not need this section to use the feature, but it explains why the\x0Aper-language pages differ, and why one of them has a caveat the others do\x0Anot.\x0A\x0AA request span needs two things: the boundaries of the request, and the\x0Aplace in the recording where it happened. CodeTracer gets these in one of\x0Athree ways, depending on what the language runtime offers.\x0A\x0A| Shape | Languages | Where spans come from |\x0A| --- | --- | --- |\x0A| **Middleware** | Python, Ruby, JavaScript, Elixir, Erlang | The server\'s own middleware layer (WSGI/ASGI, Rack, Express, Plug) publishes each request from inside the recorded process. |\x0A| **Per-worker** | PHP | The extension publishes from each worker process; CodeTracer is told the parent directory, because a pool writes one container per worker. |\x0A| **Supervisor** | C, C++, Rust, Go, \xE2\x80\xA6 | A native server has no middleware seam, so a separate supervisor binary discovers the requests. |\x0A\x0AAll three produce the same thing \xE2\x80\x94 one recording, with each request as an\x0Ainterval inside it. A request is not a separate recording, and the panel is\x0Anot reading a log file alongside the trace. This matters in practice: because\x0Arequests are intervals of one recording, you can step from one request into\x0Acode that ran before it, and shared state is genuinely shared rather than\x0Astitched together after the fact.\x0A\x0A## Languages without a server story\x0A\x0ASome languages have a recorder but nothing that usefully survives a\x0Along-running process. `ct record --server` says so plainly rather than\x0Arecording a plain run and letting you discover the difference in the panel.\x0AIf you see that message, the language is not yet wired for live request\x0Atracking \xE2\x80\x94 the recording you would get is still valid, it just has no\x0Arequest spans.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-415978709), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,115,119,97,121,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Sway (FuelVM)\x0Aorder: 116\x0A---\x0A## Sway (FuelVM)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Sway smart contracts on the Fuel blockchain. The recorder captures per-instruction execution state from FuelVM and maps it back to Sway source code.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Fuel toolchain** (`forc`, `fuel-core`) for building and running contracts\x0A- **codetracer-fuel-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Create or navigate to a Sway project (must contain `Forc.toml`):\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   forc new my_contract\x0A   cd my_contract\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-fuel-recorder record . -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   Or from a pre-compiled bytecode file:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-fuel-recorder record --bytecode out/debug/my_contract.bin -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A3. With an ABI file for variable enrichment:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-fuel-recorder record . --abi out/debug/my_contract-abi.json -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Transaction\x0A\x0ATo debug a transaction from a Fuel node:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-fuel-recorder replay \\\x0A    --tx-id 0xabc123... \\\x0A    --rpc-url http://localhost:4000/v1/graphql \\\x0A    --source-dir ./out/debug/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                   | Description                                                              |\x0A| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\x0A| `record [PROJECT_DIR]` | Record from a Sway project (must contain `Forc.toml`)                    |\x0A| `--bytecode <FILE>`    | Raw FuelVM bytecode file                                                 |\x0A| `--abi <FILE>`         | Sway ABI JSON for variable enrichment                                    |\x0A| `replay --tx-id <ID>`  | Replay an on-chain transaction (hex with `0x` prefix)                    |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`      | fuel-core GraphQL endpoint (default: `http://localhost:4000/v1/graphql`) |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>`  | Directory with forc build output                                         |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`  | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                               |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`   | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)                    |\x0A")}, {Field0: 1859905960, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,108,105,118,101,45,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,115,45,112,121,116,104,111,110,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Live Requests \xE2\x80\x94 Python\x0Aorder: 6\x0A---\x0A# Live request tracking in Python\x0A\x0AWorks with any WSGI or ASGI application \xE2\x80\x94 Flask, Django, FastAPI and\x0Aanything else that speaks those protocols. Request spans are published by\x0Athe recorder\'s middleware from inside your server process, so there is no\x0Aproxy to configure and no code change to your handlers.\x0A\x0A## Record your server\x0A\x0AStart the server under CodeTracer instead of directly:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang python -o ./trace -- flask --app app run --port 8000\x0A```\x0A\x0AAnything you would normally type after the program name still works \xE2\x80\x94\x0Aflags included. This is the same invocation for Django (`manage.py\x0Arunserver`), Uvicorn, Gunicorn or a plain `python app.py`.\x0A\x0AThe command stays in the foreground while the server runs. Stop it with\x0A`Ctrl-C`; that is a normal end, not a crash, and the recording is complete\x0Awhen the process exits.\x0A\x0A## Watch requests arrive\x0A\x0AIn a second terminal, with the server still running:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay -t ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe **Requests** panel docks itself as soon as the first request lands. Send\x0Asome traffic:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acurl http://localhost:8000/api/users/7\x0A```\x0A\x0AA row appears for each request, showing method, route, status and duration.\x0ADouble-click one to jump to the handler that served it \xE2\x80\x94 from there the whole\x0Atime-travelling debugger is available: step backwards through the middleware\x0Athat ran before your view, or forwards into the ORM call that built the\x0Aresponse.\x0A\x0A## Open a recording later\x0A\x0ANothing about the recording is temporary. To reopen it after the fact:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe panel is populated identically; only the live updates are absent.\x0A\x0A## What gets recorded\x0A\x0A- **Route templates, not just paths.** A request to `/api/users/7` is\x0A  labelled with the route it matched (`/api/users/<int:user_id>`), so all\x0A  requests to one endpoint group together regardless of their parameters.\x0A- **Status and duration** for every request, including ones that raised.\x0A- **Errors.** A request that ends in an unhandled exception is marked as\x0A  failed and carries the exception message.\x0A\x0A## Requirements\x0A\x0APython 3.10 or newer. Nothing else \xE2\x80\x94 `ct` locates the Python recorder\x0Aitself. If it cannot, it tells you exactly what is missing and how to\x0Ainstall it rather than producing an empty recording.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AIf you have used CodeTracer\'s Python support before: request spans live\x0A*inside* the recording now. There is no `session_manifest.jsonl` or other\x0Asidecar file to keep next to the trace, and nothing to pass to the GUI\x0Abesides the recording itself.\x0A:::\x0A")}, {Field0: 1018123053, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,116,111,108,107,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Tolk (TON)\x0Aorder: 118\x0A---\x0A## Tolk (TON)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Tolk (and FunC) smart contracts on the TON blockchain. The recorder instruments the TVM (TON Virtual Machine) to capture per-instruction execution state and maps TVM stack operations back to Tolk source code via debug sections.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Tolk compiler** for compiling contracts\x0A- **Node.js** and **@ton/sandbox** for local contract testing\x0A- **codetracer-ton-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Write a Tolk contract:\x0A\x0A   ```tolk\x0A   fun compute(): int {\x0A       var a: int = 10;\x0A       var b: int = 32;\x0A       var sumVal: int = a + b;\x0A       var doubled: int = sumVal * 2;\x0A       var finalResult: int = doubled + a;\x0A       return finalResult;\x0A   }\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-ton-recorder record contract.tolk -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Recording from @ton/sandbox\x0A\x0AIf you use `@ton/sandbox` for contract testing, capture the VM logs and convert them:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-ton-recorder trace-sandbox \\\x0A    --vm-log vm_logs_full.txt \\\x0A    --source contract.tolk \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Transaction\x0A\x0ATo debug a transaction from the TON blockchain:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-ton-recorder replay \\\x0A    --tx-hash abc123...def \\\x0A    --address EQBabc...xyz \\\x0A    --endpoint https://ton.org/global-config.json \\\x0A    --source-dir ./contracts/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                            | Description                                                         |\x0A| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <file>`                 | Record a Tolk source file (`.tolk`)                                 |\x0A| `trace-sandbox --vm-log <FILE>` | Convert `@ton/sandbox` VM log output                                |\x0A| `--source <FILE>`               | Path to Tolk source file (default: `contract.tolk`)                 |\x0A| `replay --tx-hash <HASH>`       | Replay a TON transaction (hex-encoded)                              |\x0A| `--address <ADDR>`              | Contract address that executed the transaction                      |\x0A| `--endpoint <URL>`              | Liteserver endpoint (default: `https://ton.org/global-config.json`) |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>`           | Contract source directory                                           |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`           | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                          |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`            | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)               |\x0A\x0A### Variable Reconstruction\x0A\x0AThe TVM has no named local variables \xE2\x80\x94 it uses a stack-based architecture. The recorder uses heuristic stack tracking combined with the Tolk compiler\'s debug sections to reconstruct variable names and values at each step.\x0A")}, {Field0: 38188330, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,114,101,99,111,114,100,105,110,103,45,97,45,98,114,111,119,115,101,114,45,97,112,112,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Recording a Browser App\x0Aorder: 199\x0A---\x0A# Recording a browser app\x0A\x0ACodeTracer\'s browser recorder (M26) captures the same execution-flow\x0Aevents the Node.js recorder captures \xE2\x80\x94 `Step`, `Call`, `Return`,\x0A`Assignment`, `Value`, correlation markers \xE2\x80\x94 and streams them to a\x0Alocal daemon over WebSocket.  The daemon converts the newline-delimited\x0AJSON stream to a CTFS `.ct` file you can load in the CodeTracer GUI or\x0Adebug from the CLI just like any other recording.\x0A\x0AThis guide walks through three deployment shapes:\x0A\x0A1. **Vite dev-server** \xE2\x80\x94 the recommended flow for everyday development.\x0A2. **AOT CLI** \xE2\x80\x94 `codetracer-js-recorder instrument` produces a\x0A   drop-in static bundle.\x0A3. **Webpack / esbuild / Rollup** \xE2\x80\x94 thin wrapper packages that share the\x0A   same SWC instrumentation visitor.\x0A\x0AThis guide describes the operational workflow for browser-app recording.\x0A\x0A## 1. Vite dev-server\x0A\x0AInstall the plugin alongside your existing Vite setup:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Anpm install --save-dev @codetracer/vite-plugin\x0A```\x0A\x0AAdd it to your `vite.config.ts`:\x0A\x0A```ts\x0Aimport { defineConfig } from \"vite\";\x0Aimport codetracer from \"@codetracer/vite-plugin\";\x0A\x0Aexport default defineConfig({\x0A  plugins: [\x0A    codetracer({\x0A      // Optional \xE2\x80\x94 defaults to ws://localhost:9230/ct-stream\x0A      endpoint: \"ws://localhost:9230/ct-stream\",\x0A    }),\x0A  ],\x0A});\x0A```\x0A\x0AStart the daemon receiver in another terminal (the M26 receiver entry\x0Apoint lives in `backend-manager`\'s `browser_stream_receiver` module \xE2\x80\x94\x0Asee the streaming-recording runbook for the host-process choice that\x0Amatches your deployment).  Then run `vite` as usual; every transformed\x0Amodule is instrumented, and the page emits events to the daemon\x0Aautomatically.\x0A\x0AHot Module Replacement keeps working: only the changed module is\x0Are-instrumented, mirroring Vite\'s default HMR behaviour.\x0A\x0A## 2. AOT CLI (`codetracer-js-recorder instrument --browser`)\x0A\x0AFor static hosting (CI smoke tests, GitHub Pages, edge deploys) the CLI\x0Aproduces a drop-in instrumented bundle plus a small browser-runtime\x0Abootstrap stub:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-js-recorder instrument ./src \\\x0A  --out ./dist-instrumented \\\x0A  --browser \\\x0A  --endpoint \"ws://localhost:9230/ct-stream\"\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe output directory contains:\x0A\x0A- The instrumented `.js` / `.ts` files mirroring the input tree.\x0A- `codetracer.manifest.json` \xE2\x80\x94 the merged manifest the daemon needs\x0A  to lower events into CTFS.\x0A- `codetracer-runtime.js` \xE2\x80\x94 the browser-runtime bootstrap.  Add it to\x0A  your `index.html` before any instrumented bundle loads:\x0A\x0A  ```html\x0A  <script src=\"./codetracer-runtime.js\"></script>\x0A  ```\x0A\x0AThe runtime opens a WebSocket to the configured endpoint, buffers events\x0Ain memory, and flushes on a 256-event threshold + on the `pagehide` /\x0A`beforeunload` page-lifecycle events.\x0A\x0A## 3. Webpack / esbuild / Rollup\x0A\x0AEach bundler has its own plugin package, all wrapping the same\x0A`@codetracer/instrumenter-core` visitor used by the Vite plugin.  The\x0Aoutput is byte-for-byte identical regardless of which bundler runs the\x0Avisitor \xE2\x80\x94 the per-bundler smoke tests pin this contract.\x0A\x0A```ts\x0A// webpack.config.js\x0Aconst { CodetracerWebpackPlugin } = require(\"@codetracer/webpack-plugin\");\x0A// (loader integration: see the package README)\x0A\x0A// rollup.config.mjs\x0Aimport codetracerRollup from \"@codetracer/rollup-plugin\";\x0Aexport default { plugins: [codetracerRollup()] };\x0A\x0A// build.mjs (esbuild)\x0Aimport { build } from \"esbuild\";\x0Aimport codetracerEsbuild from \"@codetracer/esbuild-plugin\";\x0Aawait build({ plugins: [codetracerEsbuild()] });\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Correlation markers (no protocol shims)\x0A\x0AThe browser recorder **does not** intercept `fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`,\x0A`WebSocket`, or `window.postMessage`.  Cross-process correlation rides\x0Aon the M25 user-placed marker mechanism: you place a marker at the\x0Aboundary in your own code, and the recorder evaluates it through the\x0Asame tracepoint surface it uses for any other event.\x0A\x0A```ts\x0A// In your page code, just before encoding the request body:\x0A__ct.markCorrelation(\"send\", \"outbound\", requestId, { kind: \"balance-fetch\" });\x0A\x0A// In your backend (Node, Python, Ruby \xE2\x80\x94 any recorder), just after\x0A// decoding the request:\x0Act.mark_correlation_recv(request_id, request_body, boundary=\"inbound\")\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe send/recv pair joins at trace load time, and a `ct originChain`\x0Aquery on the response value traverses both recordings end-to-end.\x0A\x0A## Uploading the recording\x0A\x0AOnce the daemon has flushed a `.ct` file, you can share it through the\x0ACodeTracer CI cluster with `ct trace upload`.  Browser recordings\x0Ausually lack the omniscient toolchain locally (no native emulator on\x0Athe page), so M31 lets the client pick how the cluster prepares the\x0AM18 / M19 omniscient artefacts (`memwrites.tc`, `linehits.tc`,\x0A`originmeta.tc`, `varwrites.tc`, `source_exprs.tc`) for the uploaded\x0Aslice:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act trace upload --omniscient-db=lazy ./recording.ct\x0A```\x0A\x0A| Mode           | Meaning                                                                                                                                     |\x0A| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `off`          | Default. Server stores the slice as-is; downstream replay runs Mode 1 with no omniscient acceleration.                                      |\x0A| `on`           | Eager prep \xE2\x80\x94 the server enqueues a high-priority job that builds the omniscient artefacts before the slice becomes replay-ready.            |\x0A| `lazy`         | Slice becomes replay-ready immediately in Mode 1; the omniscient build runs opportunistically (idle worker, or on the first omniscient query). |\x0A| `pre-prepared` | The uploaded slice already contains the omniscient namespaces (rare; needs the Nim emulator locally).  The server validates and stores them as-is. |\x0A\x0AThe mode rides on the CS-M7 `/finalize` body as the camelCase\x0A`omniscientDbMode` field.  See `Value-Origin-Tracking.milestones.org`\x0A\xC2\xA7M31 for the wire contract and `Architecture/CodeTracer-CI/\x0ACluster-Storage-And-Deployment.milestones.org` \xC2\xA7CS-M14 for the\x0Aserver-side dispatch and worker pipeline.\x0A\x0A## Troubleshooting\x0A\x0A- **Events do not appear.** Check the daemon is listening on the\x0A  configured endpoint (`ws://localhost:9230/ct-stream` by default) and\x0A  that `window.__codetracer_endpoint` matches.  The runtime silently\x0A  degrades to a no-op when the WebSocket cannot connect \xE2\x80\x94 the page\x0A  itself is never broken.\x0A- **Source maps are missing in devtools.** Pass `sourceMaps: true` to\x0A  the Vite plugin (the default) and `--source-maps` to the CLI.  The\x0A  instrumenter chains through any existing input source map.\x0A- **Recording disabled in production.** Either omit the plugin from\x0A  your prod build config or set `window.__codetracer_endpoint = null`\x0A  before the runtime loads \xE2\x80\x94 the browser runtime exposes a `disabled`\x0A  option for explicit control during local testing.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-2000332494), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,97,105,107,101,110,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Aiken (Cardano)\x0Aorder: 112\x0A---\x0A## Aiken (Cardano)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Aiken smart contracts on Cardano. The recorder steps through the UPLC (Untyped Plutus Core) CEK machine at every evaluation step and maps execution back to Aiken source code.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Aiken CLI** (`aiken`) v1.1.21+ for compiling contracts\x0A- **codetracer-cardano-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Create an Aiken project with a test function:\x0A\x0A   ```aiken\x0A   // validators/example.ak\x0A\x0A   test test_flow() {\x0A     let a = 10\x0A     let b = 32\x0A     let sum_val = a + b\x0A     expect sum_val == 42\x0A     let doubled = sum_val * 2\x0A     let final_result = doubled + a\x0A     final_result == 94\x0A   }\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-cardano-recorder record validators/example.ak -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   The recorder will:\x0A   - Compile the Aiken source to UPLC via `aiken build`\x0A   - Execute the test function through the stepping CEK machine\x0A   - Capture machine state (environment, budget, term) at every step\x0A   - Map UPLC term indices back to Aiken source locations\x0A   - Write the trace files to `./ct-traces/`\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Transaction\x0A\x0ATo debug a Cardano transaction that invoked a validator script:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-cardano-recorder replay \\\x0A    --tx-hash abc123...def456 \\\x0A    --blockfrost-key your-api-key \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe replayer fetches the transaction via Blockfrost, extracts the validator script and its datum/redeemer/context arguments, reconstructs the full applied UPLC program, and executes it through the stepping recorder.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AOn-chain scripts are flat-encoded UPLC bytecode. For source-level mapping, provide the original Aiken project source alongside the replay.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                      | Description                                           |\x0A| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <file>`           | Record an Aiken source file (`.ak`)                   |\x0A| `replay --tx-hash <HASH>` | Replay an on-chain Cardano transaction                |\x0A| `--blockfrost-key <KEY>`  | Blockfrost API key (or `BLOCKFROST_API_KEY` env var)  |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`     | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)            |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`      | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`) |\x0A\x0A### Execution Budget\x0A\x0AThe trace includes Cardano execution budget (CPU and memory units) at every step, visible in the CodeTracer variable pane. This helps identify which parts of your validator consume the most resources.\x0A")}, {Field0: 1347372594, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,114,117,98,121,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Ruby\x0Aorder: 5\x0A---\x0A## Ruby\x0A\x0AWe currently have partial support for the Ruby programming language\x0A\x0AThe recorder for Ruby is currently hosted in the [codetracer-ruby-recorder](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer-ruby-recorder) repo.\x0A\x0A## How to launch a program written in Ruby\x0A\x0AAdjust the steps below for your use case or run the exact steps to launch the space_ship program which is included with the repo.\x0A\x0A1. Navigate to CodeTracer\'s folder\x0A2. Use ```ct record <path to rb file> [<args>]``` and ```ct replay <name of rb file>``` (or directly ```ct run <path to rb file> [<args>]```)\x0A\x0A   Example: ```ct run examples/ruby_space_ship/main.rb```\x0A\x0A:::caution\x0ARecording ruby on macOS requires you to install ruby through [homebrew](https://brew.sh), otherwise trying to record ruby programs will fail due to the built-in ruby binary on macOS being more than 7 years old.\x0A\x0AOnce homebrew is installed, simply install ruby with `user $ brew install ruby`.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Recording a web application\x0A\x0ARack applications \xE2\x80\x94 including Ruby on Rails and Sinatra \xE2\x80\x94 are recorded as a\x0Arunning server rather than a single script, and each HTTP request shows up\x0Ain the Request Panel. That has its own guide:\x0A[Live requests \xE2\x80\x94 Ruby](/usage_guide/live-requests-ruby).\x0A\x0AIn short:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang ruby -o ./trace -- rails server --port 3000\x0A```\x0A\x0AThen `ct replay -t ./trace` in another terminal shows requests as they\x0Aarrive.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0ARails support is recent. If you tried Rails with CodeTracer some time ago and\x0Ait hung during boot, that was a recorder bug in integer conversion on large\x0Avalues; it is fixed.\x0A:::\x0A")}, {Field0: (-29623245), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,110,97,116,105,118,101,45,111,109,110,105,115,99,105,101,110,116,45,116,105,109,105,110,103,45,98,101,110,99,104,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Native omniscient timing benchmark\x0Aorder: 111\x0A---\x0A# Native omniscient timing benchmark\x0A\x0AThe `ct-bench native-omniscient-timing` subcommand measures the native\x0Aproduct path for a single ordinary C fixture under both MCR and RR.\x0A\x0AFor each backend, the benchmark records:\x0A\x0A1. native program runtime (`native_ms`),\x0A2. `ct record --backend <mcr|rr>` runtime (`record_ms`),\x0A3. `trace omniscient-prep` runtime (`prep_ms`),\x0A4. trace, total prep artifact, `memwrites.tc`, `linehits.tc`, and\x0A   origin-metadata sizes, and\x0A5. ratios relative to native runtime and recording time.\x0A\x0AThe default fixture is\x0A`src/codetracer-bench/fixtures/product-omniscient/rr_c_arbitrary/main.c`.\x0AIt has multiple functions, heap/global/stack writes, and filesystem I/O,\x0Aso RR records real events instead of a single trivial execution window.\x0A\x0A## How to invoke\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Ajust bench-native-omniscient-timing\x0Ajust bench-native-omniscient-timing --runs=3\x0Ajust bench-native-omniscient-timing --backends=rr\x0A```\x0A\x0AOutput lands in\x0A`src/codetracer-bench/target/codetracer-bench/native-omniscient-timing/`.\x0A\x0A## Current interpretation\x0A\x0AThe benchmark uses the same `ct trace omniscient-prep` invocation helper as\x0Athe size and slice-prep benchmarks. It does not force `--trace-kind`; the\x0Aproduct command detects materialized/MCR versus native/RR traces and routes\x0Ato the existing implementation for that trace shape. MCR slice/concurrency\x0Ascaling remains in the MCR-only `slice-prep-speed` benchmark, while this\x0Abenchmark compares per-backend record/prep time for one ordinary fixture.\x0A\x0AThe size columns deliberately distinguish the files emitted by the product\x0Aprep path for the selected backend. Backend-specific production stays behind\x0Athe component command reached through `ct trace omniscient-prep`; the benchmark\x0Aonly times that user-facing request path and reports the artifacts it creates.\x0A")}, {Field0: 722148277, Field1: [115,105,103,110,45,117,112,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Sign Up\x0Adescription: Create a CodeTracer account.\x0Alayout: minimal\x0Ahidden: true\x0A---\x0A# Sign Up\x0A\x0ACreate a CodeTracer account.\x0A\x0A:::form action=\"/sign-up\" method=\"get\" submit=\"Sign up\"\x0A@field name=\"Email\" label=\"Email address\" type=email placeholder=\"Email\" required\x0A@field name=\"Password\" label=\"Password\" type=password placeholder=\"Password\" required\x0A@field name=\"Confirm\" label=\"Confirm password\" type=password placeholder=\"Confirm password\" required\x0A:::\x0A\x0AAlready registered? [Sign in](/sign-in).\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 1380118967, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,105,110,116,114,111,100,117,99,116,105,111,110,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Introduction\x0Aorder: 0\x0A---\x0A# Introduction\x0A\x0ACodeTracer is a user-friendly time-traveling debugger designed to support a wide range of programming languages.\x0A\x0AIt records the execution of a program into a sharable self-contained trace file. You can load the produced trace files in a GUI environment that allows you to move forward and backward through the execution and to examine the history of all memory locations. They say a picture is worth a thousand words \xE2\x80\x94 well, a video is even better! Watch the demo below to see CodeTracer in action:\x0A\x0A  ![Watch the video](https://img.youtube.com/vi/xZsJ55JVqmU/maxresdefault.jpg)\x0A\x0A[Watch on YouTube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xZsJ55JVqmU)\x0A\x0A## The benefits of time-travel\x0A\x0ACompared to traditional debuggers, CodeTracer gives you two major superpowers:\x0A\x0A* **Once you capture a bug in a recording, consider it squashed!**\x0A\x0A  Bugs that are hard to reproduce can be painful to fix \xE2\x80\x94 you\xE2\x80\x99ve surely been there. Once such a bug is captured with CodeTracer, you\'ll rarely need more than 30 minutes to track it down! This is largely a consequence of the next superpower:\x0A\x0A* **Most bugs are easily revealed when you know the origin of any value in the program.**\x0A\x0A  All programs produce output. Some examples are bytes generated as a response to a web request, pixels being drawn on your screen or perhaps a simple log line written to the terminal.\x0A\x0A  When CodeTracer creates a recording, it captures a user-extensible set of output events relevant to the program. The GUI displays these events in a searchable chronological event log.\x0A\x0A  Consider a misbehaving program that prints unexpected output to a log file midway through its execution. Clicking on the specific output event in CodeTracer will take you to the precise moment and code line where it was generated.\x0A\x0A  The unexpected value must be originating from some variable that\'s being passed to the logging function. With CodeTracer, you can now ask the question \"Where did this value come from?\". CodeTracer will find another moment in the execution, potentially multiple seconds earlier, in a completely different part of the program where this particular memory location was last written to.\x0A\x0A  This could be memory corruption or a genuine logical error. Either way, CodeTracer will report the origin. Let\'s say that you end up in the correct function that is responsible for computing the problematic value, but another input there leads to the issue. You can continue the search by repeating the question \"Where did this input come from\"? It usually takes just a few of these jumps to earlier moments in time to arrive at the root cause for the bug.\x0A\x0A  Every time you jump to a new moment in the execution, you can fearlessly explore your surroundings by stepping forward or backwards, having access to a back button that can always get you to any previous point of interest. At every point of the journey, our novel UI shows you details about the past and future program states at a glance and you know your position in the precisely recorded call trace of the program.\x0A\x0A  These features combined, make for a truly powerful debugging experience.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-1182903367), Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,108,111,103,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Logs and Diagnostics\x0Aorder: 102\x0A---\x0A# Logs and Diagnostics\x0A\x0ACodeTracer consists of several components that each produce their own log\x0Afiles. When reporting a bug or asking for help, attaching the relevant logs\x0Ahelps us diagnose the problem quickly.\x0A\x0A## Log locations\x0A\x0AAll runtime logs are written under a temporary directory:\x0A\x0A| OS | Base path |\x0A|----|-----------|\x0A| Linux | `/tmp/codetracer/` |\x0A| macOS | `~/Library/Caches/com.codetracer.CodeTracer/` |\x0A| Windows | `%TEMP%\\codetracer\\` |\x0A\x0AEach CodeTracer session creates a `run-<PID>/` subdirectory containing logs\x0Afor that session. The most recent session has the highest PID number.\x0A\x0A### Log files per component\x0A\x0A| File | Component | Contents |\x0A|------|-----------|----------|\x0A| `run-<PID>/replay-server.log` | Replay server (trace loading, DAP handling) | Trace metadata, event loading, DAP request/response |\x0A| `ct-native-replay-stable-0.log` | Native replay worker (main thread) | RR/MCR replay queries, register/memory reads |\x0A| `ct-native-replay-flow-0.log` | Native replay worker (flow preloader) | Flow data preloading |\x0A| `ct-native-replay-tracepoint-0.log` | Native replay worker (tracepoint evaluator) | Event loading, tracepoint evaluation |\x0A| `rr-stderr-stable-0.log` | RR replay engine stderr | RR process output |\x0A| `run-<PID>/session-manager.log` | Session manager (backend coordinator) | Session lifecycle, worker spawning |\x0A\x0A### Finding the most recent logs\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Linux/macOS: list the most recent session directory\x0Als -td /tmp/codetracer/run-* | head -1\x0A\x0A# Show all logs from the last session\x0Als -la $(ls -td /tmp/codetracer/run-* | head -1)\x0A\x0A# View the replay server log\x0Acat $(ls -td /tmp/codetracer/run-* | head -1)/replay-server.log\x0A```\x0A\x0AOn Windows (PowerShell):\x0A```powershell\x0A$latest = Get-ChildItem \"$env:TEMP\\codetracer\\run-*\" | Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending | Select-Object -First 1\x0AGet-ChildItem $latest\x0AGet-Content \"$latest\\replay-server.log\"\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Enabling debug logs\x0A\x0ABy default, CodeTracer logs at the `info` level \xE2\x80\x94 only high-level progress\x0Amessages and errors. To get detailed diagnostic output for troubleshooting,\x0Aenable debug logging:\x0A\x0A### Environment variable (recommended)\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Linux/macOS\x0ARUST_LOG=debug ct replay my-trace\x0A\x0A# Or for maximum detail\x0ARUST_LOG=trace ct replay my-trace\x0A```\x0A\x0AOn Windows:\x0A```powershell\x0A$env:RUST_LOG = \"debug\"\x0Act replay my-trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Selective component logging\x0A\x0AYou can enable debug logs for specific components:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Only the replay server\x0ARUST_LOG=replay_server=debug ct replay my-trace\x0A\x0A# Only the session manager\x0ARUST_LOG=session_manager=debug ct replay my-trace\x0A\x0A# Both\x0ARUST_LOG=replay_server=debug,session_manager=debug ct replay my-trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Collecting logs for a bug report\x0A\x0AWhen filing a bug report, include:\x0A\x0A1. **The replay server log** from the failing session:\x0A   ```bash\x0A   cat $(ls -td /tmp/codetracer/run-* | head -1)/replay-server.log\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. **The native replay worker log** (if the issue involves trace replay):\x0A   ```bash\x0A   cat /tmp/codetracer/ct-native-replay-stable-0.log\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A3. **Steps to reproduce** \xE2\x80\x94 what commands you ran, what you clicked\x0A\x0A4. **The trace file** (if possible) \xE2\x80\x94 smaller traces are better. You can\x0A   export a portable trace with:\x0A   ```bash\x0A   ct trace export --portable -o trace-portable.ct /path/to/trace.ct\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Quick log bundle\x0A\x0ACollect all logs from the last session into a zip:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# Linux/macOS\x0ALOGDIR=$(ls -td /tmp/codetracer/run-* | head -1)\x0Azip -j codetracer-logs.zip \\\x0A  \"$LOGDIR\"/*.log \\\x0A  /tmp/codetracer/ct-native-replay-*.log \\\x0A  2>/dev/null\x0Aecho \"Logs saved to codetracer-logs.zip\"\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Frontend logs\x0A\x0AThe CodeTracer frontend (the GUI) logs to the browser/Electron console.\x0AThese logs are visible in the browser\'s Developer Tools (F12).\x0A\x0A### Desktop (Electron)\x0A\x0AOpen the Developer Tools console:\x0A\x0A- **Menu**: View \xE2\x86\x92 Toggle Developer Tools\x0A- **Keyboard**: `Ctrl+Shift+I` (Linux/Windows) or `Cmd+Option+I` (macOS)\x0A\x0AThe console shows frontend log messages with timestamps, log levels\x0A(DEBUG/WARN/ERROR), file locations, and task IDs.\x0A\x0ATo capture Electron console output to a file, launch CodeTracer with\x0Athe `--enable-logging` flag:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay my-trace -- --enable-logging --log-file=/tmp/ct-frontend.log\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Browser mode (ct host)\x0A\x0AOpen the browser\'s Developer Tools (F12) and switch to the Console tab.\x0AFrontend log messages appear with the same format as in Electron.\x0A\x0ATo filter CodeTracer messages from other page output, type `ct` or\x0A`codetracer` in the console filter box.\x0A\x0A### Index/server process (ct host)\x0A\x0AThe `ct host` process logs to stdout. When running `ct host` from a\x0Aterminal, these messages appear directly. In the Playwright test\x0Ainfrastructure, they are captured via the process\'s stdout pipe.\x0A\x0A## Configuration file\x0A\x0ACodeTracer\'s configuration is stored at:\x0A\x0A| OS | Path |\x0A|----|------|\x0A| Linux | `~/.config/codetracer/.config.yaml` |\x0A| macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/codetracer/.config.yaml` |\x0A| Windows | `%APPDATA%\\codetracer\\.config.yaml` |\x0A\x0AInclude this file in bug reports when the issue might be configuration-related.\x0A\x0A## Trace database\x0A\x0AThe local trace database is stored at:\x0A\x0A| OS | Path |\x0A|----|------|\x0A| Linux | `~/.local/share/codetracer/` |\x0A| macOS | `~/Library/Application Support/codetracer/` |\x0A| Windows | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\codetracer\\` |\x0A\x0AEach trace is stored in a `trace-<ID>/` subdirectory. To reset the database:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Ajust reset-db\x0Ajust clear-local-traces\x0A```\x0A")}, {Field0: 266307898, Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,111,114,105,103,105,110,45,107,105,110,100,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Origin Kinds Reference\x0Aorder: 103\x0A---\x0A# Origin Kinds Reference\x0A\x0AThis page enumerates the three closed enums that classify the rows of\x0Aan **origin chain**: `OriginKind` (per-hop classification),\x0A`TerminatorKind` (why the backward walk stopped), and\x0A`FrameTransitionKind` (the direction of a function-boundary crossing\x0Aon a hop). All three are part of the wire shape defined in spec \xC2\xA74.1\x0Aand travel verbatim across the DAP / MCP / CLI surfaces.\x0A\x0AThe canonical Rust definitions live in\x0A`codetracer/src/db-backend/src/task.rs`; the Python mirror lives in\x0A`python-api/codetracer/origin.py`.\x0A\x0AFor the user-facing walkthrough see\x0A[Value Origin Tracking](../usage_guide/value-origin-tracking.md).\x0A\x0A## `OriginKind`\x0A\x0ACarried on every `OriginHop`. The classifier emits the variant that\x0Abest explains the dataflow step from the *previous* hop to *this* hop.\x0A\x0A| Variant           | Meaning                                                                                                                        | Typical hop                                                                |\x0A| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `TrivialCopy`     | Source-level assignment that moves a value verbatim \xE2\x80\x94 `b = a`, `let y = x;`, `b := a`. Rendered de-emphasised in the chain.    | `b = a`                                                                    |\x0A| `FieldAccess`     | RHS reads a field of a struct/object/record \xE2\x80\x94 `x = obj.field`.                                                                 | `x = obj.field`                                                            |\x0A| `IndexAccess`     | RHS reads an element by index/key \xE2\x80\x94 `x = arr[i]`, `x = m[\"k\"]`.                                                                | `x = arr[i]`                                                               |\x0A| `Computational`   | RHS is an *expression* whose value derives from multiple sources \xE2\x80\x94 `r = a + b`, `s = fmt!(\"{x}+{y}\", ...)`. Highlighted hop.   | `r = a + b`                                                                |\x0A| `FunctionCall`    | RHS invokes a function whose return value is captured \xE2\x80\x94 `y = foo(...)`. When the chain *descends* into the callee, see below.  | `y = foo()`                                                                |\x0A| `Literal`         | RHS is a literal value \xE2\x80\x94 `x = 10`, `name = \"alice\"`, `xs = []`.                                                                | `x = 10`                                                                   |\x0A| `ReturnCapture`   | Hop captures the result of a function return (`await` / explicit return). Pairs with a `FrameTransitionKind::ReturnCapture`.   | `y = foo()` after `foo` returned                                           |\x0A| `FunctionReturn`  | Reserved alias of `ReturnCapture` re-emitted on the callee side so future renderers can style the two halves separately.       | (callee-side mirror)                                                       |\x0A| `ParameterPass`   | Hop crosses *into* a callee through a parameter binding. Pairs with a `FrameTransitionKind::ParameterPass`.                    | `def helper(a): ...` receiving the caller\'s argument                       |\x0A| `CrossThreadCopy` | RR/MCR backends \xE2\x80\x94 the write that produced the value happened on a thread other than the querying thread. Confidence \xE2\x89\x88 0.6.    | Tagged with the cross-thread icon in the chain panel                       |\x0A| `Unknown`         | The classifier could not parse the source line into a known shape. Treated as opaque; the chain typically terminates shortly. | Garbled / preprocessed line                                                |\x0A\x0ANotes:\x0A\x0A- `Computational` is the **classification of the hop**, not the\x0A  reason the chain stopped. A computational hop can sit in the middle\x0A  of a chain (the chain may keep walking the operands\' own origins);\x0A  it does not by itself end the walk. The chain ends when the walker\x0A  produces a `Terminator` row \xE2\x80\x94 see below.\x0A- `ParameterPass` / `ReturnCapture` hops are the only ones that carry\x0A  a populated `FrameTransition`. All other hops have `frameTransition`\x0A  set to `null`.\x0A- The classifier returns `Unknown` (`confidence < 0.5`) for any line\x0A  it cannot parse; the chain terminates with `TerminatorKind::UnknownSource`.\x0A\x0A## `TerminatorKind`\x0A\x0AThe bottom row of every chain is a `Terminator`, not a hop. Its\x0A`kind` is one of the variants below; the `expression` field carries\x0Athe terminator\'s source text and the `function` field carries the\x0Acontaining function when known.\x0A\x0A| Variant                  | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                       |\x0A| ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `Literal`                | Walked back to a literal RHS \xE2\x80\x94 `x = 10`, `x = \"alice\"`, `xs = []`. The chain\'s ultimate source is a constant inside the recording.                                                            |\x0A| `Computational`          | Walked back to a computational expression whose operands are themselves terminal (literals, parameters, externals). The expression is the chain\'s \"root cause\" in user-code.                  |\x0A| `ParameterAtRecordStart` | Walked back to a parameter binding at the recording boundary \xE2\x80\x94 the value entered the trace as a function argument, with no recorded caller. Common when recording starts mid-execution.       |\x0A| `ReadFromExternal`       | Walked back to a read from outside the recording \xE2\x80\x94 a system call, file I/O, network response, environment variable, randomness source.                                                        |\x0A| `RecordingStart`         | The backward walk reached the start of the trace without finding an earlier write. Typical for partial recordings.                                                                            |\x0A| `UnknownSource`          | The classifier could not parse the source line (e.g. macro-expanded, preprocessor output, missing source).                                                                                    |\x0A| `UnknownVariable`        | The queried variable could not be resolved at the requested step (e.g. typo, out-of-scope).                                                                                                   |\x0A| `DepthLimit`             | The walker hit the per-request `max_hops` cap before reaching a natural terminator. Use `--lazy` + `continuationToken` to resume.                                                             |\x0A| `OutOfBudget`            | The walker exhausted a backend-specific wall-clock or per-hop budget. Common on release-mode native traces where the assignment was elided by the optimiser. Often paired with `truncated: true`. |\x0A\x0AThe terminator\'s SVG icon in the GUI maps one-to-one to these\x0Avariants (see spec \xC2\xA73.2.2 for the icon-set table).\x0A\x0A## `FrameTransitionKind`\x0A\x0AWhen an `OriginHop` crosses a function boundary the\x0A`frameTransition` field is populated with one of:\x0A\x0A| Variant         | Meaning                                                                                              |\x0A| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `ParameterPass` | Chain descends *into* a callee. Rendered with the `\xE2\x86\x98` glyph on the hop\'s header line.                |\x0A| `ReturnCapture` | Chain ascends *back into* the caller. Rendered with the `\xE2\x86\x97` glyph on the hop\'s header line.          |\x0A\x0AHops on the same `OriginKind` axis usually pair one-for-one:\x0A`OriginKind::ParameterPass` carries `FrameTransitionKind::ParameterPass`;\x0A`OriginKind::ReturnCapture` / `FunctionReturn` carry\x0A`FrameTransitionKind::ReturnCapture`. Other hops carry no\x0A`frameTransition`.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A- [Value Origin Tracking](../usage_guide/value-origin-tracking.md) \xE2\x80\x94\x0A  user-facing walkthrough across all four surfaces (GUI, VS Code,\x0A  CLI, MCP).\x0A- [MCP tool reference](./mcp-tools.md) \xE2\x80\x94 `get_value_origin` /\x0A  `resolve_variable_step` schemas (the same enums appear on the\x0A  wire).\x0A- [`ct` CLI reference](./ct_cli.md#ct-trace-origin) \xE2\x80\x94 `--format json`\x0A  emits the canonical wire shape with these enum values.\x0A- `codetracer-specs/GUI/Debugging-Features/Value-Origin-Tracking.md`\x0A  \xC2\xA74.1 \xE2\x80\x94 the authoritative wire-schema definition.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-1660731205), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,112,104,112,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: PHP\x0Aorder: 104\x0A---\x0A## PHP\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports PHP 8.1 and newer.\x0A\x0AThe recorder for PHP is hosted in the\x0A[codetracer-php-recorder](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer-php-recorder)\x0Arepo, but you do not install or invoke it yourself \xE2\x80\x94 `ct` finds it.\x0A\x0A## How to record a PHP program\x0A\x0AUse `ct record` to record and `ct replay` to open the result, or `ct run` to\x0Ado both in one step:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act run hello.php\x0A```\x0A\x0ATo pass arguments to your script, put them after the filename. They reach PHP\x0Aunchanged, including flags:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act run script.php --verbose input.txt\x0A```\x0A\x0A## Recording a web application\x0A\x0APHP web applications are recorded as a running server rather than a\x0Asingle script. That has its own guide:\x0A[Live requests \xE2\x80\x94 PHP](/usage_guide/live-requests-php).\x0A\x0AIn short:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang php -o ./trace -- php -S 127.0.0.1:8000 -t public/\x0A```\x0A\x0AThen, in another terminal, `ct replay -t ./trace` shows each HTTP request as\x0Ait arrives, and double-clicking one jumps to the code that served it. This\x0Aworks with the built-in server and with php-fpm pools; frameworks such as\x0ALaravel, Symfony and WordPress need no special setup.\x0A\x0A## What you do not need to do\x0A\x0A- You do not need to edit `php.ini` or pass `-d extension=\xE2\x80\xA6`. The CodeTracer\x0A  extension is loaded for you.\x0A- You do not need to install the recorder separately. If it is genuinely\x0A  missing, `ct` tells you what is absent and how to install it, and exits\x0A  with a failure rather than leaving an empty recording behind.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 1564486973, Field1: [100,101,101,112,95,114,101,118,105,101,119,47,105,110,100,101,120,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Introduction\x0Aorder: 0\x0A---\x0A\x0A# DeepReview\x0A\x0AA diff is a proposal written in the language of text edits. It tells you,\x0Aexactly and completely, what the author changed. It cannot tell you what the\x0Achange **did**.\x0A\x0ASo a reviewer infers. You read `let contribution = (i as Field) * x;`, you build\x0Aa model of the loop in your head, you decide the arithmetic comes out right, and\x0Ayou approve. Only the first of those steps is evidence. The rest is a simulation\x0Ayou ran in your head against a program you have not seen run \xE2\x80\x94 and it is where\x0Areview defects come from.\x0A\x0A**DeepReview is code review with the recordings attached.** You give CodeTracer\x0Aa diff together with one or more recordings of that code actually running, and\x0Ait renders the diff with what each changed line did: which lines the run\x0Aexecuted, how many times, and which values they held on each pass through a\x0Aloop.\x0A\x0A![A DeepReview window: the VCS panel with the changed file and its coverage badge, the diff tab with the flow overlay, and the Agent Activity panel](/assets/img/deep_review/review-window.png)\x0A\x0A## The questions it lets you answer\x0A\x0AEach of these is a question about a change that a diff cannot answer at all, and\x0Athat a reviewer otherwise answers by inference, by asking the author, or by\x0Achecking the branch out and running it themselves.\x0A\x0A**Did this line run at all?** Every line inside a recorded function is shaded\x0Aexecuted or not executed, on the diff itself. A hunk that no recording ever\x0Aentered looks different from one that ran on every pass, so \"this branch is\x0Anever taken in practice\" stops being a suspicion and becomes something you can\x0Asee.\x0A\x0A**What value did it hold \xE2\x80\x94 on which pass through the loop?** Executed lines\x0Acarry inline value chips, and a loop the recording entered gets an iteration\x0Astepper above it. Stepping it walks the same line through the values it actually\x0Aheld: `contribution` through `0`, `5`, `10`, `15`, and `sum` through their\x0Arunning total. You are reading measurements, not re-deriving them.\x0A\x0A**Was this function called once, or forty times?** A recorded function carries an\x0Ainvocation stepper (`\xE2\x80\xB9 main: call 1 / 2 \xE2\x80\xBA`), and the file rows carry coverage\x0Acounts. A helper the change touched lightly and a helper it put on a hot path\x0Aare different reviews, and the dataset already knows which one you are looking\x0Aat.\x0A\x0A**Which parts of the change did the runs never exercise?** The same shading, read\x0Athe other way. It is the honest version of \"is this tested?\" \xE2\x80\x94 not a promise\x0Athat the untouched lines are wrong, but a list of the ones no recording has\x0Aanything to say about.\x0A\x0A**What did the agent that produced this change actually do?** When a review is\x0Acollected inside an agent session, the Agent Activity panel shows that session\'s\x0Aconversation, loaded by reference against the agent backend. The change, the\x0Aevidence for it, and the reasoning that produced it are in one window.\x0A\x0A## What a review actually is\x0A\x0ATwo commands and one artefact:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act review collect \xE2\x80\xA6   # build a review dataset from recordings + a diff\x0Act review <PATH>      # open it\x0A```\x0A\x0AA **review dataset** is a self-contained `review.json`: the diff, the source of\x0Aeach changed file, per-line coverage, the recorded flow (steps and values), and\x0Athe call tree. It is produced once and can be opened later, on another machine,\x0Awithout the original recordings \xE2\x80\x94 which is what makes a review something you can\x0Ahand to somebody rather than a session you have to be present for.\x0A\x0ADeepReview adds **no panel of its own**. A review routes its data into three\x0Apanels CodeTracer already has \xE2\x80\x94 the VCS panel, the Editor, and the Agent\x0AActivity panel \xE2\x80\x94 and leaves your layout otherwise alone. Everything you already\x0Aknow about the editor still applies inside a review.\x0A\x0A## What it does not tell you\x0A\x0AWorth stating in the same breath, because a tool that shows execution next to a\x0Adiff invites a stronger reading than it has earned:\x0A\x0A- **It does not tell you the change is correct.** It tells you what happened in\x0A  the runs you recorded. Judging whether that is what *should* have happened is\x0A  still the review.\x0A- **It is not a test report.** A review dataset carries no test results at all \xE2\x80\x94\x0A  no names, no statuses, no durations \xE2\x80\x94 which is why the Agent Activity panel\'s\x0A  Tests tile reads *not available for this dataset* rather than zero. `ct test`\x0A  is a separate command, and issues **no test certificates** \xE2\x80\x94 see\x0A  [Not yet available](/deep_review/not_yet_available) for exactly how far it\x0A  goes. Nothing in a review claims otherwise.\x0A- **Coverage is observation, never reachability.** A recording can say a line\x0A  was not observed. It can never say a line cannot be reached, and a review does\x0A  not pretend the two are the same.\x0A- **It reviews the changed code, not everything it calls.** Flow is collected for\x0A  the functions the diff touched; a callee gets a call count and coverage, but no\x0A  overlay of its own.\x0A\x0AThe full list of the things that are deliberately not there yet lives on\x0A[Not yet available](/deep_review/not_yet_available). It is worth a read before\x0Ayour first review: everything on it is real behaviour you will meet, written\x0Adown so you do not read it as a bug or, worse, as a measurement.\x0A\x0A## Where to go next\x0A\x0A- [Collecting a review](/deep_review/collecting) \xE2\x80\x94 the two commands, a worked\x0A  example end to end, and what happens with native (rr) recordings.\x0A- [Reading a review](/deep_review/reading) \xE2\x80\x94 the three panels, the flow overlay\x0A  and the steppers.\x0A- [The agent workflow](/deep_review/agent_workflow) \xE2\x80\x94 how an agent produces a\x0A  review, and how to make it do so reliably.\x0A- [Not yet available](/deep_review/not_yet_available) \xE2\x80\x94 the deferrals, named.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-1066472768), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,99,105,114,99,111,109,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Circom\x0Aorder: 106\x0A---\x0A## Circom\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Circom zero-knowledge circuits during witness generation. The recorder traces the witness computation and maps signal assignments back to your `.circom` source code.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Circom compiler** (`circom`) available in your PATH\x0A- **Node.js** (for the WASM witness generator) or **C++ toolchain** (gcc, make, gmp) for the C++ backend\x0A- **codetracer-circom-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Write a Circom circuit:\x0A\x0A   ```circom\x0A   pragma circom 2.0.0;\x0A\x0A   template FlowTest() {\x0A       signal input a;\x0A       signal input b;\x0A       signal output result;\x0A\x0A       signal sumVal;\x0A       sumVal <== a + b;\x0A\x0A       signal doubled;\x0A       doubled <== sumVal * 2;\x0A\x0A       result <== doubled + a;\x0A   }\x0A\x0A   component main = FlowTest();\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace using the WASM backend (default):\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-circom-recorder record flow_test.circom -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A3. Or use the C++ witness generator backend for larger circuits:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-circom-recorder record flow_test.circom -o ./ct-traces/ --backend cpp\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   The recorder will:\x0A   - Compile the circuit with `circom` (using `--wasm` or `--c` depending on backend)\x0A   - Generate a source map if using the [metacraft-labs circom fork](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/circom)\x0A   - Execute the witness generator with default inputs\x0A   - Capture signal assignments and map them to source locations\x0A   - Write the trace files to `./ct-traces/`\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                  | Description                                           |\x0A| --------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `<circom-file>`       | Path to the `.circom` source file                     |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>` | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)            |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`  | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`) |\x0A| `-b, --backend <BE>`  | Witness generator: `wasm` or `cpp` (default: `wasm`)  |\x0A\x0A### Source Mapping\x0A\x0AFor the best debugging experience, use the [metacraft-labs circom fork](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/circom) which adds a `--srcmap` flag for precise source-level mapping. Without it, the recorder falls back to heuristic source mapping.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-50531772), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,115,111,108,105,100,105,116,121,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Solidity (EVM)\x0Aorder: 109\x0A---\x0A## Solidity (EVM)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Solidity smart contracts running on the Ethereum Virtual Machine. The recorder compiles your contract with `solc`, deploys it to a local Anvil node, and captures the full execution trace via `debug_traceTransaction`.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Solidity compiler** (`solc`) available in your PATH\x0A- **Foundry** (`anvil`, `cast`) for local EVM execution\x0A- **codetracer-evm-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Write a Solidity contract with a `run()` function (or specify a custom entry point):\x0A\x0A   ```solidity\x0A   // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\x0A   pragma solidity ^0.8.0;\x0A\x0A   contract Example {\x0A       function run() public pure returns (uint256) {\x0A           uint256 a = 10;\x0A           uint256 b = 32;\x0A           uint256 result = a + b;\x0A           return result;\x0A       }\x0A   }\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-evm-recorder record example.sol --trace-dir ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   This will:\x0A   - Compile `example.sol` with `solc`\x0A   - Start a local Anvil node\x0A   - Deploy the contract and call `run()`\x0A   - Capture the execution trace via `debug_traceTransaction`\x0A   - Write the trace files to `./ct-traces/`\x0A\x0A3. To call a different function:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-evm-recorder record example.sol --trace-dir ./ct-traces/ --function myFunction\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0AOpen the recorded trace in CodeTracer:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                | Description                       |\x0A| ------------------- | --------------------------------- |\x0A| `<solidity-file>`   | Path to the `.sol` source file    |\x0A| `--trace-dir <DIR>` | Output directory for trace files  |\x0A| `--function <NAME>` | Function to call (default: `run`) |\x0A")}, {Field0: 727410884, Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,116,114,111,117,98,108,101,115,104,111,111,116,105,110,103,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Troubleshooting\x0Aorder: 2\x0A---\x0A## Troubleshooting\x0A\x0ACodeTracer is currently in an experimental state, so we expect that there are many bugs that have not been found as of now.\x0AIf you find any bug, please report it as an issue on GitHub.\x0A\x0A:::tip\x0AWhen reporting a bug, include the relevant log files. See the\x0A[Logs and Diagnostics](./logs.md) page for how to find and collect logs.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### Fixing outdated configuration/layout files\x0A\x0AYou can reset them by deleting your `~/.config/codetracer` folder currently.\x0A\x0AYou will be able to find more information in the configuration docs eventually: work in progress.\x0A\x0A### Resetting the local trace database\x0A\x0AThere are 2 commands that can be used to completely wipe all traces from your user\'s data:\x0A\x0A1. `just reset-db` - Resets the local user\'s trace database\x0A1. `just clear-local-traces` - Clears the local user\'s traces\x0A\x0A### Broken local build\x0A\x0ASometimes your local build might break. In most cases, a simple `direnv reload` and `just build` should be able to fix it.\x0AOtherwise, you can ask for help on our [issues tracker](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer), or in our [chat server](https://discord.gg/aH5WTMnKHT)\x0A\x0A<!-- TODO: Add more info here -->\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-1118910520), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,99,97,100,101,110,99,101,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Cadence (Flow)\x0Aorder: 113\x0A---\x0A## Cadence (Flow)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Cadence smart contracts on the Flow blockchain. The recorder instruments the Cadence interpreter\'s `OnStatement` hook to capture per-statement execution state, producing structured trace data.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Flow CLI** (`flow`) v2.x+ for running the emulator and tests\x0A- **Go toolchain** (Go 1.22+) for building the Go tracer helper\x0A- **codetracer-flow-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Write a Cadence contract:\x0A\x0A   ```cadence\x0A   access(all) contract FlowTest {\x0A       access(all) fun compute(): Int {\x0A           let a: Int = 10\x0A           let b: Int = 32\x0A           let sumVal: Int = a + b\x0A           let doubled: Int = sumVal * 2\x0A           let finalResult: Int = doubled + a\x0A           return finalResult\x0A       }\x0A   }\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-flow-recorder record FlowTest.cdc -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   The recorder will:\x0A   - Parse the Cadence source\x0A   - Configure the interpreter with an `OnStatement` callback\x0A   - Execute the program, capturing source location, variable state, and call stack at every statement\x0A   - Convert the execution trace to CodeTracer\'s format\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Transaction\x0A\x0ATo debug a Flow transaction from mainnet or testnet:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-flow-recorder replay \\\x0A    --tx-hash abc123...def \\\x0A    --access-node access.mainnet.nodes.onflow.org:9000 \\\x0A    --source-dir ./cadence/contracts/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe replayer uses the Flow emulator\'s fork mode to recreate historical state and re-execute the transaction with tracing enabled. Cadence contracts are stored as source code on-chain, so source is always available via the Access API.\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                      | Description                                           |\x0A| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <file>`           | Record a Cadence source file (`.cdc`)                 |\x0A| `replay --tx-hash <HASH>` | Replay an on-chain Flow transaction                   |\x0A| `--access-node <URL>`     | Flow Access Node gRPC endpoint                        |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>`     | Directory with Cadence source files                   |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`     | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)            |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`      | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`) |\x0A\x0A### Resource Tracking\x0A\x0ACadence uses a resource-oriented programming model where resources cannot be copied, only moved (`<-`). The trace captures resource lifecycle events (creation, moves, destruction), visible in the variable pane with resource type and UUID.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 74668111, Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,99,111,110,116,114,105,98,117,116,105,110,103,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Contributing\x0Aorder: 1\x0A---\x0A## Contributing\x0A\x0AWe welcome contributors!\x0A\x0AIf you want to fix something smaller, feel free to open an issue or a a PR.\x0A\x0AFor bigger changes it\'s advised to first open an issue/discussion in the relevant Github repo or to discuss it our team in our [Discord chat server](https://discord.gg/aH5WTMnKHT).\x0A\x0A### Contributors guide\x0A\x0AHere are some recommendations, however if you want more info, our docs/contributor guide are hosted [on the CodeTracer site](https://contributors-guide.codetracer.com/) !\x0A\x0AThe guide is written as a set of markdown documents and built using [mdbook](https://rust-lang.github.io/mdBook/) and [mdbook-alerts](https://crates.io/crates/mdbook-alerts) .\x0A\x0AYou can contribute to the documentation itself, by editing it in `docs/book` and making a pull request. You can iterate on it locally , by cloning the repo, activating it\'s nix devshell and running `just serve-docs [hostname port]`: it will serve it locally(by default on http://localhost:3000).\x0A\x0A### Style guide\x0A\x0AFor Rust, we are using `cargo fmt` to autoformat our code, and `cargo clippy` with some custom allow/deny rules inside the code.\x0AWe have a `cargo clippy` check in our CI, but one can also run it locally.\x0A\x0AFor Nim, we still haven\'t written down a guide or list of rules and principles that we agree on, so this is something that we hope to do.\x0AWe might also link to an existing document.\x0A\x0A### Commits/Pull Requests\x0A\x0AWe are using [the \"Conventional Commits\" strategy](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/).\x0A\x0AWe use or are ok with using more \"types\", not only those included by default in their official page: e.g. `cleanup:`, `tooling:`, `examples:` etc.\x0A\x0AWe use `git rebase`, not merge and currently use the github pull requests as the main way to add code. Any pull request would need at least one review\x0Afrom someone from the CodeTracer team.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-320190256), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,109,105,100,101,110,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Miden\x0Aorder: 107\x0A---\x0A## Miden\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Miden VM programs and smart contracts. The recorder captures per-instruction execution state from the Miden VM (a STARK-based zero-knowledge virtual machine) and maps it back to MASM source code.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Miden toolchain** for writing and compiling MASM programs\x0A- **codetracer-miden-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Standalone Program\x0A\x0A1. Write a MASM program:\x0A\x0A   ```masm\x0A   # flow_test.masm\x0A   begin\x0A       push.10       # a = 10\x0A       push.32       # b = 32\x0A       add           # sum_val = 42\x0A       dup           # keep sum_val\x0A       push.2\x0A       mul           # doubled = 84\x0A       swap\x0A       push.10\x0A       add           # final_result = 94 (but actually doubled + a)\x0A   end\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-miden-recorder record flow_test.masm -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Recording a MockChain Contract\x0A\x0AFor testing contract interactions with the MockChain simulator:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-miden-recorder contract \\\x0A    --wallet-id 0x1000 \\\x0A    --faucet-id 0x2000 \\\x0A    --symbol TEST \\\x0A    --debug \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Transaction\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-miden-recorder replay \\\x0A    --node-url http://localhost:57291 \\\x0A    --account-id 0x1234abcd \\\x0A    --transaction-id 0xdeadbeef... \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                       | Description                                              |\x0A| -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <PROGRAM>`         | Record a MASM source file (`.masm`) or package (`.masp`) |\x0A| `contract`                 | Record a MockChain contract simulation                   |\x0A| `--wallet-id <ID>`         | Account ID for wallet (hex, default: `0x1000`)           |\x0A| `--faucet-id <ID>`         | Account ID for faucet (hex, default: `0x2000`)           |\x0A| `--symbol <SYM>`           | Faucet token symbol (default: `TEST`)                    |\x0A| `replay --node-url <URL>`  | Miden node RPC endpoint                                  |\x0A| `--account-id <ID>`        | Account ID (hex)                                         |\x0A| `--transaction-id <ID>`    | Transaction ID (hex)                                     |\x0A| `--captured-inputs <FILE>` | Path to captured TransactionInputs JSON                  |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`      | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)               |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`       | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)    |\x0A")}, {Field0: 1020408527, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,118,105,115,117,97,108,95,114,101,99,111,114,100,105,110,103,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Visual recordings\x0Aorder: 103\x0A---\x0A# Visual Recordings\x0A\x0AVisual recordings capture graphics API activity together with the native program execution trace. They are used by the CodeTracer GUI to replay rendered frames, scrub draw calls, inspect pixel history, and debug shader execution.\x0A\x0AThis feature is built on the MCR recorder and the visual replay player:\x0A\x0A1. `ct record --use-interpose` records the program into a `.ct` container.\x0A1. `ct trace extract-gfx` extracts the graphics stream from the `.ct` container.\x0A1. `ct gfx-replay` replays the extracted graphics stream and exposes frame, draw-call, pixel-history, and shader-debug APIs to the GUI.\x0A\x0A\x0AWhen you open a compatible visual trace in CodeTracer, the GUI performs the extraction and player startup automatically. You usually only need to produce or open the `.ct` trace.\x0A\x0A## Recording a visual trace\x0A\x0AUse `ct record` with the interpose recording path. The `--use-interpose` flag routes through the MCR backend and captures graphics API calls:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act record --use-interpose -o /tmp/demo.ct -- /path/to/program --args\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor OpenGL tests in headless or CI environments, software rendering can make the result more reproducible:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0ALIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1 ct record --use-interpose -o /tmp/demo.ct -- /path/to/program\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe output is a CTFS `.ct` file. It contains the native execution trace and the captured graphics events needed for visual replay. Keep this file as the artifact to share, import, or open later.\x0A\x0A## Opening the trace in CodeTracer\x0A\x0AOpen the `.ct` file from the startup screen with **Open local trace**, or launch CodeTracer directly with a trace path:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act host --trace-path=/tmp/demo.ct\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor normal replay workflows, use the same GUI entry points you use for other traces. CodeTracer detects compatible MCR `.ct` files and marks the session as visual replay capable.\x0A\x0AWhen the trace opens, CodeTracer extracts the graphics stream into a temporary directory and starts `ct gfx-replay --http`. The temporary extracted stream is an implementation detail; the `.ct` file remains the durable recording.\x0A\x0A## GUI panels\x0A\x0AVisual replay sessions add panels for graphics-specific debugging:\x0A\x0A| Panel | Purpose |\x0A| ----- | ------- |\x0A| Frame Viewer | Shows rendered frames and the draw-call list for the selected frame. |\x0A| Pixel History | Shows which draw calls modified the selected pixel and what color each modification wrote. |\x0A| Shader Debugger | Shows shader source and per-invocation debugging information for a selected pixel or draw call. |\x0A\x0AIn the Frame Viewer, select a draw call to scrub the rendered frame to that draw. Click a pixel in the frame image to request pixel history. From a pixel-history entry, open shader debugging to inspect the shader source and recorded execution details for that pixel.\x0A\x0A![Frame Viewer showing a visual replay frame and draw-call list](/assets/img/visual_recordings/frame-viewer.png)\x0A\x0AThe Frame Viewer is the starting point for visual debugging. It displays the replayed frame and the draw calls that contributed to it. Selecting a draw call scrubs the image to that point in the frame.\x0A\x0A![Pixel History panel showing recorded writes for a selected pixel](/assets/img/visual_recordings/pixel-history.png)\x0A\x0AAfter clicking a pixel in the Frame Viewer, the Pixel History panel shows the draw calls that modified that pixel and the color written by each modification.\x0A\x0A![Shader Debugger panel showing shader source for a selected pixel](/assets/img/visual_recordings/shader-debugger.png)\x0A\x0AThe Shader Debugger opens from a pixel-history selection and shows the shader source and recorded debugging information for the selected pixel or draw call.\x0A\x0AClick any screenshot to open the generated full-size image.\x0A\x0A## Manual extraction and player usage\x0A\x0AThe GUI normally manages this pipeline, but the lower-level commands are useful for diagnostics:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act trace extract-gfx -o /tmp/demo-gfx /tmp/demo.ct\x0Act gfx-replay --gfx-stream /tmp/demo-gfx --http --port 9000\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor offscreen or CI runs, force the software backend:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act gfx-replay --backend software --gfx-stream /tmp/demo-gfx --http --port 9000\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe HTTP player serves the same APIs used by the CodeTracer GUI, including frame loading, draw-call scrubbing, pixel history, and shader debugging.\x0A\x0A## Development overrides\x0A\x0ADevelopment builds can point CodeTracer at non-default underlying binaries. The user-facing `ct trace extract-gfx` and `ct gfx-replay` subcommands resolve these binaries internally; the variables below override that resolution.\x0A\x0A| Variable | Description |\x0A| -------- | ----------- |\x0A| `CODETRACER_CT_MCR_CMD` | Path to the internal MCR binary that `ct trace extract-gfx` invokes to read the graphics stream from a `.ct` container. |\x0A| `CODETRACER_CT_GFX_PLAYER_CMD` | Path to the internal player binary that `ct gfx-replay` launches. |\x0A| `CODETRACER_CT_GFX_PLAYER_BACKEND` | Optional player backend override (equivalent to passing `--backend` to `ct gfx-replay`), for example `software`. |\x0A\x0AThese variables are mainly useful when working from sibling development repositories. Installed builds should find the bundled tools without extra configuration.\x0A\x0A## Regenerating the book screenshots\x0A\x0AThe screenshots on this page are generated by Playwright from a real visual recording:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Ajust capture-docs-visual-screenshots\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe command records the GL fixture from the sibling native test-programs repository when `CODETRACER_REAL_VISUAL_TRACE` is not set. To reuse an existing trace instead:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0ACODETRACER_REAL_VISUAL_TRACE=/path/to/demo.ct just capture-docs-visual-screenshots\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe generated images are written to `docs/book/src/generated/visual_recordings/`. This directory is ignored by git, so screenshots can be regenerated for local review or release builds without checking binary image files into the repository.\x0A\x0ATo inspect the rendered book page layout after generating screenshots:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Ajust capture-docs-visual-page\x0A```\x0A\x0AThis regenerates the visual replay screenshots, builds the book, and writes an ignored page screenshot to `docs/book/src/generated/book_pages/visual-recordings-page.png`.\x0A\x0A## Current limitations\x0A\x0AVisual replay depends on the recorder being able to intercept the graphics API calls made by the target program. It is currently focused on native MCR traces with captured GL/VK activity. Programs that render through unsupported APIs or bypass the interposed path may still produce a regular native trace, but they will not expose the Frame Viewer, Pixel History, or Shader Debugger panels.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-173581358), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,105,110,99,114,101,109,101,110,116,97,108,45,116,101,115,116,105,110,103,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Incremental Testing\x0Aorder: 105\x0A---\x0A# Incremental Testing\x0A\x0A**Trace-based incremental testing** makes a test suite re-run only the\x0Atests that a code change can actually affect. `ct test --incremental`\x0Arecords what each test does the first time it runs, and on later runs it\x0A**skips a test whenever none of the functions that test executed have\x0Achanged**. A test that did not touch any of the code you edited cannot\x0Aproduce a different result, so there is no reason to run it again.\x0A\x0AThis is the standalone, productized form of CodeTracer\'s runtime-dependency\x0Atest selection: it works on its own, without any external build system.\x0A\x0A## What it does\x0A\x0AThe idea comes directly from CodeTracer\'s recorded traces:\x0A\x0A1. The first time a test runs, `ct test` records a trace of it and remembers\x0A   **which functions it executed**, together with a hash of each of those\x0A   functions.\x0A2. On a later run, `ct test` looks at the test\'s recorded baseline. If every\x0A   function the test executed still hashes the same, the test\'s result\x0A   cannot have changed, so it is **skipped**.\x0A3. If a function the test *did* execute has changed, the test is **re-run**\x0A   and a fresh baseline is recorded.\x0A\x0ACode you changed that the test never ran \xE2\x80\x94 a different module, an unexecuted\x0Abranch, a helper the test never reached \xE2\x80\x94 does not force a re-run.\x0A\x0A## Using it\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act test --incremental --language <python|ruby> --program <path> \\\x0A    [--source-root DIR] [--cache PATH] [--id TESTID]\x0A```\x0A\x0A| Flag | Meaning |\x0A|------|---------|\x0A| `--language` | The language of the program under test. Live-validated: `python`. Wired: `ruby`. |\x0A| `--program` | Path to the test program to record and decide on. |\x0A| `--source-root` | Root used to resolve the executed functions\' source for hashing. Defaults to `/` (trace-recorded paths are absolute). |\x0A| `--cache` | Where the per-test baseline (executed functions + hashes) is stored, so a later run can compare against it. |\x0A| `--id` | A stable identifier for the test, used as the cache key and in the report. |\x0A\x0AEach run reports one of:\x0A\x0A- **`run (fresh baseline: <id>)`** \xE2\x80\x94 no baseline existed yet, so the test ran\x0A  and its baseline was recorded.\x0A- **`skipped (unchanged: <id>)`** \xE2\x80\x94 every function the test executed is\x0A  unchanged; the test was not re-run.\x0A- **`re-run (changed: <id> \xE2\x80\x94 functions: \xE2\x80\xA6)`** \xE2\x80\x94 a function the test executed\x0A  changed; the test was re-run and the changed functions are named.\x0A\x0AThe existing `ct test e2e \xE2\x80\xA6` surface is unaffected \xE2\x80\x94 only the explicit\x0A`--incremental` token switches into the incremental selector.\x0A\x0A## Per-language support\x0A\x0A| Language | Status |\x0A|----------|--------|\x0A| Python | \xE2\x9C\x85 Live-validated end-to-end (record \xE2\x86\x92 decide \xE2\x86\x92 skip/re-run). |\x0A| Ruby | \xF0\x9F\x94\x8C Wired on the same path (drives the production Ruby recorder); not yet covered by an automated end-to-end test. |\x0A| Other interpreted languages (JavaScript/TypeScript, \xE2\x80\xA6) | The same CTFS source-text mechanism applies as their recorders are wired in. |\x0A| Native (C/C++/Rust) | Supported in the engine via compile-time instrumentation (the `ct_instrument` call-trace facet) plus instruction-byte hashing; not exposed on this CLI yet, so a native program is rejected up front rather than silently skipped. |\x0A\x0AThe selection is **fail-safe**: any ambiguity \xE2\x80\x94 a recorder that cannot run, an\x0Aunreadable trace, a missing or malformed baseline, an unsupported language \xE2\x80\x94\x0Aresults in a **re-run**, never a false skip. A test is skipped only when\x0ACodeTracer can prove the functions it executed are unchanged.\x0A\x0A## How the decision is made\x0A\x0AFor each test, `ct test` derives the **executed-function set** from the\x0Afunction and call records in the CTFS (`.ct`) trace the recorder produces, then\x0Acomputes a **per-function shallow hash**. A test is skipped only when *every*\x0Aexecuted function hashes identically to its recorded baseline.\x0A\x0AThe hash adapts to the language so the comparison is precise:\x0A\x0A- **Interpreted languages (Python, Ruby, JavaScript/TypeScript):** a hash of\x0A  the function\'s **source text**, read from the CTFS trace. For these recorders\x0A  a function\'s identity is its source body.\x0A- **Native (compiled C/C++/Rust):** a hash of the function\'s **compiled\x0A  instruction bytes**, so a change that alters codegen is caught even when the\x0A  source line looks the same. The executed-function set comes from a\x0A  compile-time-instrumentation capture (`-finstrument-functions` via the\x0A  `ct_instrument` call-trace facet); the hash runs over a clean,\x0A  non-instrumented build.\x0A\x0ABecause the baseline records both *which* functions ran and *what* they\x0Acontained, an edit anywhere outside that executed set is provably irrelevant to\x0Athe test and is skipped.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A- [Tracepoints](./tracepoints.md) and the rest of this Usage guide for the\x0A  other CLI-driven CodeTracer workflows.\x0A- The same technique is available under the reprobuild watcher as\x0A  `repro watch --ct-incremental`, which applies incremental skipping to watched\x0A  test edges during a build.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-93694508), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,109,111,118,101,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Move (Sui / Aptos)\x0Aorder: 114\x0A---\x0A## Move (Sui / Aptos)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Move smart contracts on both Sui and Aptos. The recorder captures per-instruction execution state from the Move VM and maps it back to Move source code.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Sui CLI** (`sui`) or **Aptos CLI** (`aptos`) for building and deploying\x0A- **codetracer-move-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording from a Trace File\x0A\x0AThe Move recorder converts execution trace files (produced by the instrumented Move VM) to CodeTracer format:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder record trace.json -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor compressed traces:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder record trace.json.zst -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0AOptionally provide the Move source file for enhanced source mapping:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder record trace.json -s sources/my_module.move -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Replaying a Sui Transaction\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder replay \\\x0A    --digest <TRANSACTION_DIGEST> \\\x0A    --rpc-url https://fullnode.mainnet.sui.io:443 \\\x0A    --source-dir ./sources/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0AFor local development:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder replay \\\x0A    --digest <DIGEST> \\\x0A    --rpc-url http://localhost:9000 \\\x0A    --source-dir ./sources/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Replaying an Aptos Transaction\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-move-recorder aptosreplay \\\x0A    --txn-version 123456789 \\\x0A    --node-url https://fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com/v1 \\\x0A    --source-dir ./sources/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                            | Description                                                           |\x0A| ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <trace>`                | Convert a Move trace file (`.json` or `.json.zst`)                    |\x0A| `-s, --source <FILE>`           | Move source file for enhanced mapping                                 |\x0A| `replay --digest <DIGEST>`      | Replay a Sui transaction by digest                                    |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`               | Sui RPC endpoint (default: `http://localhost:9000`)                   |\x0A| `aptosreplay --txn-version <N>` | Replay an Aptos transaction by ledger version                         |\x0A| `--node-url <URL>`              | Aptos REST API (default: `https://fullnode.mainnet.aptoslabs.com/v1`) |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>`           | Directory with Move source files                                      |\x0A| `--profile-gas`                 | Run gas profiling (Aptos, default: `true`)                            |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`           | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                            |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`            | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)                 |\x0A")}, {Field0: 1030666964, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,119,97,115,109,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: WASM\x0Aorder: 4\x0A---\x0A## WASM\x0A\x0AThe implementation is based on a [wazero]() WASM interpreter patch and can be used for various WASM languages.\x0ATesting has been done using Sylus contracts and small rust-based WASM programs.\x0A\x0A### Steps to record / replay a WASM program\x0A\x0AAdjust the steps below for your use case or run the exact steps to launch the demo program which is included with the repo.\x0A\x0A1. **Make sure you have a rust WASM toolchain** For example you can use the wasm32-wasip1 target, which we will use for the remainder of the guide\x0A\x0A   1. Navigate to your WASM project directory.\x0A\x0A   Example: ```cd codeTracer/examples/wasm_sample_atomic```\x0A\x0A   2. Compile the rust source code to WASM\x0A\x0A   Example: ```rustc --target=wasm32-wasip1 ./sample_atomic.rs -g```\x0A\x0A   3. Use ```ct record <path to .wasm file> [<args>]``` and ```ct replay <name of .wasm file>``` (or directly ```ct run <path to .wasm file> [<args>]```)\x0A\x0A   Example: ```ct run sample_atomic.wasm ```\x0A")}, {Field0: 1799523926, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,106,97,118,97,115,99,114,105,112,116,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: JavaScript / TypeScript\x0Aorder: 103\x0A---\x0A## JavaScript / TypeScript\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing JavaScript and TypeScript programs. The recorder instruments your source code and captures execution state at every statement.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Node.js** (v18+)\x0A- **codetracer-js-recorder** installed via npm or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Write a JavaScript program:\x0A\x0A   ```javascript\x0A   function compute() {\x0A     const a = 10;\x0A     const b = 32;\x0A     const sumVal = a + b;\x0A     const doubled = sumVal * 2;\x0A     const finalResult = doubled + a;\x0A     return finalResult;\x0A   }\x0A\x0A   console.log(compute());\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-js-recorder record script.js --out-dir ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A3. With application arguments:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-js-recorder record script.js --out-dir ./ct-traces/ -- --port 3000\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A4. Filter which files are instrumented:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-js-recorder record src/index.ts \\\x0A       --include \"src/**/*.ts\" \\\x0A       --exclude \"node_modules/**\" \\\x0A       --out-dir ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Instrumenting Without Recording\x0A\x0AYou can instrument source files separately (useful for build pipelines):\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-js-recorder instrument src/ --out dist/ --source-maps\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag               | Description                                        |\x0A| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <file>`    | Entry file or directory to record                  |\x0A| `instrument <src>` | Instrument source files without executing          |\x0A| `--out <DIR>`      | Output directory for instrumented files            |\x0A| `--out-dir <DIR>`  | Trace output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)   |\x0A| `--format <FMT>`   | Trace format: `json` or `binary` (default: `json`) |\x0A| `--include <GLOB>` | Include glob pattern (repeatable)                  |\x0A| `--exclude <GLOB>` | Exclude glob pattern (repeatable)                  |\x0A| `--source-maps`    | Emit source maps during instrumentation            |\x0A| `-- <args>`        | Arguments passed to the instrumented program       |\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 1539621848, Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,105,110,115,116,97,108,108,97,116,105,111,110,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Installation\x0Aorder: 1\x0A---\x0A# Installation\x0A\x0A:::caution\x0ACodeTracer can only be installed on Linux and macOS currently.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Download binaries\x0A### Recommended: automatically\x0AWe provide a automatic installer that installs CodeTracer in the best possible way for your current system. You can run it by running the following in a terminal:\x0A```\x0Auser $ curl \"https://downloads.codetracer.com/install.sh\" | sh\x0A```\x0A\x0A:::caution\x0AOn macOS it\'s recommended that you install homebrew beforehand. This is because the installer tries to install ruby from homebrew to enable our ruby recording\x0Afeatures. Make sure you install homebrew in order to not lose this feature.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### Manually on Linux\x0AHere is a list of our Linux packages:\x0A\x0A- [Ubuntu (`deb.codetracer.com`)](https://deb.codetracer.com/)\x0A- [Debian (`deb.codetracer.com`)](https://deb.codetracer.com/)\x0A- [Red Hat (`rpm.codetracer.com`)](https://rpm.codetracer.com/)\x0A- [Fedora (`rpm.codetracer.com`)](https://rpm.codetracer.com/)\x0A- [Gentoo (metacraft-overlay)](https://github.com/metacraft-labs/metacraft-overlay)\x0A- [Arch Linux (AUR)](https://aur.archlinux.org/packages/codetracer)\x0A- [AppImage (amd64)](https://downloads.codetracer.com/CodeTracer-latest-amd64.AppImage)\x0A\x0A:::tip\x0AYou can place the downloaded app in a location of your choosing (e.g. `~/.local/bin`)\x0A:::\x0A\x0A### Manually on macOS\x0AYou can download a `.dmg` app bundle from our website:\x0A\x0A- [Download CodeTracer for macOS (`.dmg`, arm64)](https://downloads.codetracer.com/CodeTracer-latest-arm64.dmg)\x0A\x0A:::tip\x0AYou can place the downloaded app in a location of your choosing (e.g., the `Applications` folder on macOS).\x0AWhen you launch CodeTracer for the first time, it will prompt you to complete the remaining installation steps, such as adding the command-line utilities to your PATH.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A:::caution\x0AUpon the first launch, macOS users will see the error message \"CodeTracer is damaged and can\'t be opened\". To resolve this problem, please execute the command `xattr -c <path/to/CodeTracer.app>`.\x0A\x0AWe expect this inconvenience will be remedied soon through our enrollment in the Apple Developer program that will ensure CodeTracer is properly signed and whitelisted by Apple. See [this discussion](https://discussions.apple.com/thread/253714860?sortBy=rank) for more details.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A:::caution\x0ARecording ruby on macOS requires you to install ruby through [homebrew](https://brew.sh), otherwise trying to record ruby programs will fail due to the built-in ruby binary on macOS being more than 7 years old.\x0A\x0AOnce homebrew is installed, simply install ruby with `user $ brew install ruby`.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A:::note\x0APython recordings reuse whichever interpreter resolves when you run `python` (or `python3`) in your shell. Install the `codetracer_python_recorder` package in that environment (for example `pip install codetracer_python_recorder`) before running `ct record my_script.py`. You can point CodeTracer at a specific interpreter by setting `CODETRACER_PYTHON_INTERPRETER`. See the [Python getting-started guide](../getting_started/python.md) for a full walkthrough covering installation, recording, and replay.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Installation from source\x0A\x0A### Linux (and other Nix-based systems)\x0A\x0A#### Prerequisites\x0A\x0AOn systems that are not NixOS, you need to install `direnv` and `nix`.\x0A\x0ANix should not be installed through your distribution\'s package manager, but from [here](https://nixos.org/download/).\x0A\x0ADirenv should be set up in your shell, as shown [here](https://direnv.net/docs/hook.html).\x0A\x0A#### Building from source\x0A1. Clone the repository with submodules: `git clone https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer.git --recursive`\x0A2. Enter the created directory\x0A3. Add the following text to `~/.config/nix/nix.conf` if it doesn\'t already exist:\x0A   ```\x0A   experimental-features = nix-command flakes\x0A   ```\x0A4. Run `nix develop`\x0A5. Run `direnv allow`\x0A6. To build codetracer simply run `just build`. The location of the resulting binary will be `./src/build-debug/bin/ct`\x0A7. Now every time you enter the `codetracer` directory your environment should be updated\x0A\x0A:::tip\x0AUsers of Visual Studio Code might encounter issues when using `code .`. To fix them do the following:\x0A:::\x0A\x0A1. Run `direnv deny`\x0A1. Run `code .`\x0A1. Run `direnv allow`\x0A\x0A### macOS\x0A\x0A#### Prerequisites\x0A\x0AThe only dependencies for the macOS build are `git`, `bash` and `homebrew`.\x0A\x0A#### Building from source\x0A1. Clone the repository with submodules: `git clone https://github.com/metacraft-labs/codetracer.git --recursive`\x0A2. Enter the created directory\x0A3. Run `./non-nix-build/build.sh` from the root of the cloned repository. This will install all prerequisites like Rust, Nim and others using homebrew\x0A4. The resulting binary can be found at `./non-nix-build/CodeTracer.app/Contents/MacOS/bin/ct`, and a DMG installer is created at `./non-nix-build/CodeTracer.dmg`.\x0A\x0A\x0A### Building and running the tests\x0A\x0ACurrently, you can run the db-backend (Rust) tests:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0A# inside src/db-backend:\x0Acargo test --release --bin db-backend # test most cases: non-ignored\x0Acargo test --release --bin db-backend -- --ignored # test the ignored cases: ignored by default as they\'re slower\x0A```\x0A\x0Asome initial simple end to end Playwright GUI tests:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Ajust test-gui\x0A```\x0A")}, {Field0: (-2054172584), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,108,101,111,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Leo (Aleo)\x0Aorder: 108\x0A---\x0A## Leo (Aleo)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Leo smart contracts on the Aleo blockchain. The recorder captures execution state from the Leo interpreter and snarkVM, mapping it back to Leo source code.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Leo CLI** (`leo`) for building and testing programs\x0A- **codetracer-leo-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace\x0A\x0A1. Write a Leo program:\x0A\x0A   ```leo\x0A   program flow_test.aleo {\x0A       transition compute(a: u32, b: u32) -> u32 {\x0A           let sum_val: u32 = a + b;\x0A           let doubled: u32 = sum_val * 2u32;\x0A           let final_result: u32 = doubled + a;\x0A           return final_result;\x0A       }\x0A   }\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-leo-recorder record main.leo -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Program\x0A\x0ATo execute and trace a deployed Aleo program:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-leo-recorder replay \\\x0A    --program-id credits.aleo \\\x0A    --function transfer_public \\\x0A    --input aleo1abc...xyz \\\x0A    --input 100u64 \\\x0A    --endpoint https://api.explorer.aleo.org/v1 \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                       | Description                                                      |\x0A| -------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <file>`            | Record a Leo source file (`.leo`)                                |\x0A| `replay --program-id <ID>` | On-chain program ID (e.g., `credits.aleo`)                       |\x0A| `--function <NAME>`        | Function to execute                                              |\x0A| `--input <VALUE>`          | Input values (repeatable, e.g., `--input 10u32`)                 |\x0A| `--endpoint <URL>`         | Aleo node REST API (default: `https://api.explorer.aleo.org/v1`) |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`      | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)                       |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`       | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)            |\x0A\x0A### Note on Zero-Knowledge Execution\x0A\x0ALeo transitions execute off-chain with zero-knowledge proving. The recorder uses the Leo interpreter for source-level tracing of transition logic, while finalize functions (which execute on-chain) are traced at the AVM instruction level.\x0A")}, {Field0: 320824536, Field1: [115,117,112,112,111,114,116,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Support\x0Adescription: Need a hand with CodeTracer? Reach out to the team through the support form and we\'ll help you get unblocked.\x0Ahidden: true\x0A---\x0A# Support\x0A\x0ANeed a hand? Reach out and the CodeTracer team will help you get unblocked.\x0A\x0A:::form action=\"/support\" method=\"get\" submit=\"Send message\"\x0A@field name=\"Email\" label=\"Email address\" type=email placeholder=\"Email\" required\x0A@field name=\"Name\" label=\"Name\" type=text placeholder=\"Name\" required\x0A@field name=\"Category\" label=\"Category\" type=select options=\"Payments,Account,Software\" required\x0A@field name=\"Message\" label=\"Message\" type=textarea placeholder=\"How can we help?\" required\x0A:::\x0A\x0ACheck out our [frequently asked questions](/faq).\x0A")}, {Field0: (-1524529061), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,110,111,105,114,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Noir\x0Aorder: 2\x0A---\x0A## Noir\x0A\x0AThe initial release of CodeTracer has MVP support for the Noir programming language. It has been developed in collaboration with the Blocksense team and currently requires the use of the [Blocksense Noir Compiler](https://github.com/blocksense-network/noir), which is included in the CodeTracer distribution.\x0A\x0AWe support many of Noir\'s features, but not all: e.g. we don\'t support mutable references currently, we don\'t serialize struct values and some other cases.\x0A\x0AWe are planning on adding support for the missing features in the future.\x0A\x0A## How to Trace a Noir Program\x0A\x0ABefore tracing your Noir program, you must compile it and provide the inputs. CodeTracer expects your project to have a `Prover.toml` file containing the inputs for your program.\x0A\x0A1.  Navigate to your Noir project\'s directory.\x0A2.  Generate a `Prover.toml` file:\x0A    ```sh\x0A    nargo check\x0A    ```\x0A3.  Edit the necessary inputs in your `Prover.toml` file.\x0A\x0AOnce your project is ready, you can use CodeTracer\'s commands from the CodeTracer directory.\x0A\x0A### Running a Program (`ct run`)\x0A\x0AThis command executes your Noir program and displays the visual trace in the CodeTracer UI. This is the most direct way to see your code\'s execution.\x0A\x0ATo run the `noir_space_ship` example included with CodeTracer:\x0A```sh\x0A# Make sure you are in the root CodeTracer directory\x0Act run examples/noir_space_ship\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Recording a Trace (`ct record`)\x0A\x0AThis command runs your program and saves its execution trace.\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act record examples/noir_space_ship\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Replaying a Trace (`ct replay`)\x0A\x0AThis command loads a previously recorded trace and displays it in the CodeTracer UI.\x0A\x0A```sh\x0A# First, record a trace if you haven\'t already\x0Act record examples/noir_space_ship\x0A\x0A# Then, replay it\x0Act replay\x0A```\x0AYou will be asked to choose which trace to replay from a list of recent traces.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-2132489892), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,99,108,105,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Command-line interface\x0Aorder: 1\x0A---\x0A## Command-line interface\x0A\x0AThis page details some of the options, available to you through the `ct` CLI command.\x0A\x0A### Usage\x0A\x0AWhen you launch the CodeTracer GUI, it will offer you the option to also install the CodeTracer CLI. It provides convenient ways to create and load trace files from the command-line or to integrate CodeTracer with CI processes.\x0A\x0ARun `ct --help` to see the full list of supported subcommands, but the most commonly used ones are the following:\x0A\x0A`<application>` can be a source file or a project folder (depending on the language):\x0A\x0A1. `ct run <application>` - Creates a recording and load it in CodeTracer with a single command.\x0A1. `ct record <application>` - Creates a trace file that can be loaded later or shared. On Windows, `ct record` transparently routes through TTD; the underlying launcher requires an elevated shell.\x0A1. `ct replay` - Launches the CodeTracer GUI with a previously recorded trace file. Common usages are:\x0A   - `ct replay` - Opens a simple console-based dialog to choose what recording you want to replay.\x0A   - `ct replay <program-name>` - Opens the last trace of an application.\x0A   - `ct replay --id=<trace-id>` - Opens a trace by its trace id.\x0A   - `ct replay --trace-folder=<trace-folder>` - Opens a trace by its trace folder.\x0A1. `ct` - Launches the startup screen of the CodeTracer GUI.\x0A1. `ct help`/`ct --help` - Gives you a help message.\x0A1. `ct version`/`ct --version` - Returns the current version of CodeTracer.\x0A\x0AUnlike other debuggers, where the debugger is attached to your application process, here you have to launch your application\x0Athrough the CodeTracer CLI with commands like `ct run` or `ct record`(or through the user interface, which is documented in the next chapter).\x0A\x0AThink of debugging your application with CodeTracer as recording a video and then replaying it in order to find\x0Athe information you need. This is why we use commands like `record` and `replay`.\x0A\x0AFor native graphics programs, visual recordings are produced by the MCR recorder and opened in the GUI as `.ct` files. See [Visual recordings](./visual_recordings.md) for the `ct record --use-interpose` workflow and the Frame Viewer, Pixel History, and Shader Debugger panels.\x0A")}, {Field0: 1675999066, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,108,105,118,101,45,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,115,45,112,104,112,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Live Requests \xE2\x80\x94 PHP\x0Aorder: 8\x0A---\x0A# Live request tracking in PHP\x0A\x0AWorks with the built-in development server and with php-fpm pools. Request\x0Aspans are published by the CodeTracer PHP extension from inside each worker\x0Aprocess, so no framework integration is needed \xE2\x80\x94 Laravel, Symfony, WordPress\x0Aand plain PHP all record the same way.\x0A\x0A## Record the built-in server\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang php -o ./trace -- php -S 127.0.0.1:8000 -t public/\x0A```\x0A\x0AFlags after the program name go to PHP unchanged, so this is exactly the\x0A`php -S` command you already use. The recording stays in the foreground;\x0Astop it with `Ctrl-C`.\x0A\x0A## Record a php-fpm pool\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang php -o ./trace -- php-fpm --nodaemonize\x0A```\x0A\x0AA pool runs several worker processes, and each one writes its own container.\x0ACodeTracer handles this for you: `-o` names a *parent directory*, the workers\x0Awrite into it, and the panel shows the requests from all of them together.\x0AYou do not need to know which worker served which request.\x0A\x0A## Watch requests arrive\x0A\x0AIn a second terminal:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay -t ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0AThen send traffic:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acurl \'http://127.0.0.1:8000/index.php?id=7\'\x0A```\x0A\x0ARows appear as requests complete. Double-click one to jump into the code that\x0Aserved it.\x0A\x0A## Open a recording later\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A## What gets recorded\x0A\x0A- **The framework and route**, when CodeTracer can identify them.\x0A- **Status and duration** per request.\x0A- **Errors**, including the message for requests that ended in a fatal error\x0A  or uncaught exception.\x0A\x0A## Requirements\x0A\x0APHP 8.1 or newer. The CodeTracer extension is loaded for you \xE2\x80\x94 you do not\x0Aneed to edit `php.ini` or pass `-d extension=\xE2\x80\xA6` yourself.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AIf you used an early version of CodeTracer\'s PHP support: the standalone\x0A`CodeTracerSpan` PHP class and its JSONL sidecar are gone. Request metadata\x0Ais written into the recording itself, so there is no second file to keep\x0Aalongside the trace and nothing to pass to the GUI but the recording.\x0A:::\x0A")}, {Field0: (-1663844130), Field1: [100,101,101,112,95,114,101,118,105,101,119,47,114,101,97,100,105,110,103,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Reading a review\x0Aorder: 2\x0A---\x0A\x0A# Reading a review\x0A\x0AA review adds **no panel of its own**. It routes its data into three panels\x0ACodeTracer already has \xE2\x80\x94 the VCS panel, the Editor, and the Agent Activity panel\x0A\xE2\x80\x94 and leaves your layout otherwise alone.\x0A\x0A## The VCS panel\x0A\x0AThe VCS panel is the review\'s navigation surface, and it is the visible tab of\x0Awhatever stack hosts it when the review opens.\x0A\x0A![The VCS panel in a review: the Review header with the commit id, the trace-context selector, the file totals, the Unified Diff toggle, and the changed-file row with its coverage badge](/assets/img/deep_review/vcs-panel.png)\x0A\x0A- **Header** \xE2\x80\x94 `Review: <commit>` and the review\'s totals (`1 file +3 -2`).\x0A- **Trace-context selector** \xE2\x80\x94 a dropdown naming the recordings the dataset was\x0A  collected from (`run-1`, `run-2`). It appears only when the dataset carries\x0A  more than one, and today it **names** them without filtering by them \xE2\x80\x94 see\x0A  [Not yet available](/deep_review/not_yet_available).\x0A- **`Unified Diff` toggle** \xE2\x80\x94 active means clicking a file opens its **diff\x0A  tab**; inactive means clicking opens the **full file**. It changes what a\x0A  click does, not what the panel renders.\x0A- **Changed files** \xE2\x80\x94 one row per file with its status letter (`M`, `A`, `D`,\x0A  `R`), its `+`/`\xE2\x88\x92` counts, and a coverage badge (`8/8` above).\x0A\x0AClicking a row opens that file. A review does not concatenate every file into\x0Aone scrolling document; cross-file navigation is this list.\x0A\x0AThe changeset is immutable, so a review offers no refresh and no file watching.\x0A\x0A## The diff tab\x0A\x0AThe diff tab is an ordinary Monaco editor tab, so search, selection, copy,\x0Aminimap and keyboard navigation all work inside it. It shows one file: a header\x0Aline with the path and its counts, `@@` hunk headers, added lines in green with\x0A`+`, removed lines in red with `\xE2\x88\x92`, and context lines on the normal background.\x0A\x0A![The diff tab: the invocation stepper, the loop-iteration stepper, per-line flow shading, inline value chips and the context-expansion control](/assets/img/deep_review/diff-tab.png)\x0A\x0A**Context expansion.** `... Expand 10 lines above` / `... Expand 10 lines below`\x0Areveal more of the surrounding file, ten lines at a time, and repeat. In a\x0Areview this is a local slice: the dataset already carries each changed file\'s\x0Afull source, so nothing is fetched. Revealed lines are normal code lines and\x0Apick up the flow overlay like any other.\x0A\x0A**Hunk selection.** Click a hunk header to select it, shift-click for a range,\x0Actrl-click to toggle one. The toolbar shows the count and offers **Copy** (the\x0Aselection as a patch) and **Clear**. Staging, discarding and moving hunks\x0Abetween commits are *not* offered in a review \xE2\x80\x94 there is no working tree to\x0Astage into.\x0A\x0A## The flow overlay\x0A\x0AThis is the part a diff on its own cannot give you. Every line the recording\x0Aexecuted carries the standard Omniscience annotations, produced by the same code\x0Athat renders them during ordinary debugging:\x0A\x0A- **Executed / not executed shading** on each line inside a recorded function.\x0A- **Inline value chips** at the end of the line \xE2\x80\x94 a name chip (`<contribution>`)\x0A  and a value chip (`0`) \xE2\x80\x94 in the same style the debugger\'s value boxes use.\x0A  Values are never rendered as `//` comments.\x0A- **An invocation stepper** anchored above each recorded function\x0A  (`\xE2\x80\xB9 main: call 1 / 2 \xE2\x80\xBA`). A function that ran more than once has more than one\x0A  invocation, and the stepper chooses which one the values describe.\x0A- **A loop-iteration stepper** anchored above each loop the selected invocation\x0A  entered (`\xE2\x80\xB9 iteration 1 / 5 \xE2\x80\xBA`), so a line inside a loop shows the values of\x0A  the pass you asked for. It clamps at both ends and disappears for a loop\x0A  entered only once.\x0A\x0AIn the example from [Collecting a review](/deep_review/collecting), stepping the\x0Aloop control walks `contribution` through `0`, `5`, `10`, `15` \xE2\x80\x94 the four terms\x0Athe change introduced \xE2\x80\x94 and `sum` through their running total, on the same lines\x0Athe diff added.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0AThe loop stepper counts the **passes over the loop\'s header line** that this\x0Ainvocation recorded. For the `for i in 0..4` above that is five, not four: four\x0Aiterations plus the final exit test, which is a real recorded step with no `i`.\x0A:::\x0A\x0ALines with no recorded steps \xE2\x80\x94 including comments, which no collector reports \xE2\x80\x94\x0Aare shaded as not executed.\x0A\x0ABoth controls are **steppers**: previous/next buttons and a counter. The\x0Adebugger\'s draggable loop slider is not offered here.\x0A\x0A## The Agent Activity panel\x0A\x0AThe third pillar. Its primary content in a review is **the agent session that\x0Aproduced the dataset**, so you can read what the agent actually did in the run\x0Aleading up to it.\x0A\x0AThe session is loaded **by reference**: a dataset stores the session id and\x0Aenough context to resolve it against the agent backend (Agent Harbor, or an ACP\x0Aagent that advertises `session/load`). No transcript is copied into the file, so\x0Aa dataset never goes stale against the session and sharing a dataset does not\x0Ashare the conversation.\x0A\x0A`ct review collect` records the reference automatically when it is run inside an\x0Aagent session \xE2\x80\x94 it reads `CODETRACER_AGENT_SESSION_ID` and friends, the\x0Avariables the harness already exports:\x0A\x0A```console\x0AReview dataset ready: .ct/review/review.json\x0AAssociated with agent session: rv8-demo-session (acp)\x0AOpen it with: ct review .ct/review\x0A```\x0A\x0AA dataset with no session reference is a complete review; the panel simply shows\x0Ano conversation. When a reference cannot be resolved the panel says which of the\x0Athree things went wrong, in one sentence, rather than rendering an empty\x0Aconversation that reads as \"the agent did nothing\":\x0A\x0A- the agent cannot replay sessions,\x0A- the session could not be fetched (quoting the backend\'s own reason),\x0A- the session loaded but contains no messages.\x0A\x0ABelow the conversation the panel keeps a roll-up of the dataset itself:\x0Acoverage, a per-file table, and a **Tests** tile. That tile reads `n/a` \xE2\x80\x94 *not\x0Aavailable for this dataset* \xE2\x80\x94 because a review dataset carries no test results\x0Aat all. It is not a zero.\x0A\x0A## Full Files mode\x0A\x0ATurning the VCS panel\'s `Unified Diff` toggle off makes a click open the whole\x0Afile in the normal editor instead, with diff highlights on the modified lines\x0Aand the same flow overlay \xE2\x80\x94 the same value chips, the same shading \xE2\x80\x94 wherever\x0Athe dataset has data for the loaded file.\x0A\x0AFull Files mode has **no controls of its own**. It follows the invocation and\x0Aloop-iteration choices made with the diff tab\'s steppers, so the two surfaces\x0Aalways agree about which call they are showing.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A- [Collecting a review](/deep_review/collecting) \xE2\x80\x94 producing the dataset the\x0A  panels above are reading.\x0A- [Not yet available](/deep_review/not_yet_available) \xE2\x80\x94 what these surfaces do\x0A  not do yet.\x0A- [Graphical User Interface](/usage_guide/gui) \xE2\x80\x94 the panels outside a review.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-864457250), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,99,97,105,114,111,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Cairo (StarkNet)\x0Aorder: 111\x0A---\x0A## Cairo (StarkNet)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing Cairo programs and StarkNet smart contracts. The recorder consumes execution traces from the Cairo VM and maps them to Cairo source code via Sierra debug info.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Cairo compiler** (`cairo-compile`, `cairo-run`) or **Scarb** build tool\x0A- **snforge** (for StarkNet contract testing)\x0A- **codetracer-cairo-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a Standalone Cairo Program\x0A\x0A1. Write a Cairo program:\x0A\x0A   ```cairo\x0A   fn main() {\x0A       let a: felt252 = 10;\x0A       let b: felt252 = 32;\x0A       let sum_val = a + b;\x0A       let doubled = sum_val * 2;\x0A       let final_result = doubled + a;\x0A       println!(\"Result: {}\", final_result);\x0A   }\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record the trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-cairo-recorder record program.cairo -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Recording a StarkNet Contract Test\x0A\x0A1. Set up a Scarb project with `snforge` tests, then record a test trace:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-cairo-recorder trace-starknet trace.json -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A   Where `trace.json` is the execution trace output from `snforge test --trace`.\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Transaction\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-cairo-recorder replay \\\x0A    --tx-hash 0x04a3c... \\\x0A    --rpc-url https://starknet-mainnet.public.blastapi.io \\\x0A    --source-dir ./src/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                      | Description                                           |\x0A| ------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <file>`           | Record a standalone `.cairo` program                  |\x0A| `trace-starknet <trace>`  | Convert an snforge trace JSON to CodeTracer format    |\x0A| `replay --tx-hash <HASH>` | Replay an on-chain StarkNet transaction               |\x0A| `--rpc-url <URL>`         | StarkNet JSON-RPC endpoint                            |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>`     | Directory with contract source code                   |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`     | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)            |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`      | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`) |\x0A")}, {Field0: 1928409184, Field1: [102,97,113,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: FAQ\x0Adescription: Answers to common questions about CodeTracer \xE2\x80\x94 what it is, which languages it supports, recording and replaying traces, tracepoints, and installation.\x0Ahidden: true\x0A---\x0A# FAQ\x0A\x0AAnswers to common questions about CodeTracer. Can\'t find what you need? [Reach out to support](/support).\x0A\x0A:::faq\x0A:::q title=\"What is CodeTracer?\"\x0ACodeTracer is a user-friendly time-traveling debugger. It records a program\'s execution into a shareable, self-contained trace file that you can replay \xE2\x80\x94 stepping forward and backward to inspect the state of memory and variables at any point.\x0A:::q title=\"Which languages does CodeTracer support?\"\x0ACodeTracer can record and replay programs written in Noir, Stylus, WASM, Ruby and Python. See the Getting Started guides for language-specific instructions.\x0A:::q title=\"How do I record and replay a trace?\"\x0AUse the command-line interface: `ct record <program>` produces a trace and `ct replay` opens it in the GUI. The Usage Guide covers the CLI and the replay interface in detail.\x0A:::q title=\"What are tracepoints?\"\x0ATracepoints let you log expressions at specific lines across the entire recorded execution at once, without re-running the program. See the Tracepoints page in the Usage Guide.\x0A:::q title=\"How do I install CodeTracer?\"\x0AOn Linux and macOS you can install the latest binaries with a single command, or build from source. Full steps are on the Installation page.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Popular articles\x0A\x0A:::cards variant=\"compact\"\x0A:::card title=\"Installation\" href=\"/getting_started/installation\"\x0AGetting Started\x0A:::card title=\"Graphical interface\" href=\"/usage_guide/gui\"\x0AUsage Guide\x0A:::card title=\"Overview\" href=\"/getting_started\"\x0AGetting Started\x0A:::card title=\"Environment variables\" href=\"/reference/environment_variables\"\x0AReference\x0A:::card title=\"Stylus\" href=\"/getting_started/stylus\"\x0AGetting Started\x0A:::card title=\"Noir\" href=\"/getting_started/noir\"\x0AGetting Started\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Still have questions?\x0A\x0ABrowse the documentation from the navigation, or [reach out to our support team](/support) and we\'ll help you out.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-40200735), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,108,105,118,101,45,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,115,45,106,97,118,97,115,99,114,105,112,116,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Live Requests \xE2\x80\x94 JavaScript\x0Aorder: 10\x0A---\x0A# Live request tracking in JavaScript\x0A\x0AWorks with Express and other Node HTTP servers. Request spans are published\x0Aby middleware inside the recorded process.\x0A\x0A## Record your server\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang javascript -o ./trace -- node server.js --port 3000\x0A```\x0A\x0AFlags after the program name reach Node unchanged. Stop with `Ctrl-C`.\x0A\x0A## Watch requests arrive\x0A\x0AIn a second terminal:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay -t ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0ASend traffic:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acurl http://localhost:3000/users/7\x0A```\x0A\x0ADouble-click a row to jump into the route handler that served it.\x0A\x0A## Open a recording later\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A## What gets recorded\x0A\x0A- **Route patterns** rather than concrete paths, so requests to one endpoint\x0A  group together.\x0A- **Status and duration** per request.\x0A- **Errors**, including the message for requests that threw.\x0A\x0A## Concurrent requests\x0A\x0ANode serves requests concurrently on one thread, interleaving them at every\x0A`await`. CodeTracer follows the asynchronous execution context, so each\x0Arequest\'s span covers *its own* work rather than whatever happened to run\x0Abetween its start and end. Two requests that overlap in wall-clock time\x0Aproduce two correctly separated rows.\x0A\x0A## Requirements\x0A\x0ANode.js 20 or newer.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0A`ct record app.js` also works for ordinary, non-server scripts. If you have\x0Aused it before and found that recordings appeared empty, that was an import\x0Aresolution bug affecting `node_modules`; it is fixed.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Browser applications\x0A\x0AThis page covers server-side Node. To record a front-end application running\x0Ain a browser, see [Recording a browser\x0Aapp](/usage_guide/recording-a-browser-app), which uses a different mechanism.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 524176100, Field1: [100,101,101,112,95,114,101,118,105,101,119,47,97,103,101,110,116,95,119,111,114,107,102,108,111,119,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: The agent workflow\x0Aorder: 3\x0A---\x0A\x0A# The agent workflow\x0A\x0AAn agent produces a review the way a person does: it runs `ct review collect`,\x0Athen points CodeTracer at what it produced. There is no agent-specific\x0Acollection path.\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act review collect --repo . --diff main..HEAD --recordings .ct/runs -o .ct/review\x0Act agent evidence .ct/review\x0A```\x0A\x0A`ct agent evidence` takes the dataset and nothing else in the common case. The\x0Asession, task and workspace are read from the environment the agent already runs\x0Aunder \xE2\x80\x94 `CODETRACER_AGENT_SESSION_ID`, `CODETRACER_AGENT_TASK_ID`,\x0A`CODETRACER_AGENT_WORKSPACE` \xE2\x80\x94 because the harness that launched the agent knows\x0Aall three, and asking the agent to restate them only invites disagreement.\x0A`--session`, `--task` and `--workspace` override the environment for use outside\x0Aa managed session and in tests.\x0A\x0AWhen none of the three sources names a session, the command **fails and says\x0Aso**. It does not invent an id, because an invented one attaches the review to\x0Athe wrong conversation and nothing downstream can tell:\x0A\x0A```console\x0A$ ct agent evidence .ct/review\x0Aerror: `ct agent evidence` could not tell which agent session this evidence belongs to.\x0A  It reads the session from CODETRACER_AGENT_SESSION_ID, which the agent harness sets;\x0A  that variable is unset, no --session was given, and the dataset at \'.ct/review/review.json\'\x0A  carries no session reference of its own.\x0A  Export CODETRACER_AGENT_SESSION_ID, or pass --session <ID> explicitly.\x0A```\x0A\x0A`ct agent evidence` accepts either the directory `ct review collect` produced or\x0Athe `review.json` inside it. (The text `ct agent prompt` prints spells the\x0Acollection `-o review.json`; `--output` always names a *directory*, so that form\x0Aproduces a directory called `review.json` \xE2\x80\x94 which both `ct agent evidence` and\x0A`ct review` open, because they resolve a directory to the dataset inside it.)\x0A\x0A## Telling the agent to do it\x0A\x0ACodeTracer ships the prompt text. Print it with:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act agent prompt\x0A```\x0A\x0Aand install it into a project\'s agent instructions with:\x0A\x0A```sh\x0Act agent prompt >> AGENTS.md\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe text teaches the pair of commands above, tells the agent to fix a failing\x0Asuite rather than hand over evidence for it, permits collecting from a dirty\x0Aworking tree provided it says so, and forbids hand-writing a dataset \xE2\x80\x94 a file an\x0Aagent assembled itself asserts coverage and execution that never happened.\x0A\x0A## The end-of-turn hook\x0A\x0AA project that would rather not depend on the agent remembering can run the\x0Acollection from an end-of-turn hook. `ct agent end-of-turn` runs the *same two\x0Acommands, in that order*, and prints each one as it runs it:\x0A\x0A```console\x0A$ ct agent end-of-turn\x0A+ ct review collect --repo /home/you/scale_sum --diff HEAD~..HEAD --recordings .ct/runs --output .ct/review\x0ADeepReview collect complete: 2 recordings collected, 1 files, 1 with coverage, 2 function executions\x0AReview dataset ready: .ct/review/review.json\x0AAssociated with agent session: rv8-demo-session (acp)\x0AOpen it with: ct review .ct/review\x0A+ ct agent evidence .ct/review\x0A{\"sessionId\":\"rv8-demo-session\",\"taskId\":\"rv8-demo-task\", \xE2\x80\xA6}\x0A```\x0A\x0AIt takes all of `ct review collect`\'s options, and reads them from the\x0Aenvironment when they are not given, so the hook line in a harness\'s\x0Aconfiguration can be bare:\x0A\x0A| Variable                       | Fills in            | Default    |\x0A| ------------------------------ | ------------------- | ---------- |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_REPO`       | `--repo`            | the workspace |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_DIFF`       | `--diff`            | \xE2\x80\x94          |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_DIFF_FILE`  | `--diff-file`       | \xE2\x80\x94          |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_RECORDINGS` | `--recordings`      | `.ct/runs` |\x0A| `CODETRACER_REVIEW_OUTPUT`     | `--output`          | `.ct/review` |\x0A\x0AThe two are not exclusive, and the trade is real: prompting keeps the judgement\x0Aof *what is worth reviewing* with the agent, which is where it belongs; a hook is\x0Areliable but indiscriminate and will produce datasets for turns nobody wanted\x0Areviewed. A project that uses a hook should still prompt, so the agent knows what\x0Athe hook will do and does not duplicate it.\x0A\x0A## Reading the result\x0A\x0AThe session a review is collected under is what the Agent Activity panel shows\x0Awhen the review is opened \xE2\x80\x94 see\x0A[The Agent Activity panel](/deep_review/reading). The session is stored **by\x0Areference**, so the dataset carries an id rather than a transcript.\x0A\x0A## See also\x0A\x0A- [Collecting a review](/deep_review/collecting) \xE2\x80\x94 the commands, in the\x0A  non-agent case.\x0A- [`ct` CLI reference](/reference/ct_cli) \xE2\x80\x94 the full `ct agent` flag tables.\x0A- [Not yet available](/deep_review/not_yet_available) \xE2\x80\x94 the deferrals, named.\x0A")}, {Field0: (-215665308), Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,109,99,112,45,116,111,111,108,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: MCP Tool Reference\x0Aorder: 102\x0A---\x0A## MCP Tool Reference\x0A\x0ACodeTracer ships an **MCP (Model Context Protocol) server** that exposes\x0Atrace querying as tools for LLM agents. The server is part of the\x0A`backend-manager` binary and communicates over stdio using JSON-RPC 2.0.\x0A\x0AStart it with:\x0A\x0A```\x0Abackend-manager trace mcp\x0A```\x0A\x0AAny MCP-compatible client can spawn the process and exchange\x0Anewline-delimited JSON-RPC messages on stdin/stdout. See the\x0A`backend-manager` setup guide for Claude Code / Claude Desktop wiring.\x0A\x0AThis page documents the **value-origin** tools registered by the M8\x0Amilestone. For the full tool list see the tool registration in\x0A`src/backend-manager/src/mcp_server.rs`.\x0A\x0A### `get_value_origin`\x0A\x0AReturns the canonical `OriginChain` for a queried variable at a chosen\x0Astep. This is a one-shot fallback for callers that only want a single\x0Achain \xE2\x80\x94 the **preferred** multi-step path is to send a Python script\x0Athrough the existing `exec_script` tool and call\x0A`trace.value_origin(\"<variable>\", step=..., frame=..., max_hops=...)`\x0Ainside it. The Python binding composes with `trace.locals()`,\x0A`trace.history()`, breakpoints and watchpoints, and reuses the loaded\x0Atrace + classifier pattern set across calls.\x0A\x0A#### Input schema\x0A\x0A| Field         | Type      | Required | Description                                                                                                                                                                  |\x0A| ------------- | --------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `trace_path`  | `string`  | yes      | Either a local path to a `.ct` trace folder or an observability dive-in URL (see `exec_script` for URL format).                                                              |\x0A| `variable`    | `string`  | yes      | Variable identifier to query. V1 is identifier-only; dotted paths are reserved for a future milestone.                                                                       |\x0A| `step`        | `integer` | no       | Optional step id. Defaults to the current execution point of the session.                                                                                                    |\x0A| `frame`       | `integer` | no       | Optional DAP frame id. Defaults to the topmost frame.                                                                                                                        |\x0A| `max_hops`    | `integer` | no       | Maximum hops in this batch (default 16). Prefer `lazy` + `continuation_token` over bumping this above ~32.                                                                   |\x0A| `lazy`        | `boolean` | no       | When `true`, the backend may return early with a `continuationToken`. Default `false`.                                                                                       |\x0A| `session_id`  | `string`  | no       | Optional session identifier \xE2\x80\x94 when reused across calls the trace and classifier pattern set stay loaded, avoiding a re-load. Same semantics as `exec_script.session_id`.    |\x0A\x0A#### Output shape\x0A\x0AThe tool returns the canonical `OriginChain` JSON pretty-printed in the\x0AMCP text content envelope. The body matches the wire shape from spec\x0A\xC2\xA74.1:\x0A\x0A```json\x0A{\x0A  \"hops\": [\x0A    {\x0A      \"kind\": \"TrivialCopy\",\x0A      \"stepId\": 41,\x0A      \"location\": { \"path\": \"main.py\", \"line\": 6, \"column\": 0 },\x0A      \"source\": \"b = helper(a)\",\x0A      \"frameTransition\": { \"kind\": \"ParameterPass\", \"from\": \"main\", \"to\": \"helper\" }\x0A    },\x0A    { \"kind\": \"TrivialCopy\", \"stepId\": 38, \"location\": { \"path\": \"main.py\", \"line\": 5 }, \"source\": \"a = 10\" }\x0A  ],\x0A  \"terminator\": {\x0A    \"kind\": \"Literal\",\x0A    \"expression\": \"10\",\x0A    \"location\": { \"path\": \"main.py\", \"line\": 5 }\x0A  },\x0A  \"truncated\": false,\x0A  \"continuationToken\": null,\x0A  \"metrics\": { \"elapsedMs\": 12, \"hopsWalked\": 2 },\x0A  \"confidence\": 1.0\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0AWhen `truncated` is `true`, pass `continuationToken` back as the\x0A`continuationToken` argument on the next call to resume the walk.\x0A\x0A#### Example call\x0A\x0A```json\x0A{\x0A  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\x0A  \"id\": 7,\x0A  \"method\": \"tools/call\",\x0A  \"params\": {\x0A    \"name\": \"get_value_origin\",\x0A    \"arguments\": {\x0A      \"trace_path\": \"/traces/my-bug.ct\",\x0A      \"variable\": \"total\",\x0A      \"step\": 137,\x0A      \"max_hops\": 8\x0A    }\x0A  }\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0A### `resolve_variable_step`\x0A\x0AReturns the most recent step id at which `variable` was assigned in the\x0Atrace. Pair it with `get_value_origin` when you want the chain at the\x0Aassignment site rather than the caller\'s current step.\x0A\x0A#### Input schema\x0A\x0A| Field        | Type      | Required | Description                                                |\x0A| ------------ | --------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `trace_path` | `string`  | yes      | Path to the trace folder (or dive-in URL).                 |\x0A| `variable`   | `string`  | yes      | Variable identifier to look up.                            |\x0A| `frame`      | `integer` | no       | Optional DAP frame id (scopes the search to that frame).   |\x0A| `session_id` | `string`  | no       | Optional session identifier; same semantics as above.      |\x0A\x0A#### Output shape\x0A\x0A```json\x0A{\x0A  \"stepId\": 138,\x0A  \"variable\": \"total\",\x0A  \"location\": { \"path\": \"main.py\", \"line\": 53, \"column\": 4 }\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe formatter scans `ct/load-history` updates in reverse and returns the\x0Afirst match. When no assignment is found the call fails with the\x0Abackend\'s error message.\x0A\x0A#### Example call\x0A\x0A```json\x0A{\x0A  \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\x0A  \"id\": 8,\x0A  \"method\": \"tools/call\",\x0A  \"params\": {\x0A    \"name\": \"resolve_variable_step\",\x0A    \"arguments\": {\x0A      \"trace_path\": \"/traces/my-bug.ct\",\x0A      \"variable\": \"total\"\x0A    }\x0A  }\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Implementation references\x0A\x0A| Item                                       | Location                                                              |\x0A| ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| Tool registration                          | `src/backend-manager/src/mcp_server.rs::get_value_origin_tool`        |\x0A|                                            | `src/backend-manager/src/mcp_server.rs::resolve_variable_step_tool`   |\x0A| Tool handlers                              | `src/backend-manager/src/mcp_server.rs::handle_get_value_origin`      |\x0A|                                            | `src/backend-manager/src/mcp_server.rs::handle_resolve_variable_step` |\x0A| Response formatters                        | `src/backend-manager/src/python_bridge.rs`                            |\x0A| Wire-shape definitions                     | `src/db-backend/src/task.rs` (Rust) / `python-api/codetracer/origin.py` (Python) |\x0A\x0AFor the user-facing walkthrough, see\x0A[Value Origin Tracking](../usage_guide/value-origin-tracking.md).\x0A")}, {Field0: 742413540, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,108,105,118,101,45,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,115,45,110,97,116,105,118,101,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Live Requests \xE2\x80\x94 Native Servers\x0Aorder: 11\x0A---\x0A# Live request tracking in native servers\x0A\x0ACovers servers written in C, C++, Rust, Go and other compiled languages \xE2\x80\x94\x0Aincluding ones whose source you do not have, such as a stock `nginx`.\x0A\x0A## How this differs from the other languages\x0A\x0APython, Ruby, PHP, JavaScript and Elixir all publish request spans from a\x0Amiddleware layer inside the recorded process. A native server has no such\x0Aseam: there is no standard middleware interface to hook, and the server may\x0Anot be code you can modify at all.\x0A\x0ASo CodeTracer takes a different route. A supervisor discovers the requests\x0Afrom the recording itself, rather than the server announcing them. You do\x0Anot need to link a library, recompile, or patch the binary.\x0A\x0A## Record your server\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server -o ./trace -- nginx -g \'daemon off;\'\x0A```\x0A\x0Aor your own binary:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server -o ./trace -- ./my-service --listen 127.0.0.1:8080\x0A```\x0A\x0AFlags after the program name are passed through unchanged. The server must\x0Arun in the foreground \xE2\x80\x94 for daemons, use whatever flag keeps them attached\x0A(`-g \'daemon off;\'` for nginx, `--nodaemonize` and similar elsewhere), so\x0Athat stopping the recording with `Ctrl-C` stops the server too.\x0A\x0A## Watch requests arrive\x0A\x0AIn a second terminal:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay -t ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0ASend traffic and rows appear as requests are discovered.\x0A\x0A## Open a recording later\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A## What gets recorded\x0A\x0A- **Method, path, status and duration** per request.\x0A- The **position in the recording** where each request was served, so\x0A  double-clicking a row seeks there.\x0A\x0ARoute *templates* are generally not available: without framework knowledge\x0Athere is nothing that says `/users/7` and `/users/9` are the same endpoint.\x0ARows are labelled with the concrete path instead.\x0A\x0A## Requirements\x0A\x0ALinux or macOS. Recording a native program uses CodeTracer\'s native\x0Arecording backend, which needs no changes to the program under test.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0ABecause there is no middleware, request discovery depends on CodeTracer\x0Arecognising the server\'s protocol handling. Plain HTTP is supported. If your\x0Aserver speaks HTTPS directly, terminate TLS in front of it (or point\x0ACodeTracer at the plaintext side) \xE2\x80\x94 encrypted request boundaries are not yet\x0Adiscovered.\x0A:::\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-854620308), Field1: [114,101,102,101,114,101,110,99,101,47,101,110,118,105,114,111,110,109,101,110,116,95,118,97,114,105,97,98,108,101,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Environment variables\x0Aorder: 3\x0A---\x0A## Environment variables\x0A\x0ACodeTracer exposes a number of environment variables that you can use to override some of its behaviours:\x0A\x0Afor many of the flags, we expect \"1\" to enable them\x0A\x0A1. `CODETRACER_ELECTRON_ARGS` - adds arguments for launching Electron. Useful for debugging production builds\x0A1. `CODETRACER_WRAPPER_PID` - overrides the process ID of the `ct` CodeTracer wrapper\x0A1. `CODETRACER_CALLTRACE_MODE` - changes the calltrace mode\x0A1. `CODETRACER_RECORD_CORE` - this does nothing as it is only related to the unreleased system backend\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_SOCKET` - this sets the socket path for sending events to the CI integration from `ct record`(or eventually `ct shell`)\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_ADDRESS` - this sets the address for sending events to the CI integration from `ct record`(or eventually `ct shell`)\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_EXPORT` - this enables export mode for `ct record` on: exporting the traces into zip files in the folder that is the value of this env variables; (similarly to the `ct record -e=<zippath>` option, but for all records while the variable is enabled). The trace archives try to use a globally unique id in their filenames, from `std/oids` in the nim stdlib: https://nim-lang.org/docs/oids.html\x0A1. `CODETRACER_DEBUG_CURL` - if \"1\", print debug output for the raw objects sent with curl for the CI integration from `ct record`(or eventually `ct shell`)\x0A1. `CODETRACER_DEBUG_CT_REMOTE` - if \"1\", print debug output(for now process command name and arguments) for the trace sharing code and commands that call `ct-remote`(desktopclient), e.g. `ct upload`, `ct download`, `ct login`, `ct set-default-org`\x0A1. `CODETRACER_RR_SOFT_MODE` - if we are using the `rr.soft` patch in the `rr` used by `ct`: if \"1\", runs `rr record` and `rr replay` with `-W` flag of `rr.soft`: software counters/dynamic\x0Ainstrumentation. rr.soft is https://github.com/sidkshatriya/rr.soft\x0A1. `CODETRACER_RECORD_BACKEND` - this is a newer version of `CODETRACER_RECORD_CORE`: if set, and if `ct` is in PATH, lets you record the internal db-backend process when doing `ct replay`\x0Ae.g. `env CODETRACER_RECORD_BACKEND=1 ct replay db-trace`\x0Athere might be problem if you try to replay a rr trace and record that:\x0Ait seems it might work, but i think `rr under rr` might be still problematic\x0A\x0A\x0A## CodeTracer Shell\x0AThese are generally not functional right now, since they affect CodeTracer Shell, which is currently not stable/in very prototypical state:\x0A\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_BASH_LOG_FILE` - overrides the log file\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_ID` - overrides the shell ID\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SESSION_ID` - overrides the CodeTracer Shell session ID so that the current commands affect a previous shell session\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_REPORT_FILE` - overrides the report file of CodeTracer Shell\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_USE_SCRIPT` - ?\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_RECORDS_OUTPUT` - ?\x0A1. `CODETRACER_SHELL_CLEANUP_OUTPUT_FOLDER` - ?\x0A")}, {Field0: (-1832641939), Field1: [103,101,116,116,105,110,103,95,115,116,97,114,116,101,100,47,112,111,108,107,97,118,109,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: PolkaVM (ink!)\x0Aorder: 117\x0A---\x0A## PolkaVM (ink!)\x0A\x0ACodeTracer supports tracing programs running on PolkaVM, Polkadot\'s RISC-V based virtual machine. The recorder uses PolkaVM\'s built-in step tracing and LineProgram debug info for source-level mapping.\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0A- **Rust toolchain** with `riscv32em-unknown-none-elf` target for compiling PolkaVM programs\x0A- **ink!** framework (v6+) for smart contracts\x0A- **codetracer-polkavm-recorder** binary installed or built from source\x0A\x0A### Recording a PolkaVM Program\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-polkavm-recorder record program.polkavm -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Recording an ink! Contract\x0A\x0A1. Build your ink! contract:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   cd my_ink_contract\x0A   cargo contract build\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A2. Record a contract message execution:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-polkavm-recorder trace-ink \\\x0A       --contract target/ink/my_contract.contract \\\x0A       --constructor new \\\x0A       --message get \\\x0A       -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A3. With message arguments:\x0A\x0A   ```bash\x0A   codetracer-polkavm-recorder trace-ink \\\x0A       --contract target/ink/my_contract.contract \\\x0A       --message flip \\\x0A       --arg 0x01 \\\x0A       -o ./ct-traces/\x0A   ```\x0A\x0A### Replaying an On-Chain Contract\x0A\x0ATo debug a contract call on a Substrate chain:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acodetracer-polkavm-recorder replay \\\x0A    --address 5GrwvaEF5... \\\x0A    --selector get \\\x0A    --endpoint ws://127.0.0.1:9944 \\\x0A    --source-dir ./src/ \\\x0A    -o ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Viewing the Trace\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay --trace-folder ./ct-traces/\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Key Flags\x0A\x0A| Flag                          | Description                                             |\x0A| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\x0A| `record <program>`            | Record a PolkaVM program blob (`.polkavm`)              |\x0A| `trace-ink --contract <PATH>` | Record an ink! contract execution                       |\x0A| `--constructor <NAME>`        | Constructor to call (e.g., `new`, `default`)            |\x0A| `--message <NAME>`            | ink! message name (e.g., `flip`, `get`)                 |\x0A| `--arg <VALUE>`               | Message arguments (repeatable, hex-encoded SCALE)       |\x0A| `replay --address <ADDR>`     | On-chain contract address (SS58 or hex)                 |\x0A| `--selector <NAME>`           | ink! message selector name                              |\x0A| `--endpoint <URL>`            | Substrate RPC endpoint (default: `ws://127.0.0.1:9944`) |\x0A| `--block <HASH>`              | Block hash for state fetch (latest if omitted)          |\x0A| `--source-dir <PATH>`         | Contract source directory                               |\x0A| `-o, --out-dir <DIR>`         | Output directory (default: `./ct-traces/`)              |\x0A| `-f, --format <FMT>`          | Output format: `binary` or `json` (default: `binary`)   |\x0A")}, {Field0: 1634356845, Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,108,105,118,101,45,114,101,113,117,101,115,116,115,45,101,108,105,120,105,114,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Live Requests \xE2\x80\x94 Elixir & Erlang\x0Aorder: 9\x0A---\x0A# Live request tracking in Elixir and Erlang\x0A\x0AWorks with Phoenix and with plain Plug/Cowboy applications. Request spans\x0Aare published by a Plug in your endpoint pipeline, inside the recorded\x0Aprocess.\x0A\x0A## Record your server\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang elixir -o ./trace -- mix phx.server\x0A```\x0A\x0Aor, for a plain Plug application:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act record --server --lang elixir -o ./trace -- mix run --no-halt\x0A```\x0A\x0AFlags after the program name are passed through unchanged. Stop with\x0A`Ctrl-C`, which is a normal end.\x0A\x0A## Watch requests arrive\x0A\x0AIn a second terminal:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay -t ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0ASend traffic and rows appear:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Acurl http://localhost:4000/users/7\x0A```\x0A\x0ADouble-click a row to jump to the code that handled the request.\x0A\x0A## Open a recording later\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Act replay ./trace\x0A```\x0A\x0A## What gets recorded\x0A\x0A- **The route from your router**, so parameterised routes group correctly.\x0A- **Status and duration** per request.\x0A- **Errors**, including the exception message.\x0A\x0A## A note on BEAM concurrency\x0A\x0AThe BEAM serves each request in its own lightweight process. These map onto\x0A*threads* within a single recording rather than separate recordings, so a\x0Aburst of concurrent requests is one recording containing many overlapping\x0Aintervals. The Request Panel shows them as separate rows even though they\x0Aran at the same time, and the recording preserves which ones genuinely\x0Aoverlapped.\x0A\x0A:::note\x0ADouble-clicking a request row currently lands you at the request boundary\x0Arather than inside your handler function for some `.ex` modules. The span\x0Aitself is correct \xE2\x80\x94 method, route, status and duration are all accurate, and\x0Athe recording contains the handler \xE2\x80\x94 but the seek target may be earlier than\x0Ayou expect. Compiled `.exs` scripts are unaffected.\x0A:::\x0A\x0A## Requirements\x0A\x0AElixir 1.15 or newer, or Erlang/OTP 26 or newer for plain Erlang servers.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-55109775), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,99,114,111,115,115,45,116,114,97,99,101,114,45,100,101,109,111,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Cross-Tracer Demo\x0Aorder: 107\x0A---\x0A# Cross-Tracer Demo (`account-balance-with-wasm`)\x0A\x0AThe **cross-tracer demo** is a one-command launcher that opens the\x0Acanonical M29 three-trace fixture in the CodeTracer GUI for manual\x0Ainspection. The fixture records the same web request through three\x0Aindependent recorders simultaneously, producing three `.ct` containers\x0Aglued together by a `session.toml` manifest. The GUI then lets you\x0Aright-click a value in any recording and follow its origin chain\x0Aacross recording boundaries.\x0A\x0AThis demo is the user-facing counterpart of the automated\x0A`test-cross-process` envelope: same fixture, same `session.toml`, but\x0Alaunched interactively so you can chain-walk and switch recordings\x0Ayourself instead of asserting against a Playwright script.\x0A\x0A## What the demo records\x0A\x0AThe fixture is a three-tier balance-update flow:\x0A\x0A1. **Frontend (JavaScript).** A Vite-built single-page app posts\x0A   `{ balance: N }` to the backend and renders the response. Recorded\x0A   by the codetracer-js-recorder host\x0A   (`browser_stream_receiver`).\x0A2. **Frontend (WASM).** A `wasm-pack`-built Rust module that the\x0A   frontend calls into to format the balance. Recorded by the\x0A   codetracer-wasm-instrumenter shim under the same browser host. The\x0A   `frontend-wasm.ct` and `frontend.ct` files share a realm boundary,\x0A   not an OS-process boundary.\x0A3. **Backend (Python aiohttp).** A small aiohttp server that handles\x0A   `POST /balance` and returns the updated balance. Recorded by the\x0A   codetracer Python recorder.\x0A\x0AA single `POST /balance` request flows from the JS frontend, through\x0Athe WASM module, across an HTTP boundary into the Python backend, and\x0Aback. The `session.toml` manifest binds the three recordings together\x0Awith their `frontend-js` / `frontend-wasm` / `backend` role labels and\x0Aan eager-mode `[correlation]` section, so the cross-process composer\x0Acan index every send/recv marker upfront when the session opens.\x0A\x0AThe full scenario contract \xE2\x80\x94 including the exact `balance` value the\x0Achain should resolve to \xE2\x80\x94 lives in the fixture\'s `ANSWERS.md`.\x0A\x0A## How to run it\x0A\x0AFrom the repository root:\x0A\x0A```bash\x0Ajust demo-cross-tracer\x0A```\x0A\x0AThe recipe records the demo before it launches:\x0A\x0A- The three `.ct` containers and `session.toml` are **not committed**.\x0A  They are produced by `scripts/materialize-recording.sh`, which runs\x0A  `src/db-backend/tests/fixtures/cross_process/account-balance-with-wasm/regenerate.sh`\x0A  and caches the result under the gitignored `target/test-recordings/`.\x0A  So what the GUI opens is always a recording of the tree you are\x0A  standing in \xE2\x80\x94 not a copy made by whichever recorder happened to be\x0A  current when someone last committed one.\x0A- The cache is keyed on the demo\'s sources **and on the content of every\x0A  recorder binary involved** (`ct-instrument`, the `record-web` binary,\x0A  the instrumenter\'s `recorder-runtime/`, the JS recorder\'s built\x0A  packages), so a second run is instant and a rebuilt recorder re-records.\x0A- The pipeline is honestly gated on its prerequisites (see below) and\x0A  fails with an actionable, consolidated diagnostic when any are missing.\x0A  There is no outcome that quietly launches on nothing.\x0A\x0AThe launch is `ct replay -t <fixture-dir>/session.toml`, which hands\x0Athe manifest to the same DAP session loader the M24 process-tree flow\x0Auses (`src/db-backend/src/session_manifest.rs` +\x0A`session_handler.rs`).\x0A\x0A### Prerequisites\x0A\x0AThe recorder pipeline requires every component below. The recipe\x0Aexits with a single consolidated report when any are missing, so the\x0Aoperator can fill the gaps one by one and re-run:\x0A\x0A- `rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`\x0A- `ct-instrument`, built in the `codetracer-wasm-instrumenter` sibling\x0A- The `record-web` daemon \xE2\x80\x94 i.e. a built `session-manager`\x0A  (`cargo build` in `src/backend-manager`, or `just build-once`)\x0A- The `codetracer-js-recorder` sibling, built (`just build` there)\x0A- Playwright in the fixture\'s `frontend/` directory\x0A  (`npm install playwright`)\x0A- A built `ct` binary (`just build-once`)\x0A\x0AThese are the same prerequisites the Playwright spec\x0A`cross-tracer-three-recording.spec.ts` depends on: it calls\x0A`src/tests/gui/lib/value-origin-fixtures.ts::threeTraceRecordingRoot()`,\x0Awhich runs the same materialiser and **throws** rather than skipping when\x0Ait cannot record. Only the missing `ct` binary is treated as an\x0Aenvironment gap the spec may skip on, because that one genuinely means\x0Athe machine cannot run a GUI test at all.\x0A\x0A## What to expect in the GUI\x0A\x0AOnce the Electron window opens:\x0A\x0A1. **Process tree.** The left-hand process tree mounts three entries\x0A   labelled by their `role` (`frontend-js`, `frontend-wasm`,\x0A   `backend`) and prefixed with the manifest\'s\x0A   `default_thread_prefix` (`fe`, `wasm`, `be`). Each entry is\x0A   expandable to its threads.\x0A2. **Switch recordings.** Click any process-tree entry to make that\x0A   recording active. The source pane, calltrace, and state pane all\x0A   switch to that recording\'s timeline; the other two stay paused at\x0A   their last-inspected step.\x0A3. **Right-click chain walk.** In the backend recording, right-click\x0A   the `balance` variable at the response site and pick **Show value\x0A   origin**. The chain panel renders `CrossProcessSpan` breadcrumb\x0A   chips at every cross-recording hop \xE2\x80\x94 one for the HTTP boundary\x0A   between the backend and the JS frontend, one for the realm\x0A   boundary between the JS frontend and the WASM module \xE2\x80\x94 terminating\x0A   at the frontend expression that produced the input. Each chip\'s\x0A   click target seeks the *receiving* recording to the matched\x0A   message-arrival step.\x0A4. **Seek across hops.** Clicking a `CrossProcessSpan` chip flips the\x0A   active recording to the chip\'s target process and seeks to the\x0A   matched step. The chain panel survives the switch \xE2\x80\x94 the breadcrumb\x0A   chips stay in place so you can hop forward and backward through\x0A   the chain without re-querying.\x0A\x0AThe full step-by-step contract (steps 1-9, including the exact chip\x0Acounts and the expected `frontend.js` expression at the terminator)\x0Alives in\x0A`codetracer-specs/Planned-Features/Cross-Tracer-Origin-Test.audit.md`\x0A\xC2\xA7 TCT-M5, which the Playwright spec\x0A`src/tests/gui/tests/value-origin/cross-tracer-three-recording.spec.ts`\x0Aencodes as a single end-to-end pass/fail. The demo recipe is the\x0Ahuman-driven version of that same scenario.\x0A\x0A## Further reading\x0A\x0A- [Value Origin Tracking](./value-origin-tracking.md) \xE2\x80\x94 single-trace\x0A  and cross-trace chain-walking surfaces (M29 milestone).\x0A- `codetracer-specs/Planned-Features/Cross-Tracer-Origin-Test.audit.md`\x0A  \xE2\x80\x94 TCT-M5 acceptance criteria the demo mirrors.\x0A- `codetracer-specs/GUI/Test-Scenarios/Cross-Process-Origin-E2E-Test-Design.md`\x0A  \xE2\x80\x94 design notes for the cross-process chain panel surface.\x0A- The fixture sources at\x0A  `src/db-backend/tests/fixtures/cross_process/account-balance-with-wasm/`\x0A  \xE2\x80\x94 `frontend/`, `backend/`, `wasm-src/`, and the canonical\x0A  `ANSWERS.md` describing the expected origin chain.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: (-43565065), Field1: [117,115,97,103,101,95,103,117,105,100,101,47,116,114,97,99,101,112,111,105,110,116,115,46,109,100], Field2: makeNimstrLit("---\x0Atitle: Tracepoints\x0Aorder: 3\x0A---\x0A## What are tracepoints?\x0A\x0ATracepoints are a type of breakpoint that allows you to run arbitrary code every time this breakpoint is hit. In most\x0Adebuggers that support tracepoints they allow for efficient debugging of complex scenarios, like really deep recursion,\x0Aor complex control flow.\x0A\x0AIn CodeTracer, however, they can also unlock a number of strategies for additional debugging, specifically in the realm\x0Aof hotspot debugging.\x0A\x0A## Tracepoints usage guide\x0A\x0AFrom the GUI:\x0A\x0A1. Right-click on a line\x0A1. Click on \"Add tracepoint\"\x0A1. The tracepoints popup should appear\x0A1. Write your tracepoint code in the text editor in the tracepoints popup\x0A1. Press \"CTRL + Enter\" to run the trace\x0A1. After running the trace, the output of your tracepoint will be listed in the tracepoint popup, and in the event log\x0A\x0AFrom the TUI: Coming soon!\x0A\x0AFrom the REPL: Coming soon!\x0A\x0A## Tracepoints language\x0A\x0A### Syntax\x0A\x0AThe syntax of the language is similar to Noir/Rust (and therefore most C-like languages). However it doesn\'t use semicolons.\x0A\x0AIn the future it is possible to add language-specific features or dialects of the tracepoint language.\x0A\x0A#### Literals\x0A\x0AInteger (`1`, `23`, `12`), Float (`1.23`, `.234`, `123.`), Bool (`true`, `false`) and String (`\"some text\"`) literals\x0Aare supported.\x0A\x0A### `log()`\x0A\x0AThe `log()` statement is used to evaluate the argument expression and add it as output from the current tracepoint.\x0AThe `log()` statement suppors multiple values that are comma-separated: `log(v1, v2, v3)`.\x0A\x0A#### Example\x0A\x0A```rs\x0Afn test() {\x0A  let mut sum = 0;\x0A\x0A  for i in 0..3 {\x0A    sum += i;\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A    |  log(\"I\'m in the loop\", i)\x0A    |\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A    // Output:\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A    |  \"I\'m in the loop\" i=0\x0A    |  \"I\'m in the loop\" i=1\x0A    |  \"I\'m in the loop\" i=2\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  }\x0A```\x0A\x0A\x0A### Accessing variables\x0A\x0AThe tracepoint for has access to all the variables, that are defined when the line on which the tracepoint is added is\x0Aevaluated. You can reference them just by using their names in the expressions.\x0A\x0A#### Example\x0A\x0A```rs\x0Afn add(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\x0A  a + b\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  |  log(a)\x0A  |  log(b)\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  // Output:\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  |  a=3 b=5\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Comparison\x0A\x0A#### `==` and `!=`\x0A\x0ATwo values are considered eqial iff their types are the same and their values are the same. **Exception** to this rule is\x0Acomparing **Int** and **Float**, which are compared by their values, despite them being different type.\x0A\x0A#### `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`\x0A\x0AThese operators work **only with numerical values** (e.g Int and Float). If at least one of the values is of non-numerical\x0Atype, then an Error is raised.\x0A\x0A#### Example\x0A\x0A| Expression | Value |\x0A| ---------- | ----- |\x0A| 1 == 1 | true |\x0A| 1 != 1 | false |\x0A| 1 == 2 | false |\x0A| 1 != 2 | true |\x0A| \"banana\" == \"banana\" | true |\x0A| \"banana\" != \"banana\" | false |\x0A| \"banana\" == \"apple\" | false |\x0A| \"banana\" != \"apple\" | true |\x0A| \"banana\" == 1 | false |\x0A| \"banana\" != 1 | true |\x0A| | |\x0A| **1.0 == 1** | **true** |\x0A| **1.0 != 1** | **false** |\x0A| **2.0 == 1** | **false** |\x0A| **2.0 != 1** | **true** |\x0A| **\"1\" == 1** | **false** |\x0A| **\"1\" != 1** | **true** |\x0A| | |\x0A| 1 < 2 | true |\x0A| 1 <= 2 | true |\x0A| 1 > 2 | false |\x0A| 1 >= 2 | false |\x0A| 1 < 2.2 | true |\x0A| 1.1 <= 2 | true |\x0A| 1 > 2.2 | false |\x0A| 1.1 >= 2 | false |\x0A| | |\x0A| **1 < \"2\"** | **ERROR** |\x0A| **\"0\" < 1** | **ERROR** |\x0A| **\"1\" >= \"2\"** | **ERROR** |\x0A\x0A\x0A### Arithmetic operations\x0A\x0AThe supported arithmetic operations are addition (`+`), subtraction (`-`), multiplication (`*`), division (`/`) and\x0Aremainder (`%`). They work only with numerical types (Int and Float).\x0A\x0AWhen both arguments are Integer values, then the result is an Integer (for `/` the result is rounded toward 0). If at\x0Aleast one of the arguments is a Float, then the result is a Float.\x0A\x0A#### Example\x0A| Expression | Value |\x0A| ---------- | ----- |\x0A| 2 + 3 | 5 |\x0A| 2 + 3.0 | 5.0 |\x0A| 2.2 + 3.3 | 5.5 |\x0A| 2 - 3 | -1 |\x0A| 2 - 3.0 | -1.0 |\x0A| 2.2 - 3.3 | -1.1 |\x0A| 2 * 3 | 6 |\x0A| 2 * 3.0 | 6.0 |\x0A| 2.2 * 3.3 | 7.26 |\x0A| **7 / 3** | **2** |\x0A| 7 / 3.0 | 2.3333333 |\x0A| 7.7 / 3.3 | 2.3333333 |\x0A| 7 % 3 | 1 |\x0A| 7 % 3.0 | 1.0 |\x0A| 7.7 % 3.3 | 1.1 |\x0A\x0A\x0A### Conditional branching (`if`-`else`)\x0A\x0AThe tracepoint language also supports conditional evaluation and branching. If the condition expression doesn\'t\x0Aevaluate to a boolean value, then an error is raised.\x0A\x0A#### Example\x0A\x0A```rs\x0Afn test() {\x0A  let mut sum = 0;\x0A\x0A  for i in 0..4 {\x0A    sum += i;\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A    |  log(i)\x0A    |  if i % 2 == 0 {\x0A    |    log(\"even\")\x0A    |  } else if i % 3 == 0 {\x0A    |    log(\"div 3\")\x0A    |  } else {\x0A    |    log(\"odd\")\x0A    |  }\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A    // Output:\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A    |  i=0 even\x0A    |  i=1 odd\x0A    |  i=2 even\x0A    |  i=3 div 3\x0A    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  }\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Array indexing\x0A\x0AIf a value is an array, you can index it using the `[]` operators. Indices are 0-based.\x0A\x0A#### Example\x0A\x0A```rs\x0Afn arr(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\x0A  let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\x0A  let b = a[2];\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  |  log(a)\x0A  |  log(a[0])\x0A  |  log(a[1])\x0A  |  log(a[4])\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  // Output:\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  |  a=[1, 2, 3, 4, 5] a[0]=1 a[1]=2 a[4]=5\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Errors\x0A\x0AWhen an error occurs, the evaluation of the tracepoint stops.\x0A\x0A#### Example\x0A```rs\x0Afn arr(a: i32, b: i32) -> i32 {\x0A  let a = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\x0A  let b = a[2];\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  |  log(a[0])\x0A  |  if a[1] { // This will cause error\x0A  |    log(\"banana\")\x0A  |  }\x0A  |  log(a[2]) // This won\'t be evaluated\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  // Output:\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A  |  a[0]=1 Error=Non-boolean value on conditional jump\x0A  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\x0A}\x0A```\x0A\x0A### Rust-specific extensions\x0A\x0AWe have some language-specific extensions in mind, but nothing concrete yet.\x0A")}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}, {Field0: 0, Field1: [], Field2: []}], counter: 65};
